      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>Mx DEAR NELLIE, • What are the new coats to be like is a question in many a mind and on many a lip. Some decide that they are to be short, with the single exception of the Louis XV. and Louis XVI., with their deep and graceful basques. The bolero is as much as ever in favour in its elongated character, with fronts drooping slightly below the waist line. Long, basques at the back, in the fashion of an old- world riding habit, are also creeping into favour, and form a neat finish. For the sake of variety, one coat is furnisned with a stitched silk collar, another with one in brocade or painted satin or panne. This coat can be worn either open or closed down the front, the fastenings being pro- vided underneath the strapping or galon that binds the front. The material may match that of the dress, and ho cloth or velvet in black, dark blue, purple, green, or any shade of red ; or beige, lawn. cream colour or pure white.</p>
        <p>' Tun new spring huts are bigger and more pan. cake. like than over. Many are flattened turbans of twisted gauze or folded velvet, surmounted with a perfectly plain circular piece of lace much resembling a dessert d'oyley in shape and size. Others have the folds of material veiled by a lace scarf, the ends of which are tied at the back and drooped well over the hair; while a third type of hat is a wonderful affair, also a turban variety, made of labyrinthine coils of spangled tulle, caught here and there with gorgeous buckles and slightly raised on a bandeau of shaded roses, pinks, or orchids. Prettier, however, as-. the hats of ex- tremely rough, spiky straws, in delicate colourings, which are trimmed very effectively with large bows of black velvet, or choux of black chiffon, and wreaths of small flowers, such as brier-roses, or clover. The hat-pins now in vogue are all rather important, both as regards appearance and price. The heads of some are of silver-gilt chased or repousse, and those of others are big, rough lumps ef turquoise-matrix, amethyst, or 'oral, partly encased in gold or silver filagreo.</p>
        <p>On black oi\ k bodices lace is introduced as a yoke just below tie collar, the black silk showing through, and again at the top of the sleeves. A favourite way of introducing lace down the outer seam of sleeves is by putting squares of it point to point in a line carried from shoulder to wrist. The 'black silk blouses of last season, with bands of cream-coloured guipure across the chest and down each side of the front are now improved upon by having similar bands placed across the front witk the fulness of the silk gathered under them, and in this way saved from the untidy, floppy appearance that very thin silk assumes when not firmly coerced in some such fashion. At the back these blouses are stitched down or pleated in straight lines from collar to belt. To avoid the straight line round the waist that is so ugly, a black belt should never be worn of equal width all round over a white or pale-tinted blouse. A shaped belt is so easily made that it is well worth while to make one or two. They may be in satin or velvet, one of each for choice. The foundation is stiff linen of a very good quality of silk. The point at the back is carefully boned, and a similar process is applied to both sides of the front, for the shaped belts that hook up the front are much more satisfactory than those that fasten at the left side. The satin, watered silk, or velvet is then arranged in pleats and stitched firmly down on tha foundation, the upper pleat being caught down here and there so that it shall not slip below the foundation. The satin or velvet should be cut on the cross for mak- ing these belts. This enables the worker to dis- pense with a join.</p>
        <p>..4que may only be a sort of butterfly bow, Arith fluted ends, in the centre of the 'back. But in some form or another, for costumes do style, the basque will be present in most cases. Its alterna- tive will be a deep draped belt, fastened with handsome antique buttons or ribbon rosettes, and often wide sash ends will fall from the back, with rounded ends, reaching to the hem of the dress, edged with a quilting of ribbon or lace, and em- broidered in flowers.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Aldeburgh lifeboat crew the other day rescued 11 men from the wreck of the barque Soreid. The London City Corporation has voted £15,000 to cover the cost of the Coronation celebrations hi the City.</p>
        <p>We pay your grocer to sell you Fels-Naptha soap at 22d and return your money if you don't find it as good as we say.</p>
        <p>There is no other such soap in</p>
        <p>the world.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha soap Peninsular House Monument London E C</p>
