      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>McDOUGALL'S PATBINT SELF-RAISING' FLOUI ) ONCBTRIBD WOMEN'S CHAT. The King and Queen Consort will hold their first 1 Court at Buckingham Palace on the 14th inst. Invita- tions which are homed by the Lord Chamberlain' and the Chamberlain to the Queen, are fer ten o'clock, and the Court will be strictly diplomatic and official. Their I Majesties will be accompanied by many members of the 'Royal Family, and the Court—the first of a new order of things—will be an exceptionally brilliant one. The dates of the Courts to be held subsequently, have at yet been fixed, but many will be necessary if all who are desirous to attend are to receive invitations. Later on in the month the King will leave for a short visit to Cannes, whilst Queen Alexandra will go to Den- mark where Her Majesty will stay over Easter. The Riviera season is now in full swing, and since it became known that the King is going to Cannes, the hotels and houses in that town have been rapidly filling up. There are, however, ominous rumours that smallpox is preva- lent at niany!of the Riviera towns, (specially at Nice, which is always in close communication with Canoes. All these places which are so fair and beautiful to look upon, have their unsavoury quarters, in the back- ground, where infection always lingues, but the officials say that ereallpex has been brought to the Riviera by British visitors frcm London, which is not at all unlikely. There is eomethirg repulsive even about the very name smallpox, but though we are bearing much about this dread disease, it is cct doing half the mischief that influenza isi. The worst of it is that cur many experi- ences of former epidemics have not resulted in the dis- covery of any specific, and there is as much divergence of opinion about remedies as ever there Watt,. Sem" swear by eucalyptus, black-currant tea or ammoniated quinine, or ether drugs, whilst many believe simply in generous diet of oysters, champagne and other luxuries. There is however little doubt that the most effective preventive is ammoniated quinine, if taken immediately there is the first sign of a ebill or cold. It may not be effectual in all cases. Mr A .1' Balfour for instance, has bad three turns of influenza this season, and we may be sure that by this time he has tried every known remedy to ward off these attacks. Butte many people, ammoni- ated quinine, if taken instantly, is a sure and approved preventive of this melt disaerceatle complaint. Princess Christian, and Princess Victoria, both took prizes at the recent Natioe al Cat Clubs show. The Duchess of Bedford is President of the club, which in- cludes many well-known women in Society amongst; its members. Dog lovers are once more up In arms against the new order of the Board of Agriculture that, after March 15, all dogs brought from abroad are to go into quarantine for six months, at the owner's expense. The fair devotees of toy and fancy dogs, who have to leave their pets at home when going abroad, have been trying hard to persuade Mr. Hanbury to modify the order, but without success. Fashionable dog owaere have not however given up the struggle, and as they include many leading women in Scciety, they may yet make their influence felt to some ourpose. It is a good idea on the part of the Society for the Protection of Birds, to cffer prizes for essays on birds to the child/en of village schools. A similar plan had been adopted previously by the Royal Society fcr the Pre- vention of Cruelty to 'Animals. and—seeing that the best way to convince youreelf about a thing is to write en it—there can be no doubt that the preparation of the essays has had a permanent effect upon the minds of a large proportion of the youthful writer?. But while one approves the action of the Society for the Protection of Birds, one cannot help rdlecting that this is one of the many cases in which humane individuals are striving to do by moral suaeice that which it is the duty of the makers and administrators of the law, to enforce. Hair-dressing is a difficult problem to the average woman,and most of all so to her who has spoilt her lock* by cutting, crimping, and curling. Many are the rumours anent a low-down style of dressing, and vigor- ous the efforts made to introduce it. But, for the day- time, hair in the nape of the neck is decidedly out ef place. Therefore do many, especially those not too richly endewed by Nature,puff out their tresses aureele- like, all round the head, twisting up a diminutive knob of what remains of it, on the top yet inclining to the back of the head. The stage is responsible for the introduction of many of our details of fashion, but so doubtless a a those in high positions. At the Corona- tion, tiaras and coronets will, almost perforce, be worn on hair that is dressed high, and formed into a knot within the magic circle, ea thi* up-turned style of wear- ing the hair will filter down through all ranks, and retain its universal popularity for some time to come yet. • A great many of the bodices for spring wear, are pouched in front so decidedly, as to completely hide the waist. Therefore the massive buckles and clasps of last year's belts are departing, as unnecessary and unseen, and instead a crop of buttons appears on everyone. Happy these who have old Dutch or Swiss buttons that they can tbue turn to account, for these antiques are preferable to any modern imitatiens. The latter are all of one pattern ,whereas old ones are all different showing that they are hand..made, or at least hand.finished. The buttons appear en bodices down the fashionable bands that are too wide to be called strappinge, and too fiat and narrow to be box-pleate, and in eats of three, or arranged singly, have an excellent sffcct. The Norfolk is a favourite make of coat nowadays, for indoor, and outdoor wear,and here buttons stew to edvantage.Often by the way, these Norfolk jacl,ets are cut withouc basque, ending at the waist in a belt pointed back and f ont. There is, after a long fashion for hygienic woollen underwear,a re-action in favour of dainty white lingerie. This also is likely to run to an extreme, for the hand,or the machine-made embroidery once admired, is now considered to be a quite unsuitable form of ornamenta- tion. The garments are, to begin with, made of the eneet linen, cambric, lawn, or silk. These are tucked, frilled,tritorned with fine embroidery,and drawn-thread- work, until they seem more fitted for outer garments them unseen wear. As to lace, that is a matter of course. Crochet, Irish crochet, which is we know an elaborate variety of the same work, torchon and Valenciennes, in insertions and edgings, are used by the furlong. As to ribbon, that of every colour and width is pressed into the service. The fad may be an extravagant 'ere, yet those with time to devote to it, can manage to make that serve in lieu of pence. With careful washing most makes of white work and lace, are long lived and even coloured ribbons will endure the process once or twice if care- fully treated. Corset-covers are still cut in a sort of bolero shape that is open at the neck and waist, and meeting only on the bust. These take up little room, but as far as protection goes, they leave the most delicate parts of the Human frame exposed. Mr. Burgin's " A Goddess of Gray's Ina" (C. A. Peer- eon Ltd., 65.) is a story of a lawyer'a household, and contains many interesting and delightful studies. Per- haps the beet are those of Begot Ilarte,and his clerk,who held meet peculiar relative positione, and—amusing conversations. One elopes the book with regret, but without a shade of disapnointment.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>APPROACH OF NIGHT IN THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>APPROACH OF NIGHT IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Another round of duty done, Low in the west went down the sun ; And thence e'er hill and valley Um ew A tender flush of golden hue. His vacant path a while to fill, And make the world more lovely still. But 0, toe beautiful to stay, That vision gently gassed away : And twilight for a time delayed Tbe gativring gloom, the nearing shade, Whose touch would all too soon efface You ling'ring streak—day's latest trace. Then from the cicud-envelop'd sky A star looked down with kindly eye;. Forerunner of those erbs divine Which in the silent watches shine, New light to give, new charms to throw Upcn the resting world below. And:thus came Night that solcran pause, When Nature round her curtain draws, That poor and rich, and young and old, May rest within its ample fold ; While sleep a sweet oblivion throws O'er all their cares, o'er all their woes. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
        <p>The Sunderland brarch of the United Irish League have passed a resolution di;approvinz Lord Rrsebery'a " clean elate declaration,' ard affirming that any candidate desiring their suprort " must have Boma Rule for Ireland writtcn on hie slate.'</p>
        <p>- One of the latest stories as to the amenities passing between Ministers which has been current in the lobbies during the past few days is thus told : Lord Salisbury went to the Treasury to see the Chancellor of the Ex- chequer. The weather was cold, and he wore a very heavy fur ceat. Sir II Hicks-Beach had not arsived, and the Prime Minister sat iu his coat: waiting for him. When at last the Chancellor of the Exchequer arrived he was surprised to see Lurd Salisbury sitting in heavy furs, and asked the reason. "I am suffering from the latest results of your penurious administrat; Ga —there are no coals in the scuttie."</p>
