      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LALL Mann RESERVED.]</head>
        <p>LALL Mann RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>MISS FOSBROOKE'S PORTRAIT,</p>
        <p>J. MACLAREN COBBAN (Aubior of " 77e King of Andaman," 4-0.)</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IL</p>
        <p>Neat day the portrait painting was begun. At the appointed time Lady and Miss Fosbrooke arrived in a carriage, followed after a little by a servant with a flowering oleander in a big pot in a cab— (Irma Fosbrooke was to be painted in the company of the oleander). Dolly kept out of the way as she had arranged, and so she did not see the ladies, but on going out immediately after their arrival to do some necessary shopping she encountered the oleander on the stairs. She had reason to remember her first meeting with the lovely plant, for it was ever after associated in her mind with Miss Fosbrooke.</p>
        <p>When Dolly returned from shopping it was about time for her and Claude's dinner (euphemistically called "lunch "). Therefore when she entered the back room and heard no voices she almost passed oa without pause into the front, expecting to find her husband alone. She had her hand on the handle of the inner door when, as if expressly to warn her, a voice,—a *omen's voice—spoke. Then Claude'. voice replied, and then there was' a pause. "They are there still," Dolly thought, and turned away to occupy herself until they should go. Presently she heard a woman's voice—the same woman's voice—and Claude's voice in reply, with a laugh superadcled. ; then the woman's voice, with a lau'gh ;—and so it continued. Only one of the two ladies was talking—which? She could not tell. And what was the other doing? Reading? Or, 'what? Anon she heard movements, and she guessed that the sitting was at an end. By and by the outer door of the studio was opened, and Claude went downstairs talking, followed (or accompanied), it seemed to Dolly, by but one person: at least, there was but one voice engaged with his. Dolly gently opened the inner door, and peeped in. There was no one in the room. She swiftly crossed to the window, and looked out from behind a curtain. Claude came down the steps with one lady—with Miss Fosbrooke —and stood on the kerb with her, looking this way and that. Then he put his fingers to his mouth and whistled—for a cab, no doubt—and Dolly could see and hear that Miss Fosbrooke laughed at the boyish- ness of his action. It seemed to Dolly that they were already very easy and unconstrained in each other's company. In a second or two a hansom dashed up; Claude saw Miss Fosbrooke into it, shielding with his hand her dress from the wheel; the cab drove off; and Claude re-entered the house.</p>
        <p>He came up the stairs humming, and when he en- tered the room he looked preoccupied. He came somewhat out of his preoccupation upon observing Dolly. "Hallo !" he said, and turned to look at his morning's work on the canvas. Dolly looked with him. The unskilled person might have made little out of the mixture of char- coal outlines and sraudges of colour, but Dolly was not unskilled, and to her there was considerabli• loveliness that was to appear. "You have made good progress, Claude," said she.</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Claude, again preoccupied and re- suming his palette and brushes to work in certain details that occurred to him. "I wonder," he said, " if I shall ever be able to do like Duran, and paint a portrait from a single sitting." (Claude was apupfl of the great Fsench portrait-painter.) " There's 915 call," said Dolly, "for such speed as that. In this case the flower will take as much painting as the lady. Is there any need for intro- ducing it ?" "Oh, yes, I think so," said Claude. "You see Miss Fosbrooke is rather pale, and the red blossoms will help me to give some glow to the skin." "Yes, of course," said Dolly. "Who proposed that P'</p>
        <p>"I believe I did," said Claude. "And I suppose," said she, "that the 'oleander will keep us company all the while the portrait is being painted ?" " Of course it will," answered Claude.</p>
        <p>"And we shall have to lock after it. I suppose ire can more it aside when it is not in use: it would be rather in the way there, a great thing like that." "My dear Dolly," exclaimed Claude, turning and looking at her in some surprise, " surely you don't grudge the lovely thing house-room ?" "Certainly not," answered Dolly: but she blushed a little, while she wondered at herself. What had come over her, that this small grudge should spring up in her heart and this spice ,of ill- nature should season her speech? She changed the subject—apparently. "Miss Fosbrooke, I saw, went away by herself: has her mother been gone long?" "Yes," ansWered Claude, "she went Soon after she came: she had' some business or other to do. There," said he, standing back and surveying his work, "I think that's all right. Suppose we ring for food, Dolly : I'm hungry." Thus passed the first sitting of Miss FoshroOke, and the intelligent reader will have already „per- ceived—what the simple, good-natured Claudenever suspected, and the loving, faithful Dolly was herself unwitting of—that the insidious canker of jealousy had touched the heart of the wife. Miss Fosbrooke, it was plain to Dolly's perceptions and instincts, was interested in Claude, and Claude was interested in Kiss Fosbrooke. But was he interested in her more than a conscientious artist ought to be in his sub- ject? That was the unasked question which hung in Dolly's mind without an answer. As the days paned. Claude unconsciously preparesl an answer tor it.</p>
        <p>Next day Miss Fosbrooke came alone for her sitting. jolly at first said to herself that that, surely was a singular breach of social decorum, but presently she was ashamed of such small and mean criticism of conduct, and she told herself that Miss Fosbrooke was not a foolish, ignorant girl, but a tolerably ma- ture woman of the World, who might justly claim for herself complete freedom of movement She reso- lutely dismissed the matter, and went about her du- ties. One thing in particular she had arranged to do that morning, and that was to carry some finished drawings to the editor of an illustrated paper. The business took her longer than she had expected, so that when she returned home it was past the usual hour for lunch. Miss Fosbrooke was gone, and Claude was surveying and retouching his morning's work. "Oh, Claude," said Dolly, "you have not got on quite so well this morning."</p>
        <p>"No," answered Claurde, " net quite so fast as yesterday."</p>
        <p>" Yoti've been talking a good deal, I suppose," said Dolly. " Yes," said he, " we did talk a good deal." "What about 7—if one may ask," said she. " Really," said he, "I can't quite recall. We talkcd about Art, and other things. I think she's the cloverest woman lever knew. And it's notthat her education has been so good, but that berinstincts are so true. For an untrained person her ideas about Art are wonderful."</p>
        <p>"I think," said Dolly, "I remember our saying something like that before."</p>
        <p>" Very likely," said Claude, undisturbed. "It's very striking." So Claude began his extravagant praise of Miss Fosbrooke's intelligence, which, with little inter. mission. he continued directly and indirectly till the end. He expressed no opinion on any subject with- out wondering what Miss Fosbrooke's opinion might be; he did no work—drew nothing, painted nothing, composed nothing—without proposing to submit it to Miss Fosbrooke ; and on a certain occa- sion when Dolly was showing him a new article of attire he regretted on her behalf that she could not ask what Miss Fosbrooke thought of it.</p>
        <p>"Msi Fosbrooke !" exclaimed Dolly. " Its always Miss Fosbrooke ! I think I ought to know what becomes me, Claude, as well as Miss Fos- brook° !"</p>
        <p>' Claude looked So bewildered, and his praise of the lady and deference to her opinion were so simple and obvious that Dolly would have repented of her petulance had it not been for something else. • After the second day Dolly had to sit in the back room to accomplish certain drawings for her editor. As she bent over ber work, her hands but not her mind occupied, the sound of the two ,voices in the next room—Miss Posbrooke's and Claude's—filled her ears and affected her more and more to distrac- tion. It was not that she overheard anything that was said--(it might have been better for her if she had heard, for then she might have understood there was no ground for her growing suspicion and fear) --it was the low,. soft tones of confidence and ease, and the gentle murmurs of lauiliter that maddened her, made her hand shake, arm drove the blood ta her head, till she had to rise from her, work to breathe for relief and to stay the wild beating of her heart.</p>
        <p>Thus several days passed, brirging to Dolly the greatest misery and trial of her life She woulrl have put an end to it by frank speech cath her hus- band, had it not been that she felt that to be " re- solved " (to use Othello's words) might be more painful than to remain " in doubt." Moreover her pride bound her. Would it not appear weak and absurd to complain of the situation which she had</p>
        <p>herself created F Had she no refused to 13e linein to Claucle's patrons as Claude's wife ? And would she show that she repented of that refusal only a week after it had taken effect?</p>
        <p>" I am au unreasonable woman !" she declared to herself. "But oh, it is dreadful !—dreadful think and to bear."</p>
        <p>The situation came to an end thronghan unlocked. for incident, without any contrivance of Dolly's. After the usual morning sitting, when Claude and Polly were about to sit down to lunch, it was dis- covered that Miss Fotbrooke had left her purse. " She will probably- think she has lost it," said Claude. "Don't you think, Dolly, I had better take it to her after lunch ?"</p>
        <p>"Perhaps you had," said Dolly; "though it will be quite safe here till to-morrow."</p>
        <p>"But she may worry about it, you see," avid Claude; there may be a good deal of money or i some valuable papers n it." "Very well" said Dolly. So immediately after lunch Claude departed with the purse. He was barely gone when there sounded on the street-door a knock which Dolly had come to recognise as Miss Fosbrooke's. She escaped into the back room, and heard the visitor come upstairs without pause as soon as the door was opened, knock at the studio door, and then enter. The folding door between the Cro rooms was a little ajar: Dolly peeped. Miss Fosbrooke—it was she—stood looking round her.</p>
        <p>6' He is not here," she said. "And my puma" —going to the place where it had been found— " where is it ? lain sure I left it."</p>
        <p>- - --- A frantic and unworthy suspicion seized Dolly that Miss Fosbrooke had left her purse on purpose that she might have an excuse for coining to. see Claude a second time on the same day. The next thing Dolly saw precipitated her feeling and made her forget herself completely. bliss Fosbrooke picked up a glove of Claude's, pressed it to her bosom, and then to her lips,—and the next instant Dolly stood before her, while she was fixed with surprise and covered with blushes. "I am his wife !" panted Dolly, scarcely knowing what she said. "Oh, why—why have you done it Then, perceiving Miss Fosbrooke's surprise and distress, quick compunction seized her, and her heart was filled with sorrow for the lady. "It has been my fault," she exclaimed. "Forgive me ! oh, forgive me! Ido not blame you. Believe me, I do not."</p>
        <p>She advanced to Miss Fosbrooke, holding out her hands. Miss Fosbrooke took them and held them rigidly, while her own were trembling with excite- ment. She looked at Dolly, and pride manifestly contended in her with tenderness. A second or two passed before she spoke. "Why," she said, in a low, tense voice, with painful pauses to maintain control of herself, "why have you deceived me? Why did I not know? Why did no one tell me ?"</p>
        <p>" It has been my fault," repeated Dolly. "When first you came here you saw me "—Miss Fosbrooke looked at her as if she but half remembered having seen her before—" yes," she continued with rapidity, '" you saw me, but you did not notice me. I was putting the room in order. After that my husband wished to introduce me—his wife—but I said it was not necessary. I refused to be introduced." "I understand," said Miss Fosbrooke in the same low tense voice as before. "You need not explain more. It has all been a mistake. But," said she, attempting to smile, "no harm is done. Good- bye." Miss Fosbrooke dropped Dolly's hands and turned</p>
        <p>11 Oh," cried 7)ol1r-, smitten with the pity of it, " I am so very It ..vas not a wise thing to say to a pram'. dv. rd she felt that as 80011 as she had Y. r",,,rg•ire me !" she added. " Will you .</p>
        <p>A quiver • urn M:- with tender</p>
        <p>7:sassed for an instant ,r eyes were touched y's hand l again, and</p>
        <p>stooped at., . forehead. / " This, • between us two alone." Then 'l.e room and was gone, and Dolly 'sat cl. ,v 1 relief in tears. When she came to h ; ,41 NiDS seized with anxiety lest Claude should r.; )1-::1 find her thus: she agreed with Miss Foabroo.ie that this matter should be be- tween themselves alorie,—at least for the present. So she rose and made haste to compose herself. She laved her eyes in cold water, put her hair in order, and sat down to her drawing. But her hand was too unsteady for that, and she turned to attempt, some sewing. But Claude gave her ample time to get composed, and to be discomposed again. It was late in the afternoon before he came back, so that she had suffi- cient opportunity to torture herself with the ques- ti " What if he should have met Miss Fosbrooke?" When he came she was relieved. He had left th'i purse at Miss Fosbrooke's door, and then had gone to visit an acquaintance.</p>
        <p>All that evening Dolly hung about her husband to a degree unusual with her. Not that she was ever wanting in the bestowal of tokens of affection and endearment, but that she commonly shewed little of the merely clinging and surrendered wife. Now she had an overwhelming desire to assure herself that Claude was hers and not another's, at the same time as she was invaded by twinges of pity for what she congeived must be the love-lore condition of poor Miss losbrooke. In 'an access of desire for assur- ance of the complete love of her husband she almost betrayed her secret._ - -</p>
        <p>"Axe you sure, Claude," she asked, "that you love me quite—quite--entirely, as I love you ?" " Of coarse I do, my dear," answered Claude. "And will you always, for ever—love me, and nobody else r _</p>
        <p>"Love you always 2" said Claude. "To be sure, my sweetheart. But why are you asking me such questions, Dolly_ 2" - -</p>
        <p>"Only'," said Dolly, softly, " because I wonder whether you love me as much as I love you." "Don't be doubting and whimsical, Dolly," said the reassured Claude. " It's not like you." But Dolly's anxiety was not yet at an end. What would happen when Miss Fosbrooke came next day to continue her sittings? Miss Fosbrooke herself solved that question early. By the first post there came a note intimating that she • could not sit that day, nor probably the next,—she was indisposed. On the third day there came a final and more important note, which ran thus :-- _ _</p>
        <p>"Dear Mr. Cun-ningham,—l am sorry I shall not be able to give you any more sittings, as I find I must go abroad for some time. I regret that the less because I know the portrait is far enough ad- vanced for you to finish it easily without seeing me any more. You once expressed a desire to exhibit it: you have my full permission to send it to any exhi- bition you choose, and I now have pleasure in en. closing a cheque for £200." Claude was a good deal put out. "It's a great pity," said he. "I counted upon making the portrait a credit to myself, and now I ean't be Imre of it."</p>
        <p>- "But, Claude, dear," said Dolly, "you surely can, as Miss Fosbrooke says, finish it without diffi- culty : there is really little more to do." "It is very curious and unlucky," said Claude, "that she should have been taken ill and have to go abroad like this all of a sudden." That was all. Claude finished the portrait, still wondering that he had not seen Miss Fosbrooke again; be got it hung in the Exhibition of the Royal Academy, where it attracted considerable at- tention. Still ,he wondered, and was a little hurt that Lady Fosbrooke and her daughter saw him no more. But Dolly luy-ally kept the pact between herself and Miss Fosbrooke, and never told him the reason why the sittings for the portrait so abruptly tame to an end.</p>
        <p>A LANCkSHIRE Incrotryr.—A remarkable case was brought to the notice of this paper and investigated, in which a cure waseffected by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people in a striking way. The result of the investi- gation is given in another column of this issue. There is evidence on all sides that this medicine is held in the highest esteem through the country. Dr Williams' pink pills have cured paralysis, lecomotor ataxy,theumatism, and sciatica; also all diseases arising from impoverish- ment of thd blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, mem* pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of a not a sold by chemists, and by Pr. Williams' Medicine Company, appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous head- ache, and and tea neuralgia early Tarlyhdeeseca9. all -ferias of female pills are a md tonic,re a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name —Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, a 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.G., it 23 9d a box, or six boxes for 13s 9d. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr. Williams' pink pills; accept them only n the pink closed wrapper as above described.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Halifax on Tuesday on Thomas Topham, aged 40 years, chemist, of Halifax. The evidence showed that the deceased had been in the habit of taking narcotics for sleeplessness, and on January 16 he took an overdose, but was brought round after being ten hours unconscious. Harry on Sunday morning be Wed taken suddenly ill and died, and the post-mortem examination pointed to strychnine poison- mg. The jury found that be died from strychnine poisoning, but there was no evidence to show how the poison had been administered.</p>
        <p>THE NEW DOCK AT BRISTOL. OF- WHICH THE YIIO3T 500 WAS CUT BY It.H.H. THE orn:,ci, OP WALM ON WEDNESDAY. of excavating the new dock by setting iü motion steam paltry "—an interesting departure from ,usual sod-cutting ceremony. Bristol is making 'bold bid for a revival of her old-time importance as a port, and the dock extensions begun on Wednesday will place her on a level with Some o our leading ports in accommodation for shipping. The undertaking will cost the city Somect wo millions sterling, the scheme comprising the construction of a dock with a water space of some .twenty-five-and.a-half acres, capable; of further increase to forty acres in ; more than 3,000 ft. of quay frontage," about 325,000 square feet of shed accommodation, something like four miles of railway sidings, and berthage and both dry and wet docks for vessels nearly 150 feet in excess in length of anything to- day even contemplated. There is no truer Saying than that "facilities bring traffic," .and we may take it that Bristol, which has a hinterland of manufacturing and populous districts to provide it with exports,/and to take its imports will, with the good railway connection it has with London, soon again take its place as a great port of the Kingdom.</p>
        <p>We have not yet captured the De Wet we haike been ufter so long, but we have caught his son, which as far as it goes is very satisfactory, though unquestionably the capture of the father is the great strategic point of the War as it stands at the moment. However, Lord Kitchener's " Majuba Day" cable was very good news, and shows that by his indomitable perseverance and masterful organi- sation he has driven another nail into the coffin of</p>
        <p>this weary War. During the last month the two great " drives " of the Commander-in-Chief, the result of a year's planning and working— not showy, but sure—have accounted for con- siderably over a thousand Doers, which is a sub,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BRITANNIA ROYAL NAVAL COLLEGE: DARTMOUTH.</head>
        <p>BRITANNIA ROYAL NAVAL COLLEGE: DARTMOUTH.</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT TO DA:</p>
        <p>'MOUTH AND •DEVONPORT.</p>
        <p>stantial reduction from their army in Orange River Colony—to say nothing of guns, rifles, a*- munition, cattle, horses, stores and wagons, the necessary nerva belli. The scene of this second great attempt to lay De Wet by the heels, whisk failed only in the main point of capturing him personally, :was speaking roughly a stretch of coun-</p>
        <p>try. the shape of</p>
        <p>an isosceles irian-</p>
        <p>gle, with Frank-</p>
        <p>Yort as the apex,</p>
        <p>and the Drakens:</p>
        <p>berg mountains on the Natal fron- tier as the base, with Botha's and</p>
        <p>Vanßeenen's Passes as points, the Southern line or left hand side</p>
        <p>of the triangle consisting of the Wilge River, and</p>
        <p>Bethlehem.</p>
        <p>Harrismith - Van CAPTURED IN TICE GREAT "DRIVE." Reenen line of</p>
        <p>blockhouses. The columns started driving the Boers south, but they broke back north and tried to make a rush through at Tafelkop, where they failed, owing to the brilliant, but costly resistance of the gallant New Zealanders. Small numbers broke through at one or two places, but the rest found themselves in a eul-de-sao on the southern line and had to give in. But the victory was by no means cheaply bought, satisfao tory as it is, as the New Zealanders, not to mention others, suffered heavy losses, and on the other hand, it was somewhat counteracted by the important capture of a convoy and nearly five hundred melt at Klerksdorp, into which Lord Kitchener is ia; quiring closely. Over a hundred of our men were killed or wounded, and it looks as though they were caught napping isomehow. Colonel Anderson, of the Imperial Yeomanry, was in command, and his capture delayed any detailed account coming through. Major Enderby, who commanded the Infantry, was wounded, and the troops engaged principally consisted of the Ist Northumberland Fusiliers, the "Fighting Fifth," who met with a disaster at Reddersburg two years ago, and the sth Battalion Imperial Yeomanry, which was mainly recruited from Newcastle-on,Tyne. How- ever this is one of the bitters of war whioh we have to take with the sweets of victory, but we may confidently hope that Lord Kite,hener's second " drive " is a decided stepping stone to pence. Young De Wet, of whom we give a portrait, is twenty-three, and was his father's secretary. The private court-martial, which General Truman demanded to enquire into his dismissal from his post at .the War Office- and compulsory retirement, is looked upon by the general public as a sort of sop to Cerberus or partial yielding on the part of the Government to the unanimous voice of public opinion, but that is riot so, as it was simply the right of the officer in question to demand this means of clearing himself if he can. On the point lof an enquiry into the general ques- tion of remounts, meat contracts, transport an d such matters, in teeth of criticism not only from the Opposition, but also from the supporters af the Government THE .EMOUNT SCANDALS': , and the Minis- GENERAL SIR RANDOLPH terial Press, some BIDDIMPH 4 of which hate WHO PRESIDES OVER THE poken very TRUMAN INQUIRY. plainly and an- Co m promisingly against their attitude, Mr. Brodriek has all along been most contumacious, and declined to budge an inch from his original position, matters being brought to a decided head, and finally debated anent the question asked on Thursday last week by Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman in the House of Commons. The public have been unable to under- stand why a proper inquiry, covering the whole ground and conducted in public, should not take place immediately, while everyone is hot on the subject, and there is no reason why such a Commission should be prejudicial to the government through the War Office, for which it is responsible. It would be chur- lish to deny that that much - criticised organisation has done some very fine work in the course of the War, and errors in certain depart- ments are not an altogether unpardonable slur on its reputation, considering the scope and length of the campaign in South Africa. It would have been almost superhuman, if it had carried every- thing through without a hitch, and it is, to say the least of it, an error in tactics on the part of the Government not to have conceded this point without demur, basing its general • defence upon oonably done by the department. But to return to the Truman inquiry, which has been sitting at St. Stephen's House, Thames Embankment, Westminster, no criticism can ...be offered, as the proceedings have been in camera and consequently much discussed, and all we know definitely is that the War Office has endeavoured to make the General in question their scapegoat, and that he has refused the honour. However, the constitution of 'the court guarantees him as fair a hearing as any man could ask for, as his judges are presided over by the old Crimean veteran. General Sir Randolph Biddulph, G. 0.8., G.C.M.G., who is assisted by Major-General Sir Frederick Maurice, K.C,8., commanding the Woolwich District, Major-General T. A. Cooke (late 17th Lancers), Major-General Sir G. de C. Morton, K.C.1.E., C. 8., commanding the Dublin District, and Major-General Arthur Paget, C.V.O. (late Scots Guards), who did suc4 good service at the front,</p>
