      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>With the King and Queen in Devonshire and the Prince and Princess of Wales in Gloucester- hire this has been a memorable week fer the south-west of England. The Royal visits to Dartmouth, Devonport, and Bristol are, moreover, of much more than local interest. Their Majesties and the Heir-Apparent and his Consort have jour- neyed westward to perform ceremonies of national importance, and even outside the British Empire two of the functions—the laying of the founda- tion stone of the Britannia Royal Naval College at Dartmouth and the launch of H.M.S. Queen at Devonport have attracted considerable atten- tion, as is shown by the presence of the German training -ship Moltke, specially sent by the Kaiser to represent Germany On the occasion. The new Marine Academy will be a remarkably fine building, beautifully situated on the high ground above the town and the river Dart. The launch of the Queen by her Majesty and the laying of the keel of the battleship Ring Edward VII., to be built on the same slip, are, however, perhaps of more general interest. It is leis than twelve months since the building of the Queen was commenced, and as when ready for sea she will have a displacement of 15,000 tons, the short time occupied upon her construction speaks eloquently to the efficiency of the staff under Mr. H. R. Champness, the Chief Constructor at the Devonport Dockyard, and more especially of that official himself. The Queen will in most respects resemble the Bulwark, but will be superior in armour, having a steel belt nine inches thick and fifteen feet wide, commencing thirty feet from the bow and extending two hundred and twenty feet. Her engines will be capable of developing nineteen knots at 20,000 horse-power. Her dimensions are : length, 400 ft.; breadth, 75 ft.; mean-load draught 26 ft. 9 in. Electric, power will be an important factor on the Queen, which will carry, not including machine guns and tor- pedoes four 12in. guns, twelve 6in, quick-firers, and thirty smaller quick-firing guns. When com- pleted she will have cost considerably over 'a million of money. If Possible, after the launch, the King will lay the keel of the first-class battle- ship, King Edward VII., which, as already stated, is also to be built at Devonport, on the same slip as the Queen. She will be the largest battleship ever built for the British Navy, and will cost £1,300,000. With all her coal and stores on board, the King Edward will displace about 17,500 or almost 18,000 tons. Her nominal, displacement is 16,500 tons with less than half her coal on board. She will be 420 feet long, which is 30 feet shorter than the latest American designs, and 14 feet shorter than the big French ships of the Patrie clar.s ; but her comparative shortness is due to the fact that our Navy has always been against very‘long ships, probably because of their unhandiness. The battery of the new Leviathan will be a complete departure from British traditions, and with a picked crew her pins should be able to fire five tons of metal at an enemy during each minute that she is engaged. She will have speed of at least eighteen knots. Throe other battleships of the same cla.se are to be laid down— a formidable addition to our First Line of Defence, as even the Navy League must admit. At Bristol the Prince and Princess of Wales met with an enthusiastic reception. The loyal,old city Was very gaily decked, and the whole of the resi- dents must have turned out to greet their Royal Highnesses, to judge by the cheering crowds which thronged tie streets and the vicinity-of the docks. The Prince and Princess journeyed by special train from Badminton, where they had been staying since Monday as the guests of the Duke and Duchess of 13eaufort, to Avonmouth, where he started the work</p>
      </div>
