      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>MUSIC FOR THE MILLION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Fierce Competition in Manchester.</head>
        <p>Fierce Competition in Manchester.</p>
        <p>Few people have are idea of the fierce competition which exists in the profession of music teaching. It is as keen as anything in commerce. Apart from the schools and colleges of music. there is a veritable army dependent on music for a livelihood. Newspaper in- quiry shows that matters could hardly be worse for the financial welfare of those engaged in it. "Organisation might do something," said one well known music teacher to a reporter. " The situation is certainly about as bad as it could be. Now music teaching has degemerated from an art to a scramble for existenee," said another—Mils A Fog's, of 13,Eva-street, Rusholme. " Its a constant struegle," she went on,"and in my own case has been more than that. I was left an orphan early in life, and an injury to my spine made me a cripple. When doctors bills are to pay and there is no way of getting about to secure pupils the situation is not inviting. That was my case." Still Miss Fogg was cheerful, but the constant use of a walking stick to get about the house, and tes and from the piano, told of spinal weaknes. "I am not as bad as I was," she continsred. " For years I was weak and iii through my infirmity until I was fortunately persuaded to try Dr. Williams' pink pille, for pale people. The im- provement I experienced was wonderful. Fifteen years I ago, when living at Bolton, the spinal weakness first , showed itself. Since then I have had a monotonous I round of doctors, and continual pain from encasement in plaster of Paris and steel jackets. The doctors kept me alive at least. I consulted doctors at Bolton, Brad- ford, and Rochdale, being in the two last named places as governess. The Rochdale doctor was extremely kind and refused to send me a bill. 'An orphan's money would not help me,' he said when I asked him fcr it. The effect of the spinal injury on my general health was bad I needed fresh air and exercise, but could not get it. I lost my appetite and became weak and ill. I struggled on in Rochdale for three years always an invalid, but etern necessity forced me, as well as I was able, to earn my livelihood. Then I came to Rusholme, where I have been living ever since, teaching music, but it has been a hard struggle, and,it is useless to deny that I ant in only _very moderate circumstances."</p>
        <p>" May I ask how old you are, Miss Fogg 2" " Well, I am over thirty," and she smiled graciously. " And it is through taking Dr Williams' pink pills that you became as well as you are? As you sit there no one would think anything ailed you." " Yes." she replied enthusiastically, " I owe it all to Dr. Williams' pink pills." Abopt six, months ago one of my pupils maid to me, Why don't you try Dr. Williams pink pills for pale people?' She went on to tell me she had suffered from five abscesses is her - side, that several doctors had given boy up as incurable, and that . she then took Dr. Williams' pink pills, and that although she felt Worse after the first box, tour boxes cured her, and she had been to a dance since. "As I have told yeu, I tried them, and nothing has aver given me the relief that I have derived from taking, Dr. Williams' pink pills. I have taken fourteen boxes. By the time I bad taken the third box I felt a different bsine altogether"</p>
        <p>"You followed the instructions sent villa Dr. Williams' pink pills P"</p>
        <p>'I did, Most 'rigidly. I got gradually stronger, my appetite Increased ; I could Sleep better ; and I feel that if I could afford to go on taking them they would cure me outright. All I want now is to ,be able to get about without my etich. I want open air. At present my hip and left leg keep me back. I have, however, been able to rt across the road, something I have never dons, before. The doctor called to see me the other day, and he said, 'Why, Miss Fogg, you are looking better ; you are walking better.' I replied, • Yes. I have been taking Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people.'" " Are you taking the pills now ?" "No ; I finished my last box about three weeks ago," "And you are willing to have published your name and address and a report of what you have said ?" "Yes," she replied readily, and Miss Dent, who lives with Miss Fogg, was present during the whole of tho interview and confirmed the music teachci's narrative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>THE WESLEY GlllLD.—Special interest was taken in the Guild meeting on Monday evening, when the Rev Theodore Bishop paid a visit. In the early part of the evening he delighted an audience of juveniles with a beautiful selection of lantern views or Scotch scenery Later at the Guild meeting, he gave a very interesting description of his travels in Switzerland and Italy, the capital series of pictures tbrown on the screen giving a very realistic idea of the beauties of the countriee. A vote of thanks, moved by Dr Bennett, and seconded by Mr William Bennett, was accorded to Mr Bishop for his admirable lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Associated Cham- bers of Commerce which has been held this week in London, the Warrington Chamber has been represented by the president (Mr CR Parker). Mr William Long, an Councillor Bennett (hen. secre- tary.)</p>
        <p>The Election of Auditors. The poll for the election of two elective auditors for the borough took place on Saturday, in the Parr Hall. At eight o'clock the ballot boxes were taken to the Council Chamber at the Tewia Hall, where the counting was proceeded with in the pre- sence of Alderman James Pairclough (who had been appointed the returning officer by the Mayor), the Town Clad:, the candidates and others. The result WAS as follows :</p>
        <p>Edwin Bradshaw • ...... 647 John Harrison 603 Edmund Osborne ..... ......... 307 . There were 12 rejected votes. The poll was a heavy one compared with last year when there were four candidates in the held, namely Messrs Bradshaw, Harrison, Martin Flannegan and tz4_amuel Gough. Mr Bradshaw received 502 votes, Mr!-Harrison 443, Mr Flannagan 288 and Mr Gough 174, so that altogether 1412 persons voted against 1,757 on Saturday last.—Before leaving the Council ' Chamber, Mr Bradshaw proposed a vote of thanks to the Returning Officer, the Town Clerk and other officials, for the kind and courteous manner in which the candidates had been treated. It had,he said, been a perfectly friendly battle, 80 far as the candidates were concerned, and he (the speaker) was satisfied.—Mr Harrison seconded, and Mr Osborne supported the motion, the latter rel. I marking that he congratulated his opponents on the fight, which bad been a good one, lie was quite satisfied with it although he should have been better pleased if be had been at the top of the poll. (Laughter).—The motion was heartily aueed to.— Alderman Fairclough said it had been a pleasure to him to give his services. He believed all the candi- dates wen good men but one of them had to be de- feated. The result was then declared from the Town Hall steps. Prize Distribution at the Drill Hall.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the prizes gained in the recent billiard handicap, in connection with the sergeants' :mess of the Ist Battalion South Lanca- shire Regiment, Prince of Wales's Volunteers *ere distributed at the Drill Hall. About 70 sergeants and friends were present and the chair was °con- pied by Sergeant-Major Rawlinson. An excellent hot-pot supper was served by Mrs Rawlinson, and gave every satisfaction. A capital programme of vocal and instrumental items was gone through, songs,&amp;c,being rendered by the following: Pioneer. Sergeant Broadhurst, Sergeants Warburton, Scott, Richardson,Browne,and Lloyd ; and Messrs Forster, and JEmson (humorous). In the interval the prizes were distributed by Colour-Sergeant Wood (in the absence of Lieutenant Potter) to the following.— First prize, cue and case. Colour-Sergeant Cliff; 2, small marble timepiece,Colour-Sergeant Rawlinson, 3, pipes and case, Mr Walter Potter; 4. copper kettle, Sergeant Forster; 5, box of cigars, Sergeant Fern; 6, hearth rug, Colour-Sergeant Spencer; 7, bans, Transport-Sergeant Potter ; 8, leg of mutton, Quarter-master Sergeant Willcocks ; fl, shine Ser- geant Masssy ; 10, picture, Sergeant Bull ; 11, knives and forks,Colour-Sergeant Rook ; 12. wooden spoon (for smallest score), Seraatt-Instructor , lngralr•</p>
        <p>Sudden Death of an Engine Driver. At about half-past two on Tuesday afternoon an engine-driver named David Parr, of Wavertree, Liverpool, who was employed on a ballast train at Bank Quay Station, complained to his fireman of feeling, ill. The fireman called for assistance, and when deceased was removed from the engine, he immediately fell to the floor. Dr Beside was sent for, and on arrival he pronounced life extinct. The body was removed to the new Police Buildings. As death was due to apoplexy an inquest was not deemed necessary.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA can always DA replied apon for its purity. Adulterited cocoas are risky and should be avoided CADBURY' COCOA is highly beneficial to young and old the delicate and the robust : it contains all the full nourish- ing properties cf the Cocoa bean. It Is Cocoa and Cocoa Only. (3) TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable for tropical Climates; it is light, clean, cool, cmafortable, and durable; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITE &amp; WRIGHT Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw-street, opposite Top of Bold-street, Liverpool.—A.dyt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. A Bridge Street Bargain Criticised.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL. A Bridge Street Bargain Criticised.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, the Mayor pre- siding. There were also present :—The Deputy- Mayor (Alderman 11 Roberts) ; Aldermen J Fair- clough,J Shaw Citreen,T Hutchinson,F W Monks,and T H Sutton; Councillors J Baird, T Burton, James Evan., John Evans, L Ellison, W J Forohaw. L Greening, T J Hulse, A Jolley. A J Jolley, T B G Plumpton, J Ebirt, F R Roberts, William Roberts, W Rogers, J Shillcook, J Smethurst, D Tinnion, J Wilkinson; with Mr J L Whittle (Town Clerk), Mr S Wilson (Deputy Town Clerk), Mr J Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), Mr T Longdin (Borough Surveyor), Mr J Dees (Water Engineer), Mr C Madeley (Librarian), and Mr W II Ormaidale (Electrical Engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>I.A.TE MR. C .T. HOLUES.</head>
        <p>THE I.A.TE MR. C .T. HOLUES.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he had one communication to make which he was sure would have the sympathy of the whole Council. He referred to the death of a one-time Alderman of the Corporation, the late Mr CJ Holmes. He understood that he entered he Town Council in 1852, and he knew well the good work be had done during the many years he de- voted his time and talents to the services of the Council. He received the honour he well deserved of an honorary freedom of the borough, and was twice Mayor, and for a great number of years was chairman of the Finance and Estate and Gas Com- mittees. His was an example they all hoped would be followed, for his was a character they must all 3dmire as well as his devotion to business aud capacity for transacting it. He moved " That this Council receives with deep regret the announce- ment of the death of Charles John Holmes, E,q , a Honorary 'Freeman, Justice of the Peace, and formerly twice Mayor of the borough, and desires to record the great loss which the borough has sus- tained by his death, and to express its appreciation of the long and valuable public set. vices rendered by him, and that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to Mrs Holmes and her family the sincere sympathy of the Council with them in their be- reavement." Toe DENITY-MAYOR said he had the melancholy satis- faction of seconding the resolution, and of endorsing in every possible respect the utterance the Mayor had made in regtrd to the late Alderman Holmes. It there was one ming cvlrch Vie younger men ought to bear in snind it was that when a man's time for public service had expired and he still lived on they ought not to for- get the time ha had given to public work, and as far as they could they should pay him the respect that was due to him fo• that public service. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was csrried. ELECTION OF AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>The TORN CLanx announced the result of the election of elective auditors. He also intimated that the Mayor had appointed Councillo4 A J Jolley as his auditor for the ensuing year. FINANCE AND ESTATE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL NON-TRADING COMMITTEt AND THE INSPECTION OF ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on tee 10th February, the com- mittee considered the Urban Site Value Rating Bill, and a request of Mt Trevelvan, M.P., one of the promoters, to ask the Bniough Member to support the second reading, when it WAS resolved that no action be taken in the matter. The committee considered the Local Authorities Officers' Adoptive Superannuation 8i11,1902, which WWI referred to this committee by the General Purposes Committee. It was resolved that the pro- moters of the B II be requested to amend it so as to save the power of tee Corporation to establish a thrift fund for the servants of the Corporation, to which the Bill does not apply. At a meeting held on the 24th February it was resolved that Messrs Jamea Fairclisueh, Arthur Bennett, Joseph Shaw Green, and Frederick William Monks be appoMted overseers of the poor of the township of Warrington, for the terming year. Messrs Spencer Wellhead and Son baying applied, on behalf of the Municipal Non-Trading Committee, to inspect the orders for payment of accounts for the year ending 31st March, 1900, it was resolved that while the committee are advised that a burgess is not entitled to inspect the orders for payment of that year, the committee are prepared to allow an inspection to be made. The Secretary of the Municipal Officers' Association, having agreed to amend the Local Authorities Officers' Adoptive Seperannuation Bill in the manner proposed by this committee.it was resolved, that the Council be recommended to petition in favour of the Bill. The Corporation of Bradford having requested the Corporation to petition ParPament in favour of the taxation of land values, it was resolved, that no action be taken in the matter. The Town Clerk reported that the plot of land on the north side of Monks•street lead been sold by public auction for the sum of £490. Alderman &amp;taw GREEN moved the adoption of these moiautee. Alderman FAIRCLOUGEE seconded. Councillor Setwrarresr said a step in the right direction had been taken in allowing Messrs Wellhead and Son pormission to inspect the orders for payments of accounts. It was a step towards' settling all the dis- putes going on at the present time. The minute.; were nonfir med. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COM- MITTEE. THE WIDTH OF SPRIN3FIBLD-STREET.</p>
        <p>At meetings, held on the 30th January and 13th February, the Town C:erk brought under the notice of the committee the question of the widening of Spring- field-street, and after consideration it was decided that it was undevirable to widen such street. It was resolved that the following members be appointed a sub- committee to consider the provision of new rcutes from the central parts of the borough into Cheshire, viz : The Chairman, Deputy Chairman, acid Mr Councillor A J Jolley. The minutes were adopted on the motion of Alderman 'SUTTON, seconded by Councillor PLUMPTON. HE ACTH COMMITTEE. CLAUSE 11. OF TETE VACCINATION ACT, 1398. At a meeting on the 12th February it was resolved that representation be made to the Local Government Board that steps should be taken to repeal Claim IL of ths Vacenation Act, 1893. The Mediell Orficier of Health baying recommended that chicken-pox should be made a notifiable disease for a period of six months, It was eeolved that steps be taken in favour of Lhe recommen- dation. Alderman SUTTON moved the adoption of the minutes. The DEPUTY Maxon seconded, and it was carried. PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to report upon the secreting and condition of Marsh EIOU4P-‘8.136 and Padgete-lane. Councillor TINNION, in moving the coofirmation of the minutes, drew attention to the resolution, and said ' be bad received a report from the Berough Surveyor but It had not yet been submitted to the committee. This was a matter they would have taken up previously, but for the simple reason that they did not wish to overstep their estimate, but now that they were getting nearer to the financial year end they intended taking the matter up and including the cost in the estimate for next year. Tile lanes would be put in such a condition as to give satisfaction not only to the property owners but to all who lived in the neighbourhood. The Minutog were adopted., SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. THE EDUCATION BILL. REPORT OF THE TOWN CLERK.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the committee on the 20th February, the Town Clerk sub flitted the report on the Warrington Education Bill v7t.ich we published last week. Alderman SHIM Gangs moved the confirmation o the minutes. lie said the average attendance ',bowed a very considerable increase upon the attendance last year, the number b,ing no less than 11,508. He asked members to carefully consider tbe report of the Town Clerk with reference ta the interview with the Lord Chairman in London because it would have to be re- ferred to when they reached the minutes of the General Purposes Committee. Councillor Sumx.coca seconded. He informed the Council that on Thursday he attended a meeting of sub- scribers to the Industrial Ship Clio. The report was very good. The bays were in goad health and the increase in namber during the year had been from 233 to 265. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>MUSEUM COMMITTEE. THE TEACHERS' A.SSOCIATION. THE LATE MR. .PEERS. The DEPUTY-MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes, At the last meeting of the Council he referred to the unfortunate lose the committee had sustained bp the death of Mr Peers, and that fact was duly recorded on the minutes. The only other matter calling for special notice was the fact that the minutes intimated the receipt of a number of very valtrahl gifts. He thought the Town Clerk had added to the indebtedness of the committee to him by making them a present of a volume of psalms and hymns. (Laughter.) He hoped due consideration of that volume would have its proper effect upon the religious sentiments of the Council. (Laughter.) Councilor GREENING mondedi Councillor SMETHURST referred to a letter from Mr John Upson with regard to the representation of teachers n the committee, and to the resolution of the commit- tee. He hoped that when the time came to appoint any more members the teachers would be considered. They were a class who ought to be encouraged to take an active part in the affairs of the town. Alderman MONIS said he took a directly opposite view from Councillor Smethurst on this question. He did</p>
        <p>not see that they bad any right to expect to be elected on this ceminittee in this sort of way. IS would be a bad principle to permit any outsido body to nominate representatives to seats in the control and gift of the Corporation. That question was not considered at the commitea meeting, and he only thought it necessary to refer to it now because Councillor Smethurst had done so. He thought he was right in referring again to the psalms and hymns. (Laughter.) VFB•3 it not an old book which had been printed in Warrington and which was particularly interesting and valuable on that accoun t ? .</p>
        <p>amax said that was so. The minutes were then passed. RIVERS COMMITTEE, RESIGNAIION OF ALDERMS.N MONKS.</p>
        <p>AN EXPLANATION. It was resolved at a meeting on the 24. th February, that the resignation of Mr Alderman Monks, as a mem- ber of this committee, be accepted with regret. Alderman ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes, and expressed his regret at the loss of tho services of Alderman Monks aa a colleague. They still hoped, and he hoped they would not be disappointed, to have his assistance in any important work that lay in front of thern. (Hoar. hear.) Alderman SUTTON seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST regretted the Alderman was resigning We position. It WPI3 an old but a true saying that it was dangerous to " swop " horses when crossing a stream. He was not going to insinuate aoything, hut at the last mee'iag of the committee it was decided to enter upon a litigation with the Ship Canal Company. It would have been far better if the Alderman bad icitanded to resign, to have dine so 12 months ago and not now. It did not look (pito straight, becaum the resolution had been passed and carried and they were going to law with the Ship Canal Company. Tney did not know as a committee where they were going to Land, and whether the cost would he. £l,OOO er £2,000 The MAYOR • You are a little out of order in discuss- ing the merits of the matter. Councillor SMETHURST added that he was sorry Alder- man Monks had thought proper to leave the committee when such an important law-suit was going on. Alderman MONKS skid Chit 50,120 inisappreheneion or misunderstanding might follow as the result of Coun- cillor Smethurst's observations. He (the speaker) sent in his resignation six weeks ago, before the committee meeting at which it was decided to take tbe proceedings was held. He resigned at the earliest opportunity when he saw how things o ere likely to go, and before it was possible to say whether the Corporation would side with the traders or otherwise. His reason for resigning was that he thought by doing so he was the more likely to strengthen the bands of the Corporation and the Case of the Cordoration. Ho resigned at once, and before the meetings were held, that it might not be said, that he in any, way disagreed with the policy which was being outlieed. The committee had ad opted the right course, and the Corporation also, and it was merely to strengthen the hands of the Corporation that he had seen fit to act as be had done. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were there agreed to. TRAMWAYS COMMITTEE. THE PROPOSED LIGHT RAILWAYS. THE LEVEL CROSSING AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>--- -- At a meeting held on the 12th February, it was resolved, that the tender of the St Helene Cable Com- pany, Limited, for feeder cables at £lB3 31 5,1, be aecepted. Toe committee further considered terms sub- mitted by the Manchester Ship Canal Conopanv with respect to the pruposed light railways, when it was resolved that the following further clime be agreed to :—The Corp'ration to execute at their own expense such works as may be required to insure the exast lateral alignment of the rails, but this condition shall not be held to relieve the Canal Company of their responsibility as regards the vertical alignment of the bridges. rim committee considered the terms submitted by the Stockton Heath Parish Council with respect to the proposed light rail- ways. Some of the terms were agreed to, some further conditions were submitted but not agreed to. The committee considered the terms submitted by the Lon- don and North-western Railway Co. with respect to the rails over the level crossings at ‘Vilderspool, and the arr angemente for signalling, lci, in order Co secure safety, and the engineer having advised teat the terms might be accepted by the Corporatoel it was resolved that the same be agreed to. Aldermah ROBIRTS moved the confl-mation of the minutes, add alluded to the resolution with reference to the proposed light railways. He said that some of the terms proposed were accepted and some were not, but the matter had been practically disposed of by the in- qeiry held that day and which had resulted AO far as the Commiseionere were concerned in their recommendation that the application of tee Corperation should be gran' ed. He did not think it dealeable or necessary to go through the conditions because the whole of the circum- stances were to some extent modified by what had been agreed to that day before the tribanal appointed. Alderman MONKS seconded, and it was agreed to, THE GYMNASIUM—APATHY OF SCHOOL MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION proposal the adoption of the minutes of the Gymnaeium Committee, and alluded to the resolution of the committee awarding the Thonias Morrie Memorial Shields to the hope and girls of Heath Side school, who had sent in the only entries for the School Children's Drill Competition. He remarked that the committee regretted very much that the managers of the elementary school in Warrington should accord them such unfavouraisle treatment. They did not get the help that they were entitled to expect in their efforts to promote the phys,cal welfare of the children of the town. Fo,t £5 per annum the sehools could send theie scholars to the Gymticaium, and receive instruction in physical education whioh could not he beaton in Eogland. They had an excellent Gymnasium and an excellent instroctor. He was told that the schoolmasters and managers were the most peculiar people to get aleng with in Warring- ton. The committee hoped they would see their way next year to recognise a little more fully the work of the Gymnasium Committee in the interests of the chil- dren. Councillor Sturmeocx seconded and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>TUE BRIDGI3 STREET IMPROVEMENTS. ACQUIRING OF PROPERTY. ANINILITED DISCUSSION. THE PUBLIC HOUSE num' COMPANY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee, the Ciasurtere of the Sehool Attendance Committee having reported the steps taken with regard to the Education Bill and the result of an interview with the Lord Chair- man of committees on the Bill as set out in the proceed- ings of the 'Scheel Attendance Committee, it was resolved that the draft Bill be amended to meet the temporary needs of the borough with respect to elemen- tary education. The report of the Parliamentary Committee with resp.ct to coronation festivities was read. A letter from the Liverpool and District Public- house Trust Company, Limited, expressing the willingness of the company to acquire or undertake the management of licensed premises now the property of the Corporation, or which maybe acquired by them in the course of pubic improver-neat was read,when it was resolved that the company be invited to submit any offer tees, may be prepared to Make in respect of any licensed premises belonging to the Corporation; and that on thi relowitneed akin of the Finance and Este tJmnnittee. a petitinn be presented in favour of the Lace' Authmities Officers' Adoptive Saparanr,u Won Bill, 1902, as amended on the repo nmendetion of that committee. The Ceinmilten resolved th tt the c otnne ea seal of the Cor- !peratien be affixed to an agreement between Mr James Repeerd ane th! C rep oration for the sale and exchange of land at the corner of Bridge-street and Rylands- street. The filevoa moved, and Alderman SUTTON seconded the adoption of the minutes. DOW.seiliOr PLIMPTON took exception to the agreement relating to the sale and esehanee of land at the Corner of Bridge-street,and said he should move an amendment. The Tower CLERK said that upon the authority of the General Purposes Committee the matter had been com- pleted and deeds with Mr Hepherd exchange. Councillor Peuererou : It was brought forward at the meeting of the committee in such a way that not a quarter of the members present understood it. The Degurr-Meron : Nonsense. Councillor PLIMPTON : It may be nonsense with you but it is not with Us. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: It is no compliment to their ietellecte to say that. Councillor PLIMPTON Alderman Roberts never men- tioned a word about it till it was forced out of him. , The DEPUTY•MAYOR : I male a complete statement of the case. Councillor PLUMPTON : After Mr Smethurst drew your attention to it,</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MkTOR : Certainly. Coulieitior PLUMPTON : I say that when it WAS sub- mitted to the committee there ought to have been an explanation before it was forced out of him. The DEPUTY-MAYOR : It was not f weed out of him. The CHAIRMAN : When an opinion is not asked for it is not always necessary to give it. Councillor PLIMPTON : When the agreement was brought before n the meeting there was no explanation at all given until they were b sing signed. I think lam in order in proposing an amendment. The DIAgoR : The agreement is already ex3hangol. is it on the agenda for :m.erWelyhyf or confizmation of the the The :C:onumeniiise:Lent int rg T i?: minutes so far as I read it. Councillor PUMPTON : .Consequently it means that we are entitled to discuss it. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: I hope It will not be supposed that we have the slightest objection to a full and thorough discussion. We shall only be too glad. I repudiate with all the strength of my nature the sug- gestion that particulars were forced out of me. Councillor FLUMPTON : I have only to repeat what I have already said. Councillor W ROBERTS: It is not true. You know it is not.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLumerox (coatinuing) said the Corpora- tion were giving up 42 yards of law.' to Mr Hepherd and were getting in return 15i yards. It was stated at to committee meeting that rue land they were giving up would be of no value to the Corporatica to sell but he submitted it was of great value to th 3 owner of the present hotel. Ia addition to the land the Corporation were giving the awn of £1,500 and he contended that the giving of the land only would have been quite sulk int. They pulled property down in Bridge-street and brought al old dilapidated house to the front. It was in a side stree'3 before. He contended that the street was already wide enough at that point and there was ample room on the footpath so they could allow it t) remain as it was. Ha moved an amen lment that the agreement be not considered in its present form and that it be allowed .o remain as it was. Councill6r WILKINSON, in seconding, asked when it was tnat the agreements were exchanged. The Town CLERK: They were exchanged this morn- ing. COUOCiIIOr WILKINSON : Whitt time this morning because, at 3 o'clock this afternoon the amount of land being given up by Mr Hepherd was not decided. I asked for information as to the moaeurements at three o'clock,and it was given to me as 221 yards. Now you move it to-night as Isi yards. How could the agree- ments be made out and exchanged when the measure- ments were not Axed?</p>
        <p>The Tocort CLERK : The plans were prepared more than twelve months ago and agreed to by the architects oa both sides. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON : I say they have been altered since three o'clock. _ .</p>
        <p>Tile Tows CIARIE said the togotiations had been going on for twelve months. It was agreed that the agreements shcned be exchanged about a fnrtnigtit ago. _</p>
        <p>la Councillor WILKINSON: That doeslinot set aside my queitton as to be alteration of quantity of land this atternoon. The Thin/TY-MAYOR: There is none. Councillor WILKINSON : There is. The DEFUTY-M&amp;Y)I2 I beg your pardon. It is not an alteration.</p>
        <p>`lCouncillor WILKINSON : lan speaking to the Mayor, not to you Mr ex-Mayor. Will you ask the Borough Sur- veyor to give an explanation. Someone else can state facts beside you. The DEPUTY-MAYOR : It IA a mistake. Connedlor WiLwitisoat : I have seen them with my own eyes. The DEPUTY-MAYOR You are not the Chairman of the Property Purchasing Committee or you would know better. .</p>
        <p>' Councillor WILKINSON' : lam not the Chairman. The MIPUTY-MATOR : But I am. Tne MAYOR called upon Mr Longdin to give an ex- planation. Mr LON6DIN : The plan I showed Mr Wilkinson was an original plan and I told him I would reter to the agreement to get the plan that was to be put on the deed. Three plans have been prepared and I hadn't a copy at the moment, being engaged at the Light Rail- way Commissioners inquiry. The plan he saw shows that we are receiving 151 yards against 42. The MAYOR: That is the one which was before the General Purposes Committee. The DEPUTY Meeoit said the matter had been the sub- ject of +discussion and negotiations for nearly two years and the committee bed spent an amount of time upon it which certainly would not hays been considered ex- cessive if £lOO,OOO had been involved instead of the small amount involved in the present case. He did not bold a brief for Mr Hepherd or anyone else connected with this trade but he should always try to be fair te any applicant who had to be considered in any relations with that Corporation. There were twoiprincipel ele- ments the consideration of the case, and Mr Plumpton, although he knew better, and was in possession of factsiwhich ought to have guided him, stated part of the truth and only a part of it. He did not give the whole case and he (Alderman Roberts) did not know why he should not. It was quite true they were giving a larger amount of land than they were receiving, but Mr Plumpton omitted to seat° what he ( Aldesnaaa Roberts) stated at the meet- ing of the General Purposes Committee and was perfectly within his knowledge, that the lights of Mr Hepherd's property dominated this lend acd therefore the market value of this landlwas very small indeed. It was therefore out of all propor- tion to suggest that the exchange of land was anything like an equivalent for what the Corporation were taking from Mr Hepherd. They had sufficient ,evidence to satisfy them that the taking off of this addi- tional portion of Mr Hepherd's property meant absolutely internal reconstrucion of the ' Royal Court Hotel. They had not only the estimate of the architect employed by Mr Hepherd but they had the,r own architect's estimate of the coat of the alteration to Mr ilepherd's premises and he thought he was right whoa he said that the sum agreed upon was £5OO or at least £250 less Mean their own arbitrator adjulicated as being fair value. If the Committee were to be hampered by taking two years for the purchase of every little bit of property that had to he adjudicated upon in the interests of the Corporation for the widen- ing of Bridge-street when on earth was the werk going to be done. The EX-Melton proceeded to refer to the difficulties encountered by the Committee, and said he had seriously contemplated more than once retiring from the Committee altogether. The committee found that if they could privately settle with the ownera, even if they might appear t be giving a large sum, they were saving money by avoiding arbitration. The cost of arbitration was sireoly horrible, and the sooner a more eqeitable basis was found for arbitration proceedings, the better it would be for all concerned. At present they had either to pay by private arrangement, and to some extent, pay what might appear the market value of the property, or go to arbi- tration, and if the arbitrators decided uoon a Bu:a in eX:1888 of the sum the Corporation offered, the whole costs fell on the Corporation and made the whole cost more than thy contemplated. The property in question was oot old and dilapidated and it was not fair to describe it in those terms. Councillor Tneepost contended that as the question had been befeee the committee for two years t aey had shown unnecessary haste at the end of the negotiations in having the agreeroeate exehanged that morning coo- sidering that they WO:3 holding a Council meeting. He did not think that whoever was responsible for it could be blind to the fact that there was considerable opposi- tion to the matter at the General Purpeses Committee, Under the circumetances it was a great mistake that the agreernenti had been exchanged. The Toni CLERK: I accept the entire responsibility for that. Councillor Treeme : I don't care who accepts the responsibility. I am here to protest against it and it does not relieve me of my reaponsibilite.. It bad been said for sOttle time, continued Councillor Tinnien and he thought on this occasion with some amount of truth that the comtnittee had dealt with matters in such a way that was not in the best interests • of the town. This was a glaring case that seemed to corroborate the statement but he Wa3 happy to say it seldom occurred. Councillor Tinnier: proceeded to point out that it was an advantage for the public house to be brought to the main street and he considered it Was ample reward for the land Mr Hepherd gave up to the Corporation. It was not a bargain except to the owner of the property. Ile did not think the members of the Council should be " plied over tbe coals" in an unfair way for objecting to any proposal. Toe DEPUTY-MAYOR : What I object to is a oatmeal- meat of fact known to the speaker, which lees an impor- tant bearing on the discussion. Councillor TINNION : I think you could express it with a little less heat. I think not so much would have been made of it if the replies had been given in a little bit gentler manner. The DRPUTY-MAYOR : The facts were given gently at the General Purposes Committee. Councillor Wireriesme asked the Mayor's permission to resume his speech which was interrupted, when Mr Longdin was called upon to explain the plans. After discussion Councillor WILKINSON was allowed to proceed and asked the members of the Council to cora- pare the amount which would be handed to Mr Hepherd with the cost of the Feathers Hotel which was recon- structed for £2,573. Referring to the heated way in which Alderman Roberts " tackled " him he said he still maintained that since three o'clock that day there had been a" shutting " of the line mark on the plans.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR: There has not. Councillor Witatorsory : I maintain it. Toe DEPUTY MAYOR : I pretest against that statement I say the line shown on the plan to-day was the line agreed upon between the committee and Mr Elepherd when the agreement was finally settled and there has been no alteration. As a matter of fact I was absolutely ignorant until I heard the statement to-night that the agreement had been exchanged to-day. I don't think it is fair to make a suggestion that there had been any- thing that is not straightforward and above board in the matter. The Tews CLEEIT explsined that Councillor Wilkin- son was alluding to a plan showing two alternative lines. The Corporation called in Mr Kirby to give a fair valua- tion and a plan was prepared after he had advised them. Alderman SHAW GREEN pointed out that etiess had been laid upon the fact that they were giving Mr Ilepherd a frontage. That frontaze was given to him before the negotiations began. He showed haw that in puliing down property on the main street, the com- mittee sometimes neglect-A to sae what effect it wonlil have on the property that remained. When they took down the cld shops at the corner of Rylands-street, and Bridge-street, they brought Mr Hepherd's propeity to the front and the Corporation paved the street for him. They did not give it him intentionally, and Mr Hepherd innocently became toe owner of the frontage. (Laughter). When the negotiations were entered upon they were unable to take into consideration the fact that Mr Hepherel had acquired a frontage. They might do the same thing again if they were not very careful and he thought the eyes of the committee and the Council had been opened to such circumstances and he had reason to believe ne such mishap would occur again. Considering all the circumstances he thought they had made a very good bargain.</p>
        <p>Alderman SIMON said his experience of the Property Purchasing Committee was that they never desired to give too much br property. Rethought the Town Clerk was to some extent justified in taking the action he had done, as the reiriluti .n was passed by the General Pur- poses Committee which comprised the whole Council. Alderman 13IITCHINsON said th 3 front entrance to the hotel would be of no more value than the side. lie didn't consider the sum was too much, considering tbe importance of having a straight line in Bridge-street. ' CoaneillOr Jesfa3 EVANS raised the question as to whether a resolutio of the General Purposes Committee wee binding on the Councd until the minutes had been adopted. Tee TOWN CLERK, in reply, read the standing orders respecting the Geoeral Purposes Committee, one being that it should trans Act business which Could not be con- veniently postpone I to the Council. The AAYOR said he took it th .t the resolution would not be bindiny until confirmed by the Council.' Councillor Evaxs submitted that the matter under discussion was not of RUC."' an urgent character as re require action to be taken withadt the sanction of the Council. On a vote bning taken the amendment was lost, only five outing for it.</p>
        <p>*Coancillirilleanns drew attention to the resolution respecting the Public House Trost Company's letter and asked whothey the hotols wooll b lot by tender. Fie objected to the Trust Cmspany receiving any prefer- ence.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said hil feeling was that the company should be allowed to make an -.ffer in herrn-non with any apolieenr and no nrer-rence should ho enown. Councillor ROGERS, on this und,ntanding withdrew hie oppoon. id ,he minutes were confirmed. EXTENSION OF THE GAS OFFICES. Oa the motion of Alderman FAIRCLOUGH, the followine resolutinn was agreed to That the tender of Mr F W VVright for extension to the offices in Mersey-stre,t according to drawings and specifications at £993 be accepted subject to terms being made with the contrac- tor to execute the work so a+ not to interfere with the business of the department." In answer to a qu4stion bg Alderman Movers, Alder- man FAIRCLOCYCIR said that if at an g time it was found convenient for any portien of the gms offices to be removed to Bank House the Committee wonld give it favourable consideration.</p>
        <p>A rPo-wrt nf the digmevon reaneotine• the prop ,sed inquiry int) the trad,ng traleadlons of the Corperation will be found in another °ninny'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>rnE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 7 1902.</head>
        <p>rnE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 7 1902.</p>
        <p>s.e4vi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EVENING OF LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE EVENING OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>In Nature all things are admirably balanced, and the critic sands dumb at contemplation of their perf,dion. The evening is as beautiful as the moraine, and it is hard to say whieh has the greater glory, sunrise or sun- set. The freshness and increasing splendour of the one is offset by the sup-rb grandeur and decadence of the other. And so it is with mankind. The innocence and eagerness of yonsh are delightful, but not more so than the sedateness and wisdom of age. As in Nature each hour of the twenty-four has its own particular ohs rm, so in Man has each of the wen decades allotted to him by the Psalmist. Naturally, there is no reason why one period of ten years in a humAn life should be more healthful than another varied of ton years ; stronger, if you will, but not more health- ful. But what do we find ? Every day some one or other,in complaining that he or she suffers from indiges- tion, rheumatism, or what not, explains the fact by saving,!' But then, you see. I'm turned 50." or 60. as the care may he. Rubbish ! Let such folks consider the ease of Mr Gladstone, who livPd to a sine ambeing practically free from ailments the whole of his life, and I-staining his graarl intellect to the very last. Why was this ? He was of a healthy stock, took care of big 'health, and so came nearer the realisation of what the ancient Greeks called euthanasia than any other modern instance we know of. That the system of a peeson who bee passed the middle pra.;ori nf life will satisfactorily respond to cor- rect medical treatment the case of Mrs: Sailer. of West Merseamear Colcheste-, proves." I have nPver been ("an- ger tartly ill," said Mrs Steles% on 21st October, 1901. "but for years I have suffered from indigestion. I used th get up of morning with a feeling of depression an want of vitality. MY esnression was that of a person tired and worried. I had no apnetite. and a pain at the heart and between the shoulders frovently made me feel sick and faint. There Was a nauseous repetitial of whatever I ate, and altogether things were In a bad way with me. when one day a neighbour re- nommended ma to try Mother SPigei's Curative Syrup. She followed tin her recommendation by actually giving me a dose; and I round such relief from it that I hough:, a small b-t`le myself, and later a larger sized bottle. That was dl I reeded. Soon the sickening pain at the heart; left me. My appetite improved, and it Seemed as if I lied taken a new lease of life. At the least sign of the return of my former complaint I take a doze of I mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and always with the happiest result." That one has suffered for a long while Is no argutrmt that an eatit shreald he allowed to range unchecked. And Pam Sadler's case furnishes an excellent example.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>London Hackney Show. – •</head>
        <p>The London Hackney Show. - •</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Bart.; has been a very successful exhibitor at this show daring the past week, his exhibits having carried off prizes in several &amp;asses. A Successful Building Society. Alderman Sutton manager) presided at the annual meeting of the Borough of Warrington In- corporated Permanent Benefit Building Society which was held at the offices of Messrs T Sutton and Sons in Lega-st on Thursday evening.—The 27th annual report of the directors was submitted and showed that the directors were able to advise the payment of a dividend at the rate of ..£4 per cent per annum, free of income tax, and to carry .£23 63 81 to next year's amount. Tha number of members on the society's books is 60, of which 17 are borrowers. The Chairman drew attention to the fact that the Society had not transacted much business during the pad year, the reason being that the bank rate had bees so high that it would not have paid them to advance money net mortgages at 4i per cent., when they had to pay 5 or 6 per cent. to the bank. Speaking of the business methods of the Society he :Aid they had never lost a penny since the commencement and had never failed to pay anyone who wished to withdraw money from the society. The report was adopted. The following directors were re-e'ected : Ikless:s John Andrews, chairman ; George Holford, T H A Pilkington,Williars Roberts.Lienry Houghton, Alfred H Clive. Alderman Shaw Green and Mr J H Clarke were re-elected auditors. Votes of thanks having been accorded to the directors and auditors, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CONCERT AT BRUNSWICK SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT BRUNSWICK SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A concert in aid of the Scholars' Treat Fund was given in the Brunswick School by local artistes. There was a good attendance, The Runcorn Male Voice Choir contributed no less than four pieces,and it would be difficult to single out any particular item for special mention where uniform excellence prevailed. The choir has never been heard in better form. The titles of the choruses were "Martyrs of the arena," "The pilgrim's chorus," "The Spartan heroes," and "The Anvil." The audience demanded an encore after the last-named, and the choir gave "The crusaders" in responge. Miss Humphreys captivated the house with "Barque of dreams and " Nirvana," and was re-called on each occasion, her encore pieces being "The Japanese love song," and " Love 1 is kind." Mr T Norman Johnson displayed his flue bass voice to advantage in " 'Neath the rolling tide," and Mr hichardson received an encore for his effective treatment of "The White Squall." "Sweet Jennie Bell " was given in reply. Ries Et Clare made herself popular with "The forge and the Bell," while Miss Baker gave a dramatic recital of " Mary Queen of Scots." "The noontide heat is past and over" was a pleasing item as sung by Miss E Clare and Mr W Shaw, their voices blend- ing in a manner which was feelingly appreciated by the audience. The two amusing pieces on the programme were a musical sketch entitled "The pump "and " Jenks's vegetable compound," sung by Messrs Pilgrim, Kniveton, Newell and Johnson. The latter fairly brought down the house with laughter and applause. and "In absence " was given as an encore piece. "The Young Royalist," by Mr E Johnson, roused the patriotic fervour of the company, and he was vociferously recalled. "Under the male old flag," as a reply, also found favourable acceptance. Mr J S Yates was an efficient accompanist.</p>
