      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>No inquiry Yet.</p>
        <p>Another Motion Rejected by the Town Council.</p>
        <p>The large number of the general public present at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday was evidence of the deeper interest which is now taken in matters municipal. Additional intereft was lent to the proceedings by the fact that Councillor James Evans was to bring forward his second resolution with respect to the demand for an inquiry into tne accounts of the Corporation. In effect the resolution was that accountants ap- pointed by the Corporation and municipal non-trad- ing committee, should be am.horised to examine the , aceounts of the Corporation for the last five years, and the Non-Trading Committee should be requested to formulate charges against members of the Cor- poration of trading either direct, indirect, or through limited companies with the Corporation, and submit them in writing to the accountants, who would inquire into and report thereon to the I Council. It was further proposed that the Mayor be authorised to obtain the advice of an independ- ent legal gentleman upon any question arising in the report of the accountants, and that the costs of the inquiry be paid equally by the Council and the Municipal Non-Trading Committee. Councillor Evens informed the meeting that he had only just one or two words to say in explana- tion of one part of the resolution. He dared say they would all remember that the previous resolu- tion he submitted to the Council dealt with specific charges, and it was owing to the difficulties that arose out of that resolution in preferring those specific charges tbat the resolution was not carried by the Council. Neither was it acceptable to the Municipal Non-Trading Cemmittee. Some people seemed to have an idea that it would end in a cerain amount of litigation. He (Councillor Evans) wished it to be 'understood by the word" charges that the " charges " were not to be preferred exactly against the person but against the trading I either by companies or persons; to formulate in other words matters to be inquired into with regard to the trading of persons, directly or indirectly, through limited company or otherwise, with the Corporation. They left out the individual and inquired into the trading with the Corporalon by any member. That was the explanation he wished to give to that part of the resolution, and with that little comment he would move the resolution stand- ing in his name. Councillor Trustron seconded the resolution, which, he said, had been put in its present shape by Councillor James Evans and himself. He was present to say that it was an honest attempt on their part at any rata to deal with the matter in reach a way that th. 4 charges that had been made against the members of the Corporation and against the Connell as a body should be proved to be either the truth or an untruth. They had approached the Non-Trading Committee, but they were not as successful as they would like to have been in the negotiations to arrive at an understanding with them as to what they would accept and what it was desirable should be passed by the Coun- cil. In its present form the resolution wag net acceptable to the Non-Trading Committee, but the question which troabled him the most was" Is it acceptable to the majority of the rate- payers of Warrington ? " (Hear, hear.) The first part of the resolution was acceptable, so far as he understood at any rate, to the Municipal Non- Trading Committee. The last portion was accept- able except that the committee went so far as to say they were prepared to accept the Town Clerk ; they did not wish for any independent legal gentle- man. But, as he (Councillor Tinnion) pointed out, this was a sort of permissive clause. If they were content to accept the Town Clerk as the legal authority he was prepared to accept him ; but he Was not quite sure whether they were. The prin- cipal objection that had been raised so far was to the making of the charges. Councillor James Evans and himself had endeavoured to make the resolution as wide as they could without making it a wading expedition. (Laughter.) They were aware of the fact that the Non-Trading Com- mittee had made certain chargesi. They naturally and reasonably assumed that they had some infor- mation, sufficient information to make those charges in the public way in Which they had been made. Therefore it was !mourned that the committee would have sufficient information to formulate the charges against members of the Council as to the various kinds of trading, They had used the word "charges " because they could not find a better one; It might:be their misfortune,it might be their ignor- ance, or both. (Laughter.) But they had honestly tried to finl a word er words that would convey their meaning, and they could not find any word better than" charges." they thought of " accusa- tions," "allegations," and "imputations,"— (laughter)—and all that sort of thing, but these words were far too strong, and not what they intended, and so far as the mover of the resolution and himself were concerned the word " charges " should mean their objections, if they liked. Aliermsn ROBERTS: Fabrieations would be a better word. (Hear, hear. an i laughter.) Councillor lINKoN : Of course it IS the way you look at these matters. I am not intendine to use strong language. it may be that some are fabrications. Alderman ROBERTS : lam only suggesting a imitable word.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : But you did not Puzgest the word tbr) other night in the Town Clerk's cfßce when we handed the regolution I.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS : It just occurred to MP. Councillor TINNIoN: At that time we were discussing a better word, and I think you admitted that you did not know a better word and we were in good company when we were not able to find a word to satisfy every. body. But it is not a question of the word-- Conncillor JAMES EvaNS : Of the principle. Councillor Imams It is a question of prineple, whether it is desirable in the interests of Warrington and of the ratepayers and every member of the Council to give an opportunity to the Nan-Trading Committee of formulating the width, length, height, and depth of any tribunal that has to investigate these charges. Continu ing, be said 3 they did net wish to limit the matter. If these charges had not been made previously by the i Municipal Non-Trading Committee they would have contented themselves with the first portion of the reso- lution which simply said that an investigation should take place. They felt that the committee had gone so far in making these accusations, allegations and impu tations—(laughter)—that they must necessarily have some information. He did not wish the Council to vote blindly, but if they voted for the resolution that night they did so on the district under- standing that the forrnul ton of the charges should be a wide as possible, so far as the Non-Trading Committee were concerned. It should not be a " wading condi- tion to find out if there was anything. If the com- mittee were prepared to allow the list of traders to be inquired into that would be sufficient for the Council, or into the various transactions of every limited ability , company in which members of the Town Council were interested. They did not feel justified in giving the com- mittee such a field that they could inquire into charges without first stating them in writing. He contended that the resolution, if carried, would go a long way to allay the unnecessary agitation, but he was not every- body, and the Council would do a wise thing to accept the resolution in the widest and broadest spirit. (Hear. hear.) Councillor Jams Remmers supported the motion pro- viding the Non-Trading Committee would accept it as it now stood on the paper. He believed that quite two. thirds of the ratepayers were in favour of this investi- gation, and in his opinion the Council would be put to a continual annoyance until the matter was settled. If they had a clean slate—(laughter)—wity shield they fear any trading committee or anyone else ? They all had a clean slate, and they were all clean until they were proved to be otherwise. (Laughter.) How- ever, he was going to vote for the motion, and he trusted it would be carried "to put an end to this kind ,of business." _ . . _ _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor FORSHAW said he intended to support the motion, especially if it would put an end to this agita- tion. He was satisfied that the members of the Council would come out of the inquiry " right side up." He chould therefore support the motion. Councillor TAMES EVANS, in reply, said it appeared some members were going to vote against the /violation simply because t'sev had made up their minds that the Non-Trading Committee would not accede to it. The resolution had, however, been framed hone3tly, as Councillor Tinnion said, to put an end to the agitation, and they had partly been led as to the formation of the resolution from what the Nov-Trading Committee had printed and circulated in the Owe. There Was a cir- cular going round the town containing these words " We have pointed out qumereus instances in which aldermen and councillors had traded improperly with the Cerpotatioa both directly and indirectly as officers and members of limited companies." Now, having circulated that, the reselut ion was broad enough for them without personalities to prefer charges— Councillor F R Ronsurs : It is not. Councillor JAMES EVANS said that was a matter of opinion. Be contended that if there was flo method by</p>
      </div>
