        <p>which the conduct of members of that Council could be scrutinizad, then the ratepayers of the town had no tribunal to which they could appeal. . . _</p>
        <p>, The Driverv-Ilisson : 'They have a tribunal every let of November. Colin&amp;liar TIMNIIN : Not for aldermen.</p>
        <p>Councillor Jamas Evase : It is not a question of the let November. It is a question of principle underlying the whole thing. If the conduct of members was such that ratepayers of the boroueh demanded an examina- tion and scrutiny, was he to be told there was no tri- bunal to which they could anneal?</p>
        <p>-- - Councillor F R ROBERTS : Can I explain (" No, no.") This is suck a Billy mieundeistancling. Councillor JAMES EVANS : It may be to you.</p>
        <p>(Cries of " Vote, vote.") Councillor Evans No,no ;I am not going to be ruled out of order and put down. I like a little bit Of fair play,and have given deference many times to both Councillcr Robertses, who are now shouting 6' Vote." What I have to say is this. When ratepayers de- rand Couneittor FR ROBERTS : They don't demand. _</p>
        <p>-Councillor JAMES Eva : That is a question. I see they have demanded by resolution at a public meeting. Alderman SUTTON : Let them tarn out the members the next time they have an opportunity. Councillor JAMES EVANS : What members?</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTY)N : Members of the Corporatien. Councillor JAMES EVANS : I say that when a rate- payers' meeting is called in this town, and there has been more than one. Councillor Wm ROBERTS : Packed. Councillor JAMES EVANS : That is another question. I shall not finish until I am protected from that sort of thing. The MAYOR: I hope you will allow Councillor Evans to speak without interruption. Councillor Jamas Eveus said there had been more than one public meeting, and whether they were packed meet- ings or otherwise, at those meetings resolutions had been passed. He was present when one was passed unani- mously, demanding an inquiry, and he held that the consent of the Council should be given to an inquiry. MI the resolution said was that consent should be given to the people who passed that resolution to formulate charges and an inquiry be entered into and BO end the business. Be took it that the ratepayers were within their rights. A vote Was then taken, and was as follows For (8) : :—Councillors Smethurst, James Evans, Malkin, Wilkinson, Piumpton, Tinnion, John Roberts, and Forshaw. Against (16) :—The Deputy-Mayor, Aldermen Fair- otnugh, Monk, and Elutehinsen ; Councillors Shilleock, A J Jolley, Greening, Bird, John Evans, Burton, F 11 Roberts, Hulse, W Roberts, A. Jolley, Ellison and Rogers. The MAYOR declared the motion lost.</p>
        <p>- COUnCillOr TINNION : I hope that the names will be recorded on the minutes. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS I hope so. Other Council business will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>Three Fires in Warrington. At about 8.45 on Tuesday evening a fire broke out at the Mersey Cycle Company's Works, in Bridge-street. Fortunately it was at once discovered, and the limes were put Out without difficulty, littls damage being done, A fire which did con- siderable damage broke out at snout the same time on the premises of Mr a Spann. coach bander, Friars-green, A shed which was used as a store- room was burnt down, damage to the amount of about £lOO being done. The loss is partly covered by insurance. In the meantime the adjoining premises of Mr John Massey, tripe dresser, were discovered to be ablaze, and £2O damage had been suffered before the flames were put out. The Fire Brigade with the engine " Major" turned out and the fire was extinguished in about an hour. The third fire occurred on Wed- nesday morning at six o'clock at the premises of Mr Booth, butcher, 17, Pinner's-brow. By some means at present unexplained, the sun blind outside the shop became ignited. Someone rang the fire bell, and the brigade went to the shop, but the flames had been extinguished before their arrival,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>DEAFNESS AND NASAL CATARRH</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS AND NASAL CATARRH</p>
        <p>An Editorial in the last" -Supplement to the Journal for the Deaf" emphaaises very strongly the necessity for treatreg Catarrh of the' Ness and Throat when this condition exists in cennection with defective hearing. Special reference is made to a case treated by the Drouet Institute. in which restoration of hearing resulted from S combined treatment, directed to the Nasal Catarrh as well as to the ear trouble, although the Deafness was of a most obstinate character. Ths patient, Mr Thomas Gibbs, states that he bad euffered from Deafness and ' from Nose and Throat trouble as long as he can remem- ber. He was so deaf he could not hear a watch tick, unless pressed against the head. His nose wss so stopped up he often bad to breathe through the mouth ; it was Continually discharging, and the throat was often filled ith thick yellow mucus. The Drouet Treatment for Deafness and Catarrh was entirely successful, as &amp;s- -cribed by the patient ! Norton Subcouree, near London, Norfolk, January 28th, 1902. Dear Sir;—My nose is in good condition now, and the discharge from my ear has stopped comp!etely, and I feel a different person now altogethr. I shall never shrink from letting anyone know of this wonderful cure; for if anyone is suffering more from nose disease than I did, then God help them. I am completely cured. and have had no relapse. Sine-rely wishing success to the Inst.tute.--Yours faithful!y, T GIBBS. Those who are dssiroua of reading the full account of this ease, as well as the details of 25 other cases of Deaf- ness and Ear, Nose, ard Throat cmaolamts cured by the Drouet Institute daring January should obtain a copy of the " Supplement to the Journal for the Deaf." This publication will be sent by post on application to the Secretary,Drouct Institute, Regent's Park-road, London, N. W. Each journal is accompanied by a Patient's Report Forum, which enables applicants ta receive full particu- lars for applying the Drouet treatment at home. Any advice by correspondence is absolutely free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>REMARKABLE CURE OF</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE CURE OF</p>
        <p>RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>Outward Applications Did no Good.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills Proved Speedily</p>
        <p>Effective.</p>
        <p>While he is not himself a gamekeeper, Mr George Birkett, of South Frodingham, Withernsea, East York- shire, has spent many years in forestirig and gardening, and is a recognised authority on country matters. He imparted some interesting information to an " Eastern Morning News" reporter during an interesting chat. His home is in the heart of the shooting country. Mr Birkett is a hearty-looking young married man of thirty-one years and one of his experiences which he mentioned to the reporter is quite interesting. He said. "Before I was married I was working on an estate„in Lincolnshire, and was gathering walnuts for the hall,wben I got wet,and shortly afterwards was laid up with influenza and rheumatism.l had the doctor,and</p>
        <p>(FROM A PHOTO.)</p>
        <p>he gave me some medicine for my back,which was the seat of the pain. I seemed to get better at that time, but what next happened was about five or six years ago at Winestead, when the old pains in my back returned. They were sharp shooting pains, not only this time in the back, but all over my body. I felt very ill and out of sorts, and if it had kept on I should have been obliged to give up my work, as at times I could hardly stand. But Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, in a rather interesting way, saved me both from pain and anxiety. I had tried rubbing the affected parts with oil and outward applications, but that did not seem to do any good, and I noticed that the continuance of the painful complaint was weakening me probably through the worry. I went on in this way, ap- parently getting worse and worse all the time, until I happily tried the remedy I have named,and that proved speedily effectual. It happened in this way. My wife said that I ought to try some of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. She had heard of them doing good to other people, and that is how it happened that I procured them."</p>
        <p>"How soon did you feel an improvement after you had taken the pills F"</p>
        <p>"After taking about a dozen pills, the pains in back and legs got gradually less, and after I had finished one box I felt more fit for work, and began to feel mytelf fresher. I got another box,and when that was finished I was able to set about my work, and since then I have not taken any medicine of any kind. Besides removing the pain they improved my health generally. I got a better appetite, and I seem stronger and better than 1 have been for a long time."</p>
        <p>Dr Williams' Pink Pills are praised by all classes of people, from the lowest to the highest in the land, for the way„in which they have cured paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anemia, pale and sallow com- plexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache anti neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These pills are a tonic, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name,Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Via- duct, London, E.C., at 2s 9d a box, or six boxes for 131 9d.</p>
