      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>... political practice there is usually a great gulf</head>
        <p>... political practice there is usually a great gulf</p>
        <p>THE termination of Sugar Bounties and Conference of the St 4, the Exchequer. Bounties question, whit has been sitting in Brussels intermittently ft some months past, will raise in many minds tl questions: What does:the Conference mean What have the British delegates been</p>
        <p>telegrams, and despatches of greater length reliability, the real meaning of the sugar bounl is at last coming:home to the man in the st It used to be supposed that this was some nel ious measure levelled against perfidious Albion her foreign rivals ; but we have begun to rel lately that the Continental bounties are, in f 2 subsidies paid to Continental manufacturers their obedient native customers for the purpose artificially cheapening the food supply of English working-classes. No doubt what English working-classes need is not artil but natural assistance, by the reform of ti native conditions, which prevent the real</p>
        <p>of native abilities aid resources. An acadel objection can, therefore, easily be sus( against the Continental sugar bounties. between academic theory, and ortin</p>
        <p>How doesit come about, then, that the MARUI of SALISBURY has sent two or three delegates Brussels to use the full influence cif the Brit Government for the purpose of raising the price our cheap and freeaugar imports? It is aqt tion we should like to see put painly in the Lica of Commons. Alas! it is not likely that should get a plain answer. In some way, wl may be guessed at but cannot be more accurat known at present, the landlords and capitalists the West Indies have managed to get the ear the British public. Economists say that to al the Continental bounties would not materiallf heipthem, that behind the acknowledged bouni there stand powerful organisations of capit in the leading bounty-giving nations of the CO nent —the notorious cartels, for instance ; that unless the cartels be abolished the abolil of the direct bounties will be of very little But these retrograde planters seemed have pinned their faith to a political not as antiquated and unscientific as t'l4 methods of cultivation, i.e., that of count veiling duties; and the only way to countervailing duties appeared to be to agitate the abolition of the bounties, using the threat countervailing duties as a lever. Probably planters would be considerably disappointed find that their lever (or let us say, rather, threat made on their behalf) had proved suc ful. At any rate, it seems likely that a gew reduction and even an abolition of the bounf will be brought about, with the result that</p>
        <p>which constitute by far tl/ t greatestinen t a l part of four supplies at present, will to old conditions ; and, in ordcr to cultivate sol economical ideas among the European peol: and to give some stimulus to the plantera Jamaica and Barbados, the price of this neces: and valuable commodity to the British cot will be raised by an amount which has been mated appmrimately at five millions sterling annum. Who can doubt, if this should come v pass, that our Guvernment is effectively senting to the world that kind of practical pbil thropy for which England is.traditionaily famoto There is matter for tt/ought in this curl( little episode, although the daily press grams and despatches aforesaid have not received any notice from our jaded legish. We:are rapidly approaching Budget-day. No knows what the CHANCELLOR of the Exclia intends to do with sugar; but there is a active fear agitating those members of the</p>
        <p>mercial community who are directly affected he will double the tax of a half pe.iny per pow'</p>
        <p>the for doing claili aad bi atiee ;treat 9far I facf bY , of the the ;hoe° us 3 00i° Itai todvilea oti: fisocl° to itisb of !tier ,uswe Thiel/ ,tolY of ~. of .bolieh ty 4-ieS taliso aoci itio° use' )tio° toir ter ceo for of the 1 to teCagr, lere` ;tise Coe' ha rOVer! 1013° ,pIOP Of ;Sari )nsul:O° esti' rePO' • ? joie tole' tatorg' ooe vol.; coOr the;</p>
