      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 7TH 1902. a</p>
        <p>there? What are its results ? Thanks to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>which he imposed last spring. Two very impres- sive protests have been made during the last few days from bodies of manufacturers and traders in confectionery, jams, and other kindred goods—the first in the form of a Parliamentary Conference in which Mr ROWNTREE, Mr MACHIN, Mr BLACK- WELL, Mr PINK, and represptatives of other well- known firms spoke of the serious effects of the existing tax, and the second a letter to the Press in the same sense signed by a number of leading firms and by Mr R T BOYD, as Secretary of the Manufacturing Confectioners' Alliance. There seems to be no room for doubt that trade and employment have already been gravely effected ; but supposing that the Chancellor should decide to increase this burden, which is practically equal to 50 per cent, of the prime cost of the material, and that this additional tax should coincide with the rise of price to a natural level which must inevitably follow the abolition of the bounties, it requires no specially powerful mind to see that the effects of such a coincidence would be disastrous and widespread. Sir MICHAEL HICKS- BEACH has—to put it mildly—a very positive position. He has not shown the least sympathy with the many representations which have been respectfully placed kfore him. Why he should have been so ready to run away from the coal- owners and should yet be so unbending in regard to sugar and all that depends on it, we do not know. But so it is. Yet the CHANCELLOR, even when he has behind him an obedient major- ity such as the present Government yields, should be very careful not to tamper with the fundamental conditions on which E o'glish trade has been built. The ruin of the sugar-using industries would not in any way benefit the EXCHEQUER; while any rise in the price of sugar must be a serious blow to the working efficiency of the masses of the peopie,who depend absolutely upon cheap commodities for nutriment. LA us hope that second thoughts will give Sir MICHAEL pause, and that the gloomy anticipations which are just now being entertained in some quarters may be happily falsified.</p>
        <p>The cheering news re- ceived from South Africa will renew hope in many hearts that the end of the war is ap- proaching; and whatever other result it may have it will encourage patience on the part of those who are disposed to criticise the military conduct of the campaign. The difficulty since the Press censorship has been established with so much rigour has been to obtain data for an accurate judgment of the progress of the conflict. The censorship may have been necessary,and there has been no serious impeachment of Lord MILNER in that respect; but its immediate effect has been prejudicial to the good reputation of our fighting forces, for it has given an exaggerated importance to our victories, and in exercising all details of the dangers of the situation it has not given them full credit, and has led the public to expect more from them than could be reasonably demanded. A false impression has been created,and the belief has been general that their task was easy, when in truth it was arduous. The error of underestimating the strength of the enemy has indeed gone through the whole campaign, and it has given jealous Con- tinental nations the opportunity for sneers which in reality had no jostification. The best friends of officers and men are: those people who have always regarded this matter seriously and not with a light heart, When history comes to be written special credit will also be awarded to those mem- bers of Parliament who have undertaken the unpleasant duty of watching ,the War Office and demanding prompt correction of its errors. Very few of them have escaped the calumny that they were unpatriotic, and some of them have been called pro-Boors as a return for their solicitude for our military honour and good name. Everyone remembers how bitterly Mr. BURDETT-COUTTS was rebuked by Mr. BALrourt when he first called attention to the condition of the military hospitals, and what a howl went up when Miss HOBHOUSE reported adversely on the concentration camps. since then Sir JOHN Brangnita.L-MArtu has incurred resentment for exposing the blunders of the remount services and the Opposition has been taunted with petty motives of party gain for throwing lights on other contracts. The Press censorship in South Africa and the demand by the War Office for silence as to ad- ministration at home has tended to lessen our appreciation of the difficulties under which our aoldiers have toiled, and has caused us to under- value their services. At a time when Lord KITCHENER has scored a signal success, and there are indications that the principal commandoes may before long ha broken up, it is well to take a calm view of the prospect. An interesting opinion as to the future has been given by Captain DIES- PECKER, a Cape loyalist, who for a long time had command for the British military authorities at Willowmore. This officer does not attempt to 'disguise the disturbed state of Cape Colony, par- ticularly in the western portion, or that a large amount of military work will have to be done after BOTH, DE WET, and others have been:captured or surrendered. There seems, indeed, very little likelihood of speedy restoration of constitutional Government in Cape Colony. But these facts should be widely known in order that people may be prepared for the slow solution of the problem which the war has forced upon us.</p>
        <p>Good Progress In South Africa.</p>
        <p>vas. T.) give him his due, Mr Brodrick's annual speeches on the A.,.nly Estimates show him to be an excellent War Minister—for times of peace. His theories of army reform are excellent, and he pro- pounded a scheme of recruiting which should stave off for a long while the danger of conscription. He proposes to meet Mr Atkins both as re,ards pay and period of service, arranging the latter also with an eye to a great improvement in efficiency. The ordinary pay is in future to be an actual shil- ling a day without the deductions which have beers so irritating. All recruits (with the iexceenpltiot for norf those for the Household Cavalry) v three years, with nine years in the reserve. At the end of the second year the soldier will, subject to two provisions, make his election whether his service with the colours shall finish with the three years, to be followed by nine years in the reserve, or whether be will make up his period of active service to eight years, leaving four in the reserve. In the latter event he will get is Gd a day if a good shot, or is 4d a day until he reaches the desired shoot- ing standard. In that way Mr Brodrick makes enlistment attractive, because 103 6d a week in addition to Board and maintenanc3 will compare favourably with ordinary labourer's remuneration, and the option of short service at a slightly heightened pay will appeal to those who desire only a short career in the army. There will, how- ever, be a demand that the increase of guerdon shall apply at once, and not two years hence, for the soldiers who have been enduring so much in South Africa deserve well of the country. There should not be a distinction made to their dis- favour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>Whether it be, as some cynics say, owing to Uncle Sam's preoccupations in the Philippines, or in setting up the new Government in Cuba, or in settling constitutional rights or wrongs in Porto Rico, or whether from sheer inexcusable neglect, it is certain from many reports that the great statue of Liberty enlightening the world, of which New York used to be so proud, is getting into a very dilapidated condition. This work, in itself more remarkable than ever the Colossus at Rhodes can have been, is of gigantic size. Even from vessels entering the harbour it is impossible to get a just idea of its proportions, which are so great that a marriage ceremony, it is said, has actually been performed in the thumb of the figure, the ball of which contains a hollow hamber large enough to contain bride and bridegroom, clergyman, and the necessary witnesses. Such a structure, it might have been thought, would have received all the attention necessary ; but this has not been the case, and the great statue is rapidly becoming an eyesore and a danger instead of the thing of beauty it was intended to be. The neglect, indeed, would almost seem purposeful, from an order which has been issued that the light once streaming from the figure is to be quenched. It is unnecessary, it appears, to navigation, and an utilitarian ago fails to see why for any other reaspn the illumina- tion should continue. The Administration, says a local democratic journal, has come to the con- clusion that Liberty need no longer enlighten the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Provincial enthusiasm for our Colonies is pro- bably such that it will survive the victory of the latter at cricket over an English team. But it is distinctly disappointing to those among us who think that the victories over StoMart's team four years ago should have contented the Australians for some time, to learn that on Tuesday last ithiclaren's men lost the last of the five test matches, so that the Australians have won four out of five exactly as they did when Stoddart was captain of the visit- ing English. Provincial admirers of the game are not slow to affirm that the English ought not to have lost this match. With tiles " ifs " allowed, tha English representatives would have won ; that is to say that this would have been the result if rain had not spoiled the wicket, on the last day, if Mac!aren had not been run out when well set, and if Barnes had been able to play. It was hard luck on the Englishmen, but it is the element of luck that gives cricket the "glorious uncertainty " which everybody appreciates in turn. The cricket world will not have long to wait for a chance of "revenge," that will be friendly, of course. For it is not much more than two months now to the date fixed for the first test match in England, and then if the Colonials win again all one can say is that they will deserve to.</p>
        <p>Those who road the War Secretary's speech de- , livered in the House of Commons on Tuesday, on introducing the Army Estimates, will no doubt have ' thought that many of the changes announced are excellent, as no noubt they are—in theory. The country is to have a Militia reserve, a Yeomanry reserve, and a Volunteer reserve, and if these can be secured without weakening the forces of which they are to be the reserve, we must say so far so good. But the experience of all recent attempts at army reform is that:they are admirable on paper,but that they" won't march " ; hence it may be said once again that it is as well to withhold praise until it can be seen whether the promised reforms are ac- tually obtained. Mr Brodrick again defended the Army Corps system, and proposes that the terms of service shall actually be shorter, a man being allowed to enter the army for three years only,with nine years in the reserve, and have at least a shilling a day. Under certain conditions, a soldier would receive eighteenpence a day ; and he propose' further to form the resertes, as alreaiy stated, for Militia, Yeomanry, and Volunteers.</p>
        <p>Combating consumption is a laudable work for individuals or for public bodies to be engaged in, and it is gratifying to observe the number of Boards of Guardians now taking an intelligent and practical interest in the subject. May their num- bers continue to increase 1 For consumption is pre- ventable, and the deaths that result from it, which hitherto have been legion, represent a loss to the community of a large sum in money every year, even if we look at Lhe matter only in this light. It is a light which has only recently begun to dawn upon the members of some of these boards, but, urged and encouraged by the Press and the medicos, there seems now to be a disposition abroad to make up for past neglect and apathy. Birmingham is among the last, or rather the latest,to make a move and is at present " considering " tee advisableness of adopting a system of notification. Liverpool and Manchester, as we know, adopted the notifica- tion Act some years ago, and why Birmingham, with its 800 deaths per annum from consumption, should have ignored this simple means of gaining knowledge, it has been for the local Board of Guar- dians to inquire. The Board is doing this, but hitherto has not made any great progress with the Health Authority. It is with the City Council there as with various sanitary authorities else- where ; no thought is given to the cost of bad health and a high rate of mortality, until the truth is pressed home in some extraordinary or disagree- able way. Yet common sense ought to show that this is the point of view from which any question of combating disease should be approached by these authorities. It is, at least, somening new to find boards of guardians pointing the way for city councils and other municipal bodies, and may serve to indicate a healthy and welcome " sign of the times."</p>
        <p>COEISCiOII9 of the higher plane which they have in recent years attained, one is reminded that Boards of Guardians appear to have at the same time acquired what the Scotch minister is said to have prayed for, "a guid conceit o' airsels." At the recent meeting of the Council of Poor Law Unions, a suggestion was submitted that the Council should make representations in the proper quarter that the forthcoming Coronation will be a fitting time for bestowing some msrk of distinction on Chairmen of " the leading Boards," and the President of the Association. The matter was diseased with all seriousness; several members expressing the view that honours asked for were no honours at all, while others, like" Oliver" of immortal memory as one who believed in" asking," were not so squeam- ish. The Mayor of Cardiff went the length of sug- gesting that the chairmen of such boards might be made ex-officio magistrates, after the manner of chairmen of urban district councils, and this sug- gestion was adopted, the President himself being directed to bring the hint before tbe Local Govern- ment Board with a view to its being submitted to the King Growing bolder, it was then agreed to take the opinion of the constituent unions as to a request by the Association to be represented at the Coronation in June next, and, having regard to the increasing importance of boards of guardians, both in their own estimation and that of the public, it is trusted that the response of the " constituent unions " may prove satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Lord Lamington's question in the House of Lords on Monday,as to the attitude which the Post-Office would assume in regard to Wireless Telegraphy, Jed to a brief but interesting statement and discussion. To understand hew the matter previously stood, it may be mentioned that M Marconi in a recent ad- dress made it clear that be felt the Post Office had assumed andpposoiltiicoyn hoEtile to himself and his inven- tion. In this country the Post-Office holds the monopoly of telegraphic inter-communication its own interests. isi of course to protect The monopoly is en- forced .within the three-mile limit, and M. Maroon has explained that on no occasion on which he had attempted to conduct experiments within that limit had the authorities failed to send him a warning. Thus it follows that if this official attitude be maintained, as it appears from what w will, the receiving Marconi stations a s saidn otorlitiroanndsTiantio messages must, on the British side, be posted just three miles from the coast. In a possibly proper spirit of precaution, the Post-Office last year declined to grant a licence to M. Marconi to transmit mes- sages over territorial waters, and in the meantime have been making extensive and exhaustive experi- ments on their own account. There is a desire in doing this, according to the emphatic assurance given by Lord Londonderry, that the public shall obtain the utmost possible advantage from the extraordinary discovery. To the present time, how- .ever, the attempts which have been made to pw;</p>
        <p>wireless telegraphy to practical use, as, for instance, in the naval manes uvres, have not bad any actual results of specific value, although they have estab- lished the triumph of the wireless system over all initial difficulties. But the public feeling is that so long as existing embargoes are enforced, it is impossible to say of how much or how little value the Marconi apparatus would be in the transmission of inland messages. All that is known at present is that the feat of sending clearly legible signals across a part of the country wherein there are towns and natural obstacles nes been achieved ; but, in order to be convincing, these demonstrations will have to be conducted. on a much larger scale, and in this country, at any rate, they cannot be 30 conducted except with the co-operation of the Post Office authorities. People generally have not for- gotten the attitude of Parliament to the earlier developments of electric lighting in this country, the result of which was that, while the new light made rapid progress abroad, Great Britain lagged sadly behind, and the prospects of the industry were injured and hampered to a very serioas extent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
