      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>filE EXAMINER Fltl A MARCIE 7, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ST. DAVID'S DAY. ---- Eisteddfod at Earlestown.</head>
        <p>ST. DAVID'S DAY. ---- Eisteddfod at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>Following their usual custom, the Welsh connexions of E irlestown, celetr ated the festival of their patron Saint by an Eisteddfod, which thee held in the Town Hall en Saturday. Extrs.ordinary efforts had been made on this occasion to ensure success, and their eff :rts met with due „reward. The afternoon was devoted to the various contests in singing, reciting &amp;c., and the judg- ing of the various events, and the proerammes were of an attractive aed interesting character. Alto.ether thero were 150 entries. The fallowing were the officials and judges :—Presidents, Messrs T Ormand, Earles- town, and W Venmore, Liverpool ; Conduntor Rev Wynn Davies. Liverpool ; musical adjudicator, Mr Wilfred Jones, R A M Wrexham accom- panist, Miss Profit ; .:djuclicat irs ot iiteeature, art, &amp;c., the Rev R I Jones' 8.A., Tyldesley ; RwJO Williams (Pedrog) Liverpool ; Rev Wynn Davies, Lmer- pool, Mrs Collingwood, Mrs W Latham, Mrs George, Miss Evans, Mrs ReChandley, Mr R King—The plat- form in the afternoon was occupied by Mr T Ora-land (chairman), Rev Wynn Davies (conductor), Rev R Jones, Mr Wilfred Jones, and Mr J /Iceland (secretary). —Two competitors were selected for the openinz item, a solo competition fr children under 14 years of age, any Welsh air, viz., Pollie Morrie, 12, Sr John-s.reet, Eirlestown, and Eva Jones, Wargrave-road. The children were prettily attired in Welsh costumes, and the pieces they chose for the competition were rather Idifficult for vocalists so young. Tee first prize was awarded to Pottle Morris, and the second to Eva Jones. 11—With repard , to the latter the Judge said she had a , very agreeable q rality of tone, her enunciation was clear, and there was a fair attempt at expression, but unfortunately she was a little out of tune almoit troth the begin niog to the end. With regard to the former her veice was not 60 good, her enunciation not so clear, but she was in perfect tune. They asked for good intonation, without that unless there was something extraordinary the person would be out of the competi- tion. He therefore awarded the prize to Yoffie Morris for good intonation. . _</p>
        <p>Ormaad, the prraident, who had a cordial recep- tion, said he did not know that he ceuld by claim to being a Welshman, either directly or indirectly, but he could say that whether Welsh or English, no one took a greater or more intense delight in such gatherings as those than himself. (Applause.) If his knowie lge of history did net fail, it was about the year 1176 when the first eisteddfod was 1171 d, and it took place at C iron Ran. That was over 700 yeas ago. Of course in those days the means of advertising were not so prolific, and the consequence was this eisteddfod wig made known twelve months in advance. It was gratifying to know that the honoura of the day were equally divided be- tween North and South. The South Wales men were awarded the chair for music, and the North Wales men the chair for poetry. He believed he was correct in saying that poetry and music combined was a natural passion with the Weigh. They could not wonder at that when they looked at the delightful scenery in North and South Wales—the mountain., valleys, aid rivers seem, d to trend that way and become part of the very being ot Welsh people, so that they were permeated with it,and in their compositions practically spoke the music ard poetry of those mountains and rivers. It was also a literary meeting they had been holding for over 700 years and holding it on the day of days,namely,the day of St. David, who was the patron saint of Wales. (Applause.) Teem was a great deal more in the Welsh literature than the average English reader dreamed of, and and they ought not to forget they, English people, the debt of gratitude they all owed to George Herbert, William Morris and hosts of others he could mention. Among other Welsh celebrities he men- tioned, he said we were indettsd to John Gibbons for sculpture, and Burne-Janes for painting. As colonists the Welshmen had no superior. All honour was due to such men, and we would be lacking in our duty as Englishmen if we did not acknowledee it. One import- as-it faotor of meetings of that kind was the education of the people. They hid only to look at the list of sub- jects in the ajmbable pro ;rename, and they found cern- petitione in literature, domestic economy, and many other subjects besides music. He was much struck with the item,an essay entitled "The importance of labouring for knowledge while young." (Applause.) The next item was a duet (tenor and bass) " Call to Aries." Two were selected and the priz s was awarded ' to Messrs Weedall and Abraham Jones of St Helens, the adjudicator stating that he gave them the prize for the quality of voice,richness of tone as well as good reading. , With regard to the other competiters he s sid that their singing blended fairly well, and sounded well, but the bass was somewhat throaty and unsympathetic and wanting in tone. In spite of th.,t, however, there was a fair rendering. The winners were hater, especially the bass, which was of good quality and tone. Both Fang well together. In the laaies' choir competition only the Earlestown Welsh Ladies' Choir put in an appearance. Toe piece selected was "The gentle bird." The adjudicator said the sing- ing of the choir was certainly good, richlin quality and power. In toe reciting competition for children under 16 years of age "Two bumble bees," the first prize was awarded to Ellis Hill, Davies-street, Widnes, and the second to Eleanor Williams, Tuomas-street, Earlestown. The prize for the chief essay was divided between John Harrison and John Holland of Earlestown. FA. the tenor sole- competition "Fairest maiden" the pries was awarded to Mr F Edwards, Ashton. Eleven posts soot in their eroductions for poetry, and the adjudicator awarded the prize to R B Williams of Bettwaycoed, but the prize was withheld as the competitor did net reside in the di4trict. The p-ize for the Welsh recitation " Y Fioadur " was awarded to John Williams, Earlesrown. The children's choir competition was won by "The little Britons." The mixed choir competition was carried off by the Cor Carmel (Ashton) conductor Mr John Davies. At the evening meeting Mr Profit, in the absence of Mr Venmore, presided. Toe proceedings opened by a piece by the Carmel choir and an address by the Presi- dent. The chief piece of the evening was the Male Voice Choir competition " The Crusaders," in which the following choirs took part :—St Helens Prize Choir, Wigan Harmonic, Garswood Hall Institute, Warrington Male Voice Warrington Apollo, Nutgrove, and Sutton United. ihe prize was £lO and a medal for the con- , ductor. Toe adjudicator placed them in the following order :—St Helens (conductor, Mr Berry) the winner, Wigan second, and Garswood Hall third. The followine is a list of the other results :—Smorano solo competition, " She loves and loves for ever," Miss Watkins, Earlestown ; essay, Toe importance of labouring for knowledge when young," William Pearce, Earlestown ; quartette, Moonlight and Music," I' Powell and party, Enrlestown ; pencil drawing of a steamer, Griffith Jones, Ashton ; English recitation, Ellis Hall, Widues ; set of d'oyleys, Mrs J H Hughes, Earlestown ; baritone competition, Mr Geo. Greenough, Warringten ; competition, party of twelve, Earlestown Glee Party ; best get up white shirt, Mrs Parry, Earlestown. Votes of tanks to the officers, and the singing of the Welsh and English National Anthems brought the proceedings to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MR, HERRINGTON'S SURPRISE.</head>
        <p>MR, HERRINGTON'S SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>Certain muscular pains, sciatica, rheumatism, lum- bago, together, with gout and gravel, are all due to the presence of excessive uric acid in the blood. The kidneys should remove all excess of nti3 acid, and if they are well they do so. If the kidneys are weak and inactive they don't, and the result is the different forms of uric acid trouble. Mr William Herrington, of Cross-yard. Yarm, near Stockton-on-Tees, gives interesting testimony in sup- port of this assertion. Writing on March at'a, 1901, he states that he had been suffering from sciatica for a long time. From Christmas, 1899, to July, 1900, he did not do a day's work, for he adds : " The pain was so great that I could scarcely sit, walk, or stand. From July to November he was unable to do much work, but in November was induced to try Dodd's Kidney Pill&amp; He says : " I was surprised at the result. I felt from the first they were doing me good. I only took uTJA be, but I have been a new man, and I leave not lost a day since ." Thousands have been surprised in the same way. We are continually printing testimony similar to Mr Her- rington7g. All uric acid troubles give way before Dodd's Kidney Pills, which never fail to curs the cause, the kidneys. Good „ kidneys moan good health. A good kidney cure means good kidneys. Dodd's K,dney Pills are the best kidney cure, and they have spelled good health for thousands of hopeless sufferers Dodd's Kidney Pills are 23 91 per box, of all Chemist. or post free on receipt of price from The Dodds Medicine Co., 23, Farringdon Avenue, London, B.C. Be sure of the name, D-o-d-1-'</p>
        <p>V.</p>
        <p>lIMIIMMI=IIMEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CYCLISTS TAKE NOTE.</head>
        <p>CYCLISTS TAKE NOTE.</p>
        <p>Mr Li,ghtburn drew the attention c,f the Council to the fact that the sidos of Norman-road wens cow Wanted with shrubs which constituted a footpath, yet cyclists still persisted in r,ding down the path to the danger of pedestrians. He asked the Council to declare it a foothpath, and persons would then be liable to pains and penalties if they rode bicycles along it. The Chairman promised to bring the matter before the notice of the superintendent of police. Mr Imison informed the Council that another por- trait had been received, the sender being Mr William Lyon a late chairman.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.I This vvesk five Runcorn Volunteers have left Runcorn via Chester and Southampton for South African Shores. Thera was no sound of band or rush of crowds to witness the fact. They stole out of the town silently as though none knew or cared that; they were going to uphold the prestige of the British Arms it necesQary with their lives. Some how or other the great British public seems to have got over its nine days enthusiasm for "Tommy " and the War. Perhaps the repeated calls for money have damped the enthusiasm. At all events Run- corn seems now to care very little for its gallant sons.</p>
        <p>Mr Grice after spending fifteen years of the best of his life for the benefit of his townsmen on the Board of Commissioners and the Urban C3uncil has altength had to resign owing:to ill heath.t am sure thatlhe will carry with him into his enforced retire- ment all the goollwishes of his fellow councillors and the community together with the sincere hopes that he will be restored to health at an early date lie has devoted himself ungrudgingly to the services of the ratepayers and it is as little as they can do to thank him for his unswerving devotion to duty.</p>
        <p>In view of the coronation we are to have an officially appointed town crier in uniform to stump the streets with his " Oyez, Oyez." Runcorn is I sure to come to the front now that this appoint- ment has been confirmed. Already the coronation has indirectly conferred on the town an honour which at one time used to be considered an import- ant and dignified one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>In every respect the match lest Saturday on the frwell-lane enclosure was a great one. Owing to the facts that Bradford had lately been displaying fine form and that in toe first encounter Runcorn were the victors an unusual amount of interest centred in the match, And the erpectatione of the spectators were quite realised, for the game was undoubtedly one of the best witnessed tic Runcorn this season. Had a certain person who loves to attack Runcorn in Fe asou an I out of season, been I present he would probably have had to retract his strictures on the machinery of the team. As it was the game was a singularly open one, at all times interesting and occasionally rising into I brilliancy. There was not a dull momsnt from the kick-off to the time the referee's whistle blew, 'finally which says much for a club whose alleged fondness is to play the kick and rush game.</p>
        <p>The whole of the game was very evenly contested and a draw would have been a far better con- clusion to such a well fought game. As it is the game, it has been asserted, was lost owing to bad generalship', more than anything else. There may be a modicum of truth in this and doubtless we might have fared better had Moulton been allowed to take the shies at the sticks but the fact remains that Runcorn lost the match owing to their inability to Ilse to the occa- sion when necessity demanded.‘,Nothiog could have been finer in football history than the move- ments initiated by the home rear guard, yet at the very moment when a cool head and rare judgment were needed they were minus. Two of the Run- corn tries were obtained witn startling rapidity and just at the very moment when the home de- fence seemed to be broken up. The Yorkshire , pushers were coniderably heavier than the "green I uns," and this gave them the advantage. Still Jolley and his partner gained possession over and over again' and with a little luck Runcorn would certainly have been cro wing over a victory instead of moaning over a defeat. Richardson was decid edly off form and. Jones was the pick of the three- quarters. In the forward rank Walker and Farmer were continually in evidence and put in some first class work. Broadley for the visitors proved a stumbling block and materially, helped to win the game for his side.</p>
        <p>The match on Wednesday when the pride of Spindledom visited the Cheshire town was in every respect a splendid exposition of Rugby football. But, to the merest tyro it was apparent that Old- ham are not the team they were two seasons ago. They were outclassed by the superior tactics of the home contingent both fore and alt. And there was but little of that brilliant combination 1 which at one time made them such formidable op- I portents. On the whole their defensive work was good but it was not capable of withstanding the severe onslaught of the Linnets. Wilkinson was in- deed the only weak spot in the team and was de- cidedly off colour. Faulkner, a mere lad, played a splendid game and ought to make his mark. Le played a better dashing game that he did on Satur- day and that is saying a good deal. Houghton, Jolley, Butterworth and Warder also nobly up- held the traditions of the club while Walker and Farmer were to the front continuously. There was a capital understanding between the home players and an utter absence of that injudicious kick and mark and rush game which brought about such dis- astrous results in the early part ot the season.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. BAILLESTOWN BAPTIST CEIMICII.</p>
        <p>The annual tea and entertainment pr4moted by the spinsters connected with above place of wor- ship was held in the schoolroom on Thursday. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by spinsters, and the wants of the visitors were al,o attended to by the ladies. The entertainment was also carried through by the ladies, and the affair throughout was a complete success. RESCUE FROIL DROWNING A' EA.RLEsTOwN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a lad abouii--N;—ft-r; .O .Cage was walking along the narrow projecting footway of the lock gates at the Common Lockz,Earlestown when he slipped and f,ll into the canal. A man named Walter Duncan living in Earle-street, was passing at the time, and he immediately jumped into the water, and effect2d a timely rescue. The lad was in an exhausted condition, but be ultimate- ly recovered and was assisted home._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES,</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A BOTTLE OF WFIISKY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday Edward Thompson, a youth was sent to goal for fourteen days with hard labour for stealing a bottle of whisky, value 3516.1, from the bar of the .c!'tanley Arms oa Wednesday. Prisoner came into the house and asked for a pint of beer, which was given to him free. As soon as the landlord had turned his back prisoner took the whisky from the shelf. He was apprehended by the police siortly after wito the bottle in his possession. CHILD NEGLECT/AT WIDNES. . _</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Petty Sessions, on Tourday, Rose Ana Owens, a young marrie 1 woman living in Caroline-street with her husband,was charged with neglecting her two coildren on divers dates during the last six months.—The megistrates said the case was a shocking one, and the worst they had ever heard. It was scandalous for a young woman to have given way to drink. They were inclined to give her a heavy sentence, but, taking her promise into consideration, she would have to go to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON THE BRITISH EMPIRE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the Ellesmere-street Mutual Improvement Class, Mr W H Leachwood gave an interesting and instructive lecture upon "The British Empire.". The Rev W bray, pa4tor of the Methodist Free ()lurch, occupied the chair, and there was a good attendance of members. The lecturer treated at length the growth and history of the colonial system, from the discovery of America He made a distinction between the colonies proper, and the connecting stations upon the five great commercial highways of the world, saying that whilst the first had been the result of the Anglo-Saxon people, the last were due to the sagacity ot the REglish statesmen. The chief point dwelt upon by Mr Leathwood was that the,British empire proper was an empire of expansion, and not one of conquest. England was pre-eminently a naval and not a military country; and the present spirit of militarism was against the genius of tie people. He declined to say much upon the South African question,preferring to let the lecture speak for itself, especially that part of it referring to the loss of our old colonial empire,America upon which he dwelt at length. A. detailed history of India was given,and proved to be of absorbing interest. The lecture occapisd about an hour and half, and the attention of the audience was maintained through- out. At the clop a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Lsath wood, and the hope was ex- pressed that he would favour the societ4 with another lecture at an early date. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held on Thur.day morning in the Council Chamber. Present: Me3:r3 Rll Posnctt (ia the chair), J D Bisbro F CE , J Wilsoq. J W Lightburn, E Gatloy, S Walton, E Inino, I' Jones, T Dan, W aandley, with Messrs E Marshill (secretary). F A Like (law clerk), J Wilding (aurveyor), and Dr McDougall (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HEALTH CF THE TOWN.</head>
        <p>HEALTH CF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>I : was reported that the &amp;ate; fir the past month ainouated to 21. aril tho births t ) 33. Far the corre9- poading period ivit year the dsaths amouated t) 30, and the births to 57.</p>
        <p>Mr Sweetman seconded. It was not right to pay two surveyors, one ot whom drew a good salary aud did prac ically nothing. . .</p>
        <p>- Mr Lightburn opposed the amendment. They were asking Mr Wilding to do the work and yet refused to pal. him. He felt as keenly as anyone the necessity for kee: big expenses down. But this was a matter which had to be reported upon monthly and if it were not done satisfactorily a Government official would be coming down and doing it for them, in which case £2O would be a small item. On surveyor, it should be remembered, was only a consulting surveyor. Mr Dean pointtd ou: that toe Llcal Goverment Board had teld thorn that they had certain duties to perform which had bither:o been neglected. It was work where friction would have to bl avoided between works and employers of labour, and Mr Wilding was the only suit- ab e man for the post.</p>
        <p>The amendment, on being put, was lost, only the mover and seconder voting for it,</p>
        <p>PRECEPT'FOR £165.</p>
        <p>The Markets line Cemetery Committee recommended that a precept be served on the Overseers of the Poor for £165 for the six mo iths ending 30;i1 September next for toe repayment of loans. Passed on the motion of Messrs Walton and Gatley. . . . . .</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committee recommended ; that application be made to the Delegates of Local Examinations, Oxford, for the appointment of a small local centre at Euncernsand that the required guarantee be gio,n and the Consultative Committee consisting of the Chairman of the Council. Chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee, Mr Imison, and Mr James M Frith, he the Committee to carry out all rueessary arrangements, and that such Commitee have power to actl to its number. Mr Wilson moved the confirmation of the minutes. It was their aim, be said, to cultivate the social, as well as the physical and mental, side of pupils and teachers. They wanted to bring them in touch with one another so that more could be got out of their work. Toe local Oxford cen're could arranged for without any cost to the Council,which would be a saving to those ratepayers who would have to send their children to sit for their exP.minations in other towns,</p>
        <p>Mr El andley slconded. The Carman pointed out that there was now a private room at tae institute for the girls, which would supply a want. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>Too Finance Committee re.s.olved that the offer of Miss Sarah Rwb)ttorn, of Aston, net'. Runcorn, to lend the sum of £223 101 for seven years at interest at 31. per cent per annum, parable half yearly, be acceped. that the offer of the Warrington Corporation to borrow the sum of £l,OOO for ten years at interest at 33, per cent, per a num, payable half-yearly, be accepten. Tric commi,tee reported being in communication with the Local Go,erninent Board rzspecting the proposed forms of the demand and receipt of the general district rate and water rate and now recommended that there be a joint demand note bu: that separate receipts should be given in payment. Passed on the motion of Meagre Imison and Jones. APP ,INTUENT OF OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>On the propostioa at Mr Ligntburn seconied by Mr Bisbrown, sLsira Taylor and Inibion were rc-appointed oversecrF.</p>
        <p>MR. GRICE RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr GriC3 resigning his place on the Coancil for Mersey Ward on the graundsof ill health and asking that the usual fine of £1 be remitted. Oat se prop-lition of the Chairman; seconded by Mr Dean, the u,signation ws accepted. the Clerk being instruved to indite a letter expressive of regret, and trusting that he would be restored to health at an ea:ly date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>IMPROVEMENT OF CANAL-STREET.</head>
        <p>THE IMPROVEMENT OF CANAL-STREET.</p>
        <p>The Highways and Building Committee recommended that application be made to the Local Government Board fmr their sanction to the borrowing of a sum of £750 to be repaid within a period of thirty years for the improvement of Canal-street It was also recommended that a retaining wall, sixty- tour feet long, on the crest side of Heath-road, be built at an estimated cost of £47 2s 6, and that a letter be seat to the Manchester Ship Canal Co., admitting that the wall is built on the land belonging to such company; that the Council se eat r level &amp;o, Carron-street, Haverg,l- - and Curzon Passage and that the expenses be defrayed by application to tho Local Government Board for the required amount, the, expe ases to be repaid by the owners by half-yearly instalments, interest at the rate of four per cent per annum being charged. Mr Handley moved the adoption of the minutes. Re- marking on the appointment of Mr Thomas Saunders as town crier be said that that appointment had descended from father to son for over a century. The other day he went to the Parish Church IYard to look at the stone recording the services of one who held the appointmect a century ago namely Salomon Shopheef. And in passing he thought some- thing might be done to bring about something like order in the church yard for he believed that the Council by the Act of 1396 was practically its custodian. The inscription on the stone read :—"eTo the memory of Solomon Shepherd. Heroes have died in defence of their country 'rile world at various times has lamented the loss of eminent statemen, divines, physicians and other wise men. But the Pariah of Ruacorn has to lament th loss of one who as sexton, hellringer, and grave digger executed his offi 'ea faithfully." That was the test:flatly to a man who died at an age bordering on seventy and who had held the office since he had been a young man. It had since descended from father to son,but the bolder of the office, David Shephir 1, was no longer in the town and Thomas Saunders, who had for some time fulfilled the duties had applied to be officially installed in the position. It would be a good plan if he were to have a coat as a semblance of hi a authority as in larger towns. With reference to the paving .ke, of the streets named it was only fair that the inhabitants should have proper footpaths for their children tea walk on. They were only the proper necessaries of civiliza- tion. The improvement of Canal-street made it very probable that other works would be erected on this land. One half of the cost was to too provided by the o teeters and the oocupiers of property. And the Council was therefore being asked to contribute only one half of the cost to this much needed improvement. They as a Council would derive substantial benefit from the undertaking. Mr Imison seconded.</p>
        <p>The Law 61-e-rk—t-hO.ught that no obligation.; with I reipect to the churchyard devolved on the Council. It lay entirety in the bands of the churchwardens. The Chairman proposed as a rider that the trustees of the late Mr Wright who bad given land to the amount of £35 be added to thoee who bad contributed to the improvement in Canal-street.</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed with this addition. THE ANTI-VACCINATOR. NEW DUTIES FOR MR. WILDING.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommended that the Council "is strongly of opinion that, in the intereara of Public Health, Clause 2 of the Vacci:lation Act, 1898, relating to the ' Conscientious Objoctor ' should be repealed,and thst with a view of securing this desirabl result a copy of this resolution be sent to the Local Govern- ment Board ;" that Mr James Wilding, the Surveyor,be appointed to pPrform the duties under the Factory and Worksnop Act, 1901, at a salary at the rate of £2O a year, and that such salary dare from the 613 March 1902. The committee submitted the annual report of the Medical Officer of Health for the year ended 31st December, 1901.</p>
        <p>Mr Dean, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said that in consequence of the new duties put upon them by the Local Government Board—duties of a delicate nature to be carried out without friction—they had considered Mr Wilding to be the only suitable man to take up the duties. Mr Jones seconded. • Mr Blibrown moved an amendment that the minute relating to the conscientious objector, and the latter portion of the amendment relating to the payment of Mr Wilding for his nyw duties be deleted. In view of the discussion which had taken place in the House of , Lords sums three weeks ago, it seemed to be useless for tho committee to gas such a resolution. The Act of 1899 was only for a term of five years, and it had aow only two years to run. At that time the question would be reconsidered, but nothing would be done by the Government prior to that date. With regard to the £2O salary to be giveo to Mr Wilding he disclaimed any personal motive but said that the old Act was carried out by the nuisance inspector, Mr Percival, and he thought thl.t the duty Should devolve on Mr Percival who if be could not carry out the duties was certainly not fit for his present position. As Mr Wilding had his hands already full, something would have tI be neglected ha order to do the work.</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a tea party and concert was given by the Men's Bible Claris connec'ed with the above place of worship. Upwards of 200 people were present and the excellent tea providea was much enjoyed. The following programme of songs kc., was efficiently rendered and thoroughly appreciated. Violin solo, " The broken melody," Mr G Edmonds; song," A Jovial Mock am 1," Mr ci Dring, quartette, " On the banks of Allan Water," song, " Angel of My Dreams," Mr James Nichols ; song, "0 hear the wild waves blow," Mr T 0 wen ; song, " The Botnany Lass," Mr 0 Kidd ; violin solo, " Warp that once," 51r G S tildnuonds Duet, " Excelsior," Messrs Kidd. and Dring ; quartette, "Comrades in Arms," Messrs Owen, Dring. Sephton and Nicholls; ssing, "Same of the Palm," Mr 'l' Owen; song, " Queen of Angels,' Mr James Nichols ;:song "The Gladiator," Mr S Dring. GOOD TEMPLA.DY.</p>
        <p>! The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the co-operative dall,Wargrave-road,on Monday, Bro Cutler C T presiding. Three candidates were proposed and two initiated. The SecrPtary pre- sented the report of Bro and Sister Hatton's "At Home," the proceed of which had been given to the Lodge funds, and the thanks of the Lodge were accorded to Bro and Sister Hatton. Bro Woods, L D, read the Digest of No 1 Convention meeting at Earlestown at which Bra Walker D T, Liverpool, read a paper on "Juvenile Templary,"also an invit- ation to attend the opening of a new Lodge at Leigh. The programme being " Musical Night," Sisters Mrs Williams and Mrs Woods responsible, the Lodge wasopened to visitors. The programmci was opened by singing ode, " Come friends and Brethren all unite," song, ‘. Beyond," Bro Hatton ; recitation, " The dying child.," Sisrer Miss Ord ; song, " Tee good-bye at the door,"Sister Mrs Owen; reading, Bro Newman; song, " tier bright smile haunts me still," Sitter Mrs Wilson ; recitation, Bro Williamson; song, " The Slave's dream of free- dom," Sister Mrs Smith ; reading, " Never marry a drunkard," Bro Woods. Bra Newman presented greetingsifrom Bro Proctor, Seychelle Lodge 64, Khans', India, and the Lodge was closed in the usual manner by Bro Cutler. C T. PRESENTATION T 1 MRS. A. HOWARD (NEE GREENALL). - .</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a social gstherirg was held in the Lecture Hall of the Wesleyan Cusps!, Earlestown, for the purpose of making a presents- tiou to Mrs A. Howard, who as Miss E L Greenall was organist of the Cnapel for mans years, and wtio only retired at Christmas in consequence of her marriage. About 200 of the congregation were present, and the proceedings were preriided over by the Rev C E Dove, supported by the officers of the Church. The presentation was made by Mr 13 Howarth in an eulogistic speech. and in which he d-alt, • with the rise and progress of the Church during Miss Greenall's , connection with it.—Mrs Howard, who was considerably affected, briefly, acknowledged the gift. The present consisted of a handsome case of cutlery, on •-,he outside of which was the mono- gram " E L. 1.1.." The inscription was " Presented to Miss E Ii Greenall, by the Earlestown Wesleyan Church, Sunday school and teachers on the occa- sion of her marriage as a mark of their apprecia- tion of her services .as organist and Sunday school teacher for 31 years. January Sth, 1902." Addresses were given by Messrs J Roberts, J Good- ing, P Parr, T Clare, W Clare, &amp;a and songs were rendered at intervals by Misses Adams, Holliday, Barr and Mr G Kidd. Refreshments were after- wards partaken of and the evening was spent in games, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOTES BY THE NAY AT</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE NAY AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDIMT] The War fever has not lost any of its virulence in Earlestown, if one may judge of the conduct of its inhabitants. This we a pro-Boer would have had a rough time of it, if he had ventured to parade his opinions in public during the demonstration on Tuesday night. Several young fellows have joined the Imperial Yeomanry, and will sail for South Africa next week. Prior to departure, however, some of them came home on furlough, and two, Davies and Jones returned to Alders'lcr.; on Tuesday night, two oters, Chandley an I J ickAon, return- ing on Wednesday night. All are well known in the town, and on Tuesday night there was a great send-off. The ViaducG Brass Band turned out, and there was a procession round the town, Davies and Jones being carried shoulder high in the centre of a big crowd. The station approaches were besieged, and for safety the police had to guard the entrance. Still the platform was packed and the scene remin- ded one of Mafeking night, but on a smaller scale. Evidently patriotism runs rife in Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer's annual report to the Council can,on the whole,be considered satisfactory,but the doctor draws the serious attention of the Council to the necessity for providing an isolation hospital for the treatment of infectious diseases as early as it is possible to do so. In the year ',here have been 166 cases of scarlet fever reported, and probably the majority of these had to be sent to Warrington for treatment. We have to pay for this accommo- dation, but why we should be put to the trouble of going to Warring on I am at a loes to understand. The site for a hospital has been offered to the Council, end if they would spend a little money in the erection of a hospital, and waste less on bogus water schemes, it would be of more advantage to the town. It is reassuring to find that the Isolation Hospital Sub-committee is not a defunct body, and, that the Medical Officer's report is likely to some- what stimulate that dormouse-like body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Ear:estown club had what might be called an off-day on Siturday. They had a match on with Southport Central, but it was only a friendly en- counter, and with the Central reserve team, conse- quently there was only a very poor "gate." Up to half time the game was very well contested.although the home &amp;even had slightly the advantage, and neither side were able to score. On turning round, however, Harlestown exerted themselves somewhat with the result thqt they altogether overplayed their opponents and ran out.easy winners by four goals to one. The weather was of the right kind from the spectators' point of view, but rather too mild for the players, and as the home lot have several League fixtures to decide at home before the close of the season a continuance of ne present fine weather ought to bring out some good "gates, and thus assist the finances of the club.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown Rovers received their quietus on Saturday from Winnincton Park in the competition for the "Observer" Cup. The Rovers have not played a very strong game this seasol and the defeat they sustained on Saturday was not alto- gether unexpected.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Guardian's team, after their experiences on Saturday will euraly take into serims consideration the necessity for holding an auction sale, for the purpose of disposing as soon as they can, of the paraphernalia belonging to the club. A defeat by 17 goals to nothing could cer- tainly not afford any room for doubt as to whether they or Burtonsvood were tlie better team. The referee must have bad a very easy time, in regard to decisions, but a lively period of bock-keeping.</p>
