      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CONTAGIOUS EPIDEMIC.</head>
        <p>CONTAGIOUS EPIDEMIC.</p>
        <p>Sometimes from sudden relapse, or contagious epidemic, the system becomes enfeebled, and nerves are unstrung. At such a time, a prolonged illness may otten ba saved by an immediate use of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. Its grand tonic effects after influenza or fever are magical, and as a preventive to failing strength, low spirits loss of appetite, and all chest troubles, it is invaluable. A'so as a blood purifier and strengthener. Are you a victim to neuralgic attacks or sick headaches? Tnere is no greater specific than Gwilym Evans' Quinine hitters the best remedy for Weakness, Nervousness, Indigestion, Flatulence. Loss of Appetite, Law Spirits, Sleeplessness, Guest Affections, Influenza, and Impure Blood. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a safe, certain, sure, and rapid Pink-me-up. Don't be without it any longer, and keep it by you for use when you want it. Tiae cnnoonents of this great remedy consist of Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Bur- dock,l Lavender, and Dandelion, with just enough Quinine to complete its marvelle:,s powers. NEED BE NO APPREHENSION.</p>
        <p>There is probably no need for any apprehension as to the state of your health, if you have Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters witbid easy reach. It puts vigour into all the vital powers, gives tone to the stomach, stimu- lates the function of the liver, braces t .e nerves, enables the patient to throw off all despondency and promotes hopeful exertion. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a seven-fold blessing, of Beneficent Nature, compounded with scientific exactness for helping the vital powers of brain, nerves, and muscles, cleansing the fount of life— tor Blood is the Life—and pr.merving the strength to stand the common attacks of Rheumatism, Gout, Lumbago, Sciatica, Neuralgia, and Disorders of 7the stomach Don't break down for the want of trying what Gwilyro Evans' Quinine Bitters will do for you. Tak3 it, and you will feel fresh in the morn- ing for your day's work. Take it, and yeti will feel strong in the evening when you leave off. Be cheerful. enjoy your food and your labour. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is used by the Medical Profession, it is a purely vegetable compound, ibis absolutely free from Mercury, it has been tested for a quarter of a century, and it is the Best Tonic. There are many medicinal tonics offered to the Puhlic, but they may be classified under two heads :—The Best and the Rest. Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Thtters is the Best. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2 4 91, and 4s 6 l each, or will be sent, carriage free, on receipt of stamps, direct from the Sole Proprietors, the Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wale'. When purchasing Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters beware of worthless imitatiele. See the name, "Gwilym Evans" on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle without which none are genuine.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Rev. J. F. Matthews and Un!tarians. The Pastor. the Rev J F Matthews, was the preacher at Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday. In the even- ing he eillivered a special sermon to young rneo, taking as his subjoct "Jesus, the Son of God." He said any attempt to assail the dignity of the Lord Jesus Christ was looked upon as both irreverent and blasphemous. Criticism was by no means irreligious, and he rejoiced greatly when anyone ventured to diezuss the great theme of their religion, even adversely. In concluding an interesting sermon he said he was compelled to believe that the keen intelligence, the gracious humeri sympathies, the generous, high minded, and disinter- ested efforts of their Unitarian friends in their work for God in the service of man were all the result of the operating grace of the Son of God whose deity they intellectually denied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A Society.</p>
        <p>The above named society commenced its tenth year on Sunday under very auspicious circumstances. Sixty-six new members were enrolled, which speaks volumes fo the popularity of the movement, and augurs well for it future prosperity. The President, the RIV John Yonge presided and in his address referred to the inaugurnticm of the society at Wycliffe. He (Mr Yonge) believed that the success which had attended this branch of work At Wycliffe, had inspired other churches in the town to commence similar work amongst working men and raany men who originally were members of the Wycliffe Sonicty, had joined those classes. He did not regret this, but rather rejoiced at the efforts put forth to reach the large number of men who were still loitering about our streets on Sunday afternoons. Mr Watson of Liver- pool was the speaker and delivered a very good address on the "Threefold nature of life."—Ths animal nature, the worldly nature, and spiritual nature.—Mrs R Ii Pearson delighted the audience with an excellent render- ing of the solo " The Worker," ,and Mr R H Pearson contributed a violin solo with nia accustomed ability. Mr W H Payton presiled at the organ. The service was taken part in by Mr B Farrar and Mr J J Evans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Sr. TROMAS'S SCRIPTURE Utitotr.—fhe monthly meet log was held on Sonday, in the Girls' Schoolroom. Mr Carter, who conducted, briefli, referred to the loss the Scripture Union in common with the church and parish had sustaired by the death of Mr Robert Davies, who was a friend and well-wisher of the Union and a regular subqcriber LI its funds. Mr Powell (St Peter's Mission) 1 ollowed with an address on the hist.ory of t. a penny " and its lessors from the young Christian's standpoint.</p>
        <p>An unusual number of su'cides in Austria have been reported during the last two days, says the "Daily Chronicle's" Vienna correspondent. In the south 'I yrol many persons have been attacked by a terrible skin disease, and four of the victims have committed suicide. In Vienna ti-day a well-known officer in the army and his wife, both society people, were found shot dead. They were rich, much devoted to each other, and no reason can be given for the rash deed. Another &amp;Beek shot himself in Bohemia and a girl sought death in the Elbe River. The " Daily Telegraplea " New York corre=pondent says :—According to a despatch front Nashville. Tennessee, the youngest bride in this country is Mrs Jas. Irwin, who is stated to he ten years Old. Her hus- band is said to be between 45 and 50. - • -</p>
        <p>_ cIA vardict of suicide whilst of unsiund mind waS on Tuesday returned at an inquest held at RPedness. near Goole. concerning the death of a girl of 13, named Ethel Lfihton, whose body was recovered from the Ouse on the prevlous day. She had left her situation, and on being persuaded by her father to go back left her home in a temper and went direct to the river and drowned herself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A MAN'S LIKING FOR CHOCOLATE.</p>
        <p>At the Couict37B-o-ro-u-g-h-e-Oric-e-d-ou-r-t On—Ss-it-us:day, Thomas Houghton, 9 ateveroson-street, was charged with stealing from the shop of Messrs W ti and C Lawless, Market-place. a quant ty of chocolates, of the value of is 6d, on Thursday evening.—Mr Browne prosecuted, and said that for some time past quantities of chocolates and sweets had been missed from the shop. On three o2nasions during the week the prisoner had been into the shop and purchased a pertayworth of Barcelona nuts. The man, who lived so far away as B3ttersby-lane, had a brother who had formerly been em- ployed at his client's shop. The nuts which prisoner asked for Were at the back of the shop and on the last occasion he was seen to fill his pockets with chocolates while the girl went to get the nuts. Whoa the girl returned he took the nuts and left the shop. The Ban who had seen prisoner pat the chocolates in his pockets then informed Mr Lawless, who succeeded in bringing prisoner back to the shop. On the way prisoner threw a quantity of the chocolates into the gutter. When searched his pockets were found to be full of chocolates. While Mr Lawless consulted his wife as to what he should do with prisoner, the man knocked dawn Mr Clare, wro was in the shop, pus.ced over a box, and bolted. Me was arrested oa Vriday evening and offered to pay Mr Lawless 3s for the chocolates. The magistrates inticted a fine of 20s including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court. FRlDAY.—Before Mr Thornton (in the chair), Councillors Smethurst and John Roberts,</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court. FRlDAY.—Before Mr Thornton (in the chair), Councillors Smethurst and John Roberts,</p>
        <p>CRUELTY To A HORSE.—Nicholas McCoy, Mount Pleasant, Liverpool. was summoned for cruelty to a horse by working it in an unfit state on the 113th Inst. and John Whittinghans, Whiston, Prescot, was summoned for causing the animal to be worket—Bvidence was given by P S Evans to the effect that the horse which belonged to Messrs fdaginn, had been running the mail coach from Prescot to Warrington. After being released at Warrington on the date In question it strayed to Bridge Foot and was there examined by the Sergeant who found it to be se/ening from six wounds on the tear shoulder.—Mr Browne, on behalf of the owners, said that when the horse was sent out there was one wound on the shoulder, out the saddle was fixed so as not to rills against it. It was thought that after being released the animal muit have (*den down and caused other wounds.— Defendants were each fined 10s.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT.—Msry Harris, 3, Pierpoint-street, was fined 10s for assaulting Mary B.ierley. 1, Pierpoint-street, on February 25th.</p>
        <p>Warrington Occasional Court.</p>
        <p>At this court on Monday morning before Mr Nield, John Smith and Thomas Michael, were charged with damaging. with intent to steal. a quantity of cabbages of the valise of 3d the property of William Pierpoint, Grappenhall. The evi- dence was to the ekct that the men were seen to leave a Reid with the cabbages in their pen usesion. Information was givers to the notice, and defendants admitted the offence They were lined 23s including costs.—Georga Mandalay was charged before Mr F A Frost with being a sleserter frins the 3rd York and Lansaster Regiment. He was remanded to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MOXDAY —Before Messrs F J Norman (in the chair) and It II</p>
        <p>Posnett.</p>
        <p>TRAWSPIR4.—The following transfers of licenses were granted by the bench —That of John Henry Pierce of the Royal Oak. Weston. to Archibald Raining ; that of Elizabeth Waterworth of the Egerton Arms, Bridgewater-street to Samuel Houghton ; that of Samuel Houghten, of the Nelson. street Arms to Thomas Warder ; that of James Ravenscroft, of the Lord Rodney to James Butterworth ; that of Fronds Geakin of the Friendship Tavern to Dominick McCoy.— Temporary authority was also granted to George Ouraon to Bell at a beerhouse called the Royal Hotel in Mersey-street. POOR RATA: OLIIEL —There were about sixty poor rate Cases the majority being settled out el court and in the re- mainder the usual order was mule,</p>
        <p>A RIMY PROOEDITRE TO SELL A Sow.— A young pig- dealer named Isaac Williams, Chalkpits Farm, Machin Green, Relax WRS summoned for making a false declart- tion to the Runcorn Pollee Authorities.—Superintendent Hanlon stated that the defendant's house was within a swine fever Infected area and that he had Geld a sow to a man named Dunbavin at Halton. In order to obtain a removal order the defendant had to ill up a certain form daolaring that his house was not withia ten miles of a fever infected. area. He had done so and for- warded the same to the Runcorn pollee. They hal eqmmunteated with thd Essex pollee and had found that the defendant had stated what he knew MI be untrue.</p>
        <p>The docusnents had been forwarded to the Chief Constable of Cheshire and it was at his instance that proceedings were being taken.—The defendant stated that he had Bold the sow to Dunbavin previous to being served with the notice from the Board of Agricuaure and expected to be free on the 28th January as he was afterwards served on the let January whereas he was served with a second notice unex- pectedly on the lit February.—Defendant was lined 40e and costs. the Chairman remarking that he had committed a very seeious offence and that the bench had taken a very lenient view of the case.</p>
        <p>A TREA:r Ilf STonn Yon litst,—&amp; rough looking couple named Arthur and Maria Evans, rean and wife, were sum- moned for using o'lscene language on the 22ad January.— P.C. Bowyer prove I the c•se end both defendants were Rae&amp; 10 and costa. or In default 7 days —An amusing conyersation than ensued.—Male 4atendant " What le It to be ?" pro- ducing 10s and laying It on the table. "Me pay and you go or you pay and I go."--leemale defendant : "You pay and I will go. - Maria Brans was accordingly conducted to the eellsAbut just before disappearing down below shouted out to her worthy spouse Vraa't I give it to thee when I come back." (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>STRANGE CHAPTER OF ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>A STRANGE CHAPTER OF ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>" Became the nail came out the fastener came off. Because the fastener fell off, the back of the cart came away. Because the back came away the coa s all fell out and while I sought them in the dark, somebody stole the horse." Be 113 a cadet's excuse. It at any rate explained a strange series of us;sfortunes. in matters of every day life, there is generally some great line on which event follows event and leads on to one extreme or the other. So with matters of health and disease. In many households where Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness are not used, indigestion land liver disorder are very prevalent just now. The ' man or woman who can't digest food becomes weak. The blood fails to receive nourishment, and because the blood is starved, aosernia appears. Anaemia means bloodlemaness. Bl,ndlessness means palpitation, "tau," depression and headache. Bloodlessness in turn starves the nerves. The nerves cry out in the form of tic, face- ache and neur .Icia in various parts of the body. The whole circulation becomes and the cutting, biting, east winds of March feel to chill one's very marrow.</p>
        <p>AN the above-named ailments are common at this eoason, and they all arise from one or two root ailments of the liver and digestive system. Where Chas. Forcle's Bile Beans are used, liver and digestive disorders are unknown. Bile Beans are a certain cure for ell such ailments and therefore a rccveatite of tbe others men- tioned above. This is no more theory it bas been proved by thousands. Bile Belo9 may be had from all chemists for one and three-half-pence or two and nine per box. Never sold loose.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. A visit was paid to the above on Monday evening last by the members of the Ellesmere-street Charles Garrett Lodge who provided an excellent pro- gramme. Mr E Horton was the chairman and be gave a short and spirited address. Mr J Ellison was the principal speaker and the impression made upon the minds of those present will not readily be , erased. A duet was sung by Mrs Davenport and Miss Taylor. Master Joseph Whitmore sang " Kil- larney " in a most pleasing ttyle. Miss Minnie Whitmore recitA "An angel in a dramshop "and " A little girl's troubles " to the delight of all pre- sent. The choir rendered several choruses and the male members gave dumbbell exercises which were much appreciated. A dialogue entitled "Tom Wil- drake's folly" And a hearty vote of thanks to all who had so kindly gi 'en their services, brought a plea- sant and enthusiastic meeting to a termination. Making Free with a Platelayer's Cabin. At the Warrington County Petty Session on Wed- nesday before Mr S Ii Armitage and Colonel J C Ridgway, John Stofour was charged with stealing a padlock, the property of the London and North- Western Railway, at Penketh, on Tuesday. Detec- tive Howells said a platelayers' cabin had been locked by John Thompson, foreman at Penketh Hall Crossing on Tuesday, and on going to it next morning he found a fire in it, and prisoner cooking a mutton chop. The padlock was missing, and wheu asked by Thompson what he was doing there, prisoner said a platelayer had let him in. Asked where the padlock was, he said the platelayer had taken it with him. He, however, afterwards ad- mitted having broken the lock, which was found in the cabin. Prisoner was fined 5s and costs, or seven days' hard labour in default. FATAL RESULT OF A FALL.</p>
        <p>On Moncli;;;Ft:ri-O-Ori. Mr ti Brighouse. coroner held an inquest at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on the boiv of Rachel Drummond, widow, 72 yeais of age, of 273 Crow-lane, West Earlestovvn, who died on February 28th as a result of a fall on February 2nd. Evidence was given by Lily Merrick, daugh- er of the deceased, to the effect that on the after- noon of February 2nd, her mother went down the back-yard, and on returning to the house she fell. Witness'e young son ran in and told her that his grandmother was lying on the ground. She atm her husband went out and carried her into the house. She ref used to be attended b a doctor, but on the following .Friday, as she complained of being worse, Dr Valentine was sent for, and he found she had broken a leg, and he set the injured limb. Dr Gauge was also called in, and he put the leg in splints and attended deceased until her death, she being unconscious a greater part of the time. Wit- ness said her mother was subject to fainting fits and had had several nasty falls in consequence. Dr Gauge had written the Coroner that death was due to the fall and injury. A verdict of "Accidental , death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>11114, EXAMINER FRI, JAY, MARCH 7 1902</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>CRICKBTBES will be glad to know th,t GEO. G. Bt's3NE. AND Co.'s Catalogue is out and may be obtained from Sports Dealers. This MAnufacturing Firm is always bent ou improvements Their new Comoound Ball " POLLOID "Is the most perfect resemblance to the leather Match Ball that has been produced, costing under half the price. For Lead- ing Clubs G. G. B. and Co.'s " Duplex Cover" reigns para- mount over all Cricket Balls. Atlrt (2)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRISGTON LOSS AT SALFORD.</p>
        <p>The first of this season's meetings between Warrington and Salford took place at the Willows on Saturday in spendid weather. but on a somewhat heavy turf. Warring- ton were with.)ut Isherwood, the vaciney being filled by Jones while Salford turned out as ii,lected. Tnere were abouCE,OJO spectators present Mr J H Smith (Widnes) was the referee and the teams lined out as follows</p>
        <p>Salford : D smith. full back Bone, WTlliam—s. Davies and Price, threequarters ; Lomas, and Griffiths, half-backs ; Rhadris , Tunney, Buckler, Gledhill, J WiLliams,&amp;law, Ileatla and Brown, forrwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington : Hallam, full back .; Fish, Jones, Dickenson and Harris. threequarters ; Edmonds and Hockenhall, half- backs, Boardman, Morrison. Roberts, Edmondson, Fell, Swift. Ashton and Taylor, forwards,</p>
        <p>J Swift started against the sun, and wind. Rhapps being caught in possession at the quarter line. Salford secured from the scrummage and a pass to Bone and a punt by the latter carried play to the centre where Jones marked. Dickenson intercepted a pass and ran down to Smith, Warrington hav- ing slightly the best of matters, until Lomaa got away cleverly into the other half where his pass went forward. Salford worked their way into Warrington territory and Hallam mulled a fair catch near the line. Lomas got bold tram the nest pack and served Torn Williams who gave Bone a pass which the latter turned to good account, scoring easily at the corner, no goal resulting. Warrington soon dashed dawn the field ..gain, Jones punting and Fish fo.lowiug up at top speed only to fall a victim to numbers near the home line. lidmonde drop?ed at goat and just missed- Still keeping up the pressure W..rrington at last got level. Fish punted and secured agalnstricked Smith.and gave Hoolcenhall an easy try far out. The goal-kiek missed, thoegh only by a tries. Half-time came with the score : 1 try .each.</p>
        <p>Bhapps restarted, and Dickenson hesitating to repiv, way tackled in his own quarter. Lomas received from r,rillths a minute later and ran in easily under the posts. himself iidd- ing a goal. Past play on both sides followed, Salford getting the ball nearly every time but being well watched behind. Bone and Price both :nada good runs but were thrown in touch. Fish was prominent on the left and charged Smith's kick down. With an open goat he dribbled straight for the line, and would have scored when the home full back obstructed him. Thu referee only avvirded a penalty kick and Fish misled an easy place by inches. This was hard look for Warrington, and soon after Price squeezed in at the corner though tackled by Harris. Sal'ord attacked hotly and Bone passed everyone but Hallam who tackled finely. While in the Warrington quarter, Lomas got hcld and repeated his previous performance, the goat kick included. Warrington were not quite done with and Hockenhall. Dickenson and Harris combined so well at the centre that th 3 wing man ran in splendidly. The final score was : Salford. two goals four tries (16 points); Warrington, two trios (6 points.)</p>
        <p>OLDEAV Y. lho The return snatch between these clubs took plaee at Old- ham before about 11,003 spectators. At half-time the scsre was two points eaoh. It was in the seooad half that bwinton showed so much superiority, and Oldham 0.-ed such a feeble resistance to their combined movements that they were almost continually defending their line. Cooper opened the scoring after a fine passing movement between Davies, Morgan and Valentine, but Preston failed at goal. Morgan next scored a try, which Preston improved. and Cooper also scored two more. Then, from a pass by Morgan, Valentine crossed the Oldham line, and the same player shortly after- wards intercepted the ball from Huzzey to Civil and soared another try, which Preston improved. Oldham were com- pletely overplayed till the finish of the game, when the score was :—Swinton, three goals and six tries (24 points); Old- ham, one goal (two points) BATLEY Y. WUNtiILET. At Bat'ey, before 4.000 spectators. Result '—Batley, three goals and three tries (15 points); idunslet, one goal and one try (ave points),</p>
        <p>• . BRIGHoUsE RANGERS V. HULL. At Brighouse. Saville kicked two goals. Hull Up'. up the pressure, (Stem scoring. Hull attacked to the finish, but could not score again. Result —Brighouse Rangers, two goals (four points) ; Hull, one try, (three points). HUDDERSFIELD Y. HALIFAX. . .</p>
        <p>The finish of an even and exeit.ing-1;17;a. s a draw, with nothing scored.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Rii.RIORD v BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>Rii.RIORD v BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Saturday, before 2.000 spectators. Brat Hord started against a nice breezc, and sent Into teach. Off side play by the Bradford seruntmagers allowed Runcorn to reach the centre, where several scrummages eecurred. Jolley at length, near the centre. get hold, and,doiging splendidly, gave to Junes who outdistanced his opponents and scored \t the corner. Moulton failed at gotl. Faulkner now exe- c ted a beautiful dribble, Mosby stopping his eareer. Brad- t., d reaehed the Runcorn quarters, and Broadley passed splendidly to Sinton, who scored a try. Half-time score : One try each. Restarting, the game was even for sorn• time, until the Runcorn forwards by close following up caused Cooper to be cornered near the line. Wilson was all but over when Houghton ttekled him. Then Broadley dashed over from a scrummaze. Mosby failed at goal. Senates but for obstruction hada certain try at his merey.and then Warder by a foolish flying kick again placed his line in danger. Brcadl-y kicked across the field. and Sinton picked up, and, rounding the opposition, fscored behind the pests, Cooper kicking a goal; After splendid passing Warder scored for Runcorn, and the next minute Williams scored Runcorn's third try, Result :—Bradford, one goal and three tries (eleven points) ; Runcorn, three tries (nine points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRPBAT HULL A SHOOND THIN.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRPBAT HULL A SHOOND THIN.</p>
        <p>The twlee-postponed return match between Hull and War- rington was played at Warrington on Monday afternoon before 4,000 spectators. Weather and turf were excellent and a good game was witnessed, both teams being well-repre- sented. Hull, in feet, were quite at full strength, while Warrington played the 811,11211 teem as on Saturday. Mr Oak- land. of Outwood Church, was the referee, and the Hull team was as follows :</p>
        <p>Taylor.fall back Parry, Jacques,Driseoll and Hambrecht, threequartere ; Franke and Thompeon, half backs ; Stitt, Hitson, Spencel,y, Hiller. John Ides, Parkinson, and Harmer, forwards,</p>
        <p>Parkinson started for Hull, Warrington reply- ing well. Fish punted down beautifully and Jones racing up beat Taylor and [would have scored but for the referee erroneously ruling him offside. Null got back to centre and Thompson dodged away, but his pass was badly mulled by the visiting forwards. For a time Hull attacked, but eventually Hallam cleared, and Harris was almost mat the far corer. Warrington Passed very smartly and Fish dodged his way down touch until Taylor passed him *Par the line. The home attack wee very hot and Roberts ran over only to be brought back again. A cross-kick by Fish nearly did the trick, Hambrecht marking in the very nick of time. Before Hull could clear Hocken- hall got hold and with a wonderful dodgy run scored behind the poste, Fish kicking a goal. The visitors new go: going on the right and Parry made a couple of speedy runs which I ended in touch. Rltson ones dropped out and caught War- rington napping but he was caught well In the home quarter and effectually tackled. Harris followed UD a long punt and robbed Taylor at centre. Then with the field et his heels Harris raced for the line and just got in first, no goal result- ing. Half time : Warrington, 8 points ; Hull. nil. Hull turned out after the interval minus Harmer who had sestaineda fractured rib. Warrington played a bit lazily and were forced to defend. Ildrnonds being held near the line. Hull heeled out and Thompson got over with a strong effort. No goal followed. Warrington worked back to centre but were let down by Hallam. and very goon after Jacques landed a penalty goal. Both teams now played keenly bet the quality of the football deteriorated. Thomp- son nearly scored again, and Fish was also dangerous on one or two occasions but for the most part play was confined to midfield. Time came with Warrington winning by 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) ; to Hull 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>LEIMI V BRIGROUSE EAFISERS. This match, which was postponed owing to the frost, was played on Tuesday at Leigh. before 1 tEI spectators. Leigh were superior e all points to their opponents• Play was limited to thirty minute! each way. Result :—Leigh, three goals and four tries ad 'points); Brighotise Hangers, one goal (two points). BATLEY Y. HUDDERSFIELD. At Batley on Tuesday, before 2,030 spectators, in grand weather. Oakland got over for Batley. Davies kicking a goal. Result : Batley one goal and one try (Ewe pilots); Huddersfield, nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v OLDHAM. At Runcorn. Fast and open play characterised the open- ing stages of this match. One smart, quick movement ended in Butterworth scoring for Runcorn. and Oldham's efforts to draw level'were well frustrated. In the second half Butter- worth added another try for Runcorn, and then Oldham had a nrolonged attack. Runcorn reasserted themselves. and Richardson scored, Moul.on klaking a goal. Result : Runcorn. 11 points; Oldham, nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
