      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Ittterl to the Ebitor.</head>
        <p>Ittterl to the Ebitor.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church, Newton-le-Willows</p>
        <p>—T he 'Organist.</p>
        <p>Sir,—.l wave of disturbanc—e seems to have passed lately over our local organics, and to have displaced most of them. There is one organist—Mr Peter Fair. dough, of St Peter's Church, Newteis—whose retirement should have more than a mere passing record. For thirty-five years as organist, and for thirty years as organist and choirmaster, Mr Fairclough has steadfastly served the congregati an of St Peter's Cnurch. There are many ot us who think that due recognition should be male of such meritorious service and a substantial testimonial be given to Mr Fairclough a: the end of it. I do not anticipate there will be one dissentient voice in the whole congregation. Now, if it is to be done it will be best to do it quickly. A general representative c marnittee might Ise formed, and out of that an executive committee elected to carry out their beh, ste.—l am, is;., JOHN W. WATKINS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Kirk-by, Ne arton-!e-Willowg, 5t13 March, 1902. Liberalism and C.B.</head>
        <p>Kirk-by, Ne arton-!e-Willowg, 5t13 March, 1902. Liberalism and C.B.</p>
        <p>Sir.—lt is a significant fact that now Lord Rosebery has returned to active politics there should be so many people, hitherto good Lthdrala. ready to cast off their allegiance to Sir HC B ianerman in preference to the former. Lord Rotiobery has done practically nothing for Liberalism during the past five years and is more- over at the present moment site hula, therefore should be regarded tardily. The contempt which Sir H C Bannerman has endured at the hands of critics who have nothing to recommend them- selves but empty brag and v,ortbless speech has been abnormal yet he has Wield this ignorant outburst with all the stolidity for which the ancient Radical Etalwaits are noted. He deserves com- mendation and support for his equiformity, and is a worthy follower of a party waich cart boast such great names in history as Cobden and Bright. Imperialism of the highest order has been enablazened on his banner and the Tarty should recognise his standard qualities. Lord Jonn Russell says it is possible for a nation to progress towards decay as well an towards efficiency and it is evident that if the r resent governing regime are to be allowed "free passes" the former epitaph will be eligible for inscrip- tion on the page of constitutional government. The progresiive element in the country should unite in the commoe Cause and not allow squabbles to disentegrate their strength. Principle should be in the forefront and personality M a back seat, and Lord It)ailbery and Sir H C Bannerman stand for what they are worth, and if the slate has to be cleaned and rewritten on let it be with the consent of the " stalwarts "who have held to the ship during equalls.—Yours, T.H.F.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Death Rate—lnteresting Corn-</p>
        <p>_ parison. According to the Registrar-General's official re- turns the following table shows the rate of mortal- ity in Warrington and other Lancashire towns last week •—•</p>
        <p>Death rate. Death rate. Stockport._ ..... 230 Oldham 21.0 St Helens_„.... 28 0 Burnley 230 Wigan 230 Blackburn 26.0 WARRINUTOIS. 170 Preston ..... 290 Bolton 20.0 Huddersfield... 21.0 Bury 16.0 Halifax ....... 24.0 Manchester •••.,., 21.0 Bradford ....... 17.0 Salford ....... 240 Rochdale The average for the 76 great towns was 23 3 per 1,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MR, PITT'S FAVORIIE REMEDY.</head>
        <p>MR, PITT'S FAVORIIE REMEDY.</p>
        <p>William Pitt tried to overcome his constitutional weakness by drinking freely of port wine. Ue fairly saturated himself with it. Seasoned topers were alarmed to se how much he drank. His friends protested, but, setting what George 111 called" that infernal obstinate upper lip "of hie, Pitt kept on drinking pert. Did it help him ? Not it. Alcohol is first a stimulant, then a narcotic ; never a food ; and good authorities say it is seldom a useful remedy. A real medicine is an article adapted to relieve and cure a disease, or group of diseases, just as a glass of water relieves and cures a thirst. A fellow may try to fight a complaint with an "I-don't-mind" AND DOSES OR DUTCH COURAGE, but it won't work. He has got to use the proper remedy—if there is one and he can get hold of it. If his trouble is a cough, a cold, a lame back, an attack of rheumatism or neuralgia, a temporary knock down with liver or kidney failur3, an onset of asthma or other lung disorder,—the remedy is Benson's Porous Plasters ; one or as many as may be needed. To be sure there are other plasters ; and there are oils, ointments, liniments and so on, no end. Some of 'em may not be hialf bad, but Benson's are as much better as the dome of St. Paul's is higher than a haystack. They will set you right when other applications (or mixtures) will only go to sleep over you, or make you sick. They are the remedy that its the ailment. Nothing else in England is so cheap tor a shilling. Get the genuine Benson's. For sale in Warrington by H. M. Ashton, 63, Sankey. street, Boots Ltd., 5 and 35 Bridge-street, Hill and Co, Buttermarket-street, H. Miller, 77, Bridge-street, and J. R. Young, 40, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MUSIC FOR THE PEOPLE. Annual Meeting of the Association of Bands.</head>
        <p>MUSIC FOR THE PEOPLE. Annual Meeting of the Association of Bands.</p>
        <p>4ftiteccle</p>
        <p>Mr T J Down pre,idod at the annual meeting of the Warrington Association of Bands which was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Monday tivening.</p>
        <p>TRH PAST MR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr B WADSWORRI (hon. sec.) read the following re- port :—" During the past year our associaton has sup- plied music in the Park every Sunday,and one other per- formance during reach week, generally on a Wednesday evening. In all, 34 performanees have been given, 20 on Sundays, and the remainder on Wednesdays. As useal, tho Volunteer Band opened the season on Easter Sunday, April 7th. The final performance of the year , was accompanied by a display of fireworks, under the I charge of Mr Parkinson, provided at the expense of Messrs P Walker, and Son, whose band occupied the stand on that evening. The weather during the sum- mer was specially favourable for outdoor entertsia- ment with the consequence that not one Sunday, and only one week-day performance had to be postponed. This fact alio compelled us to discontinue performances earlier than usual. Our funds, which in a wet season might have lasted into September, were more than ex- hausted by the end of August. Our balance sheet for the year (prepared by Mr Fairhurst, borough treasurer, and hon. treasurer to this association) stands as follows: —Receipts : To contribution from Warrington Corpora- Von, £63 les 61; balance due to the bask. December 31, 1901, £7 16s 10d ; making a total of £7l 133 4d. Expenditure : Balance due to b.nk.llth January,l9ol, £3 16a 6J ; payments to bands, £67 5 6J ; printing and postagem. 9s 6d ; bank interest, is 10.1, making a total of £7l 133 41. Our association now consists of eleven bands, but three of these did net appear in the park last season, viz., Monks, Hall and Co', Padgate Industrial, and Latchford, the last named only having been ad- mitted as members at the end of the Season. The Latchford band obtained permission to give one or two ; performances in the Victoria Park, and attracted appre- ciative audienees. An application for membership bas been received from the Legh-street Subscription Band. lam pleased to notice that more attention is being given to the character of the music performed by our banie. Personally, I hope all bands will choose the best music obtainable, and play it so as to do credit to themselves, this association, and the town, It is pro- , bable that the general public understand more about the music than we perhaps givA them credit for. They generally assemble in large numbers if a good perform- ance of the best music is expected. It is our business,and I hope our aim, to at tempt to elevate the musical tastes at the people, and to increase their intelligence in musical matters. I have often thought that interesting information about composers and their music might occalionally be included in the performances, and dur- ing the coming season shall probably supply a little to the papers. The president, vice-presidenre, kc., of the association are all eligible for re-election." (Applause.) Mr Wadsworth added that they were in debt to the amount of £7 16s 10d. A day orrtwo ago he received a letter from the Town Clerk, in response to an applica- tion for an increased grant to the bands, to the effect that hie (Mr Wadsworth's dommunicatien) had been laid before the committee, but as this attendance of members was so small the matter was deferred to the next meeting to be held some time this month. He had been told that evening by a member of the committee that the application of the association was early enough for the estimates.</p>
        <p>The report and statement of amounts were adopted THE CHAIRMAN'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>The CRAIRMAN in the course of his speech said he would urge all of them to aim high, to aim at playing good music and playing it well. They must not be con- tent with playing a few quick steps, a sort of walking. day programme, but good overtures. Thee might find I difficulties at first, but in the end they would be repaid a hundred-fold more than by playing the trashy stuff they were accustomed to hear from some bands. They could not all be players like the members of the Besses o' tb' Barn hand, but they should try to get as near to them as possible. That ought to be their object—to do everything to the best of their ability. (Applause.) The bands connected with the association load done good seo-k during the past season in the way of public performances, but, unfortunately, they had run into debt. He hated debt, and he should not like to be assciated with any institution in the habit of getting into debt. This *as the second year they had been in debt, and he wished them to devise means to prevent a continuance of that state of things, Ho hoped they would obtain an increased grant from the Corporation, which would enable them to go on comfortably and satisfactorily without getting into debt. He admitted that the Corporation had many cells upon them at the present moment, and that they would be reluctant to give the bands more monev,although his own experience of the committee was that they were always willing to listen to reason and were most kindly disposed towards the Association of Bands. (Efear, hear.) They were not very expectant. The Warrington bandsmen were not nearly as well paid as the Manchester bandsmen. In Manchester the men got on the average £4, whereas in Warrington they only got £2, although the average attendance at the performance was about 2,000. He considered,therefore,that that was money well spent,He hoped they would have good weather for the ensuing performances and a successful season. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr T ARMSTRONG, seconded by Mr Fltrones, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman for his services to the Association. Tne CHLIRMAN said it was a great pleasure to him to be associated with the association, and to do anything he could to help the members. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The Following officers were appointed : President, Mr T J Down ' • vice-presidents, the Mayor, and Messrs F Monks, A HCros6eld, R A Naylor. 7Aldermen Bolton, Heaton and Monks, and the members of the Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee ; hon. treasurer, Mr John Fairhurst ; hon. secretary, Mr B Wadsworth. These gentlemen, as well as the Chief Constable, were also thanked for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TO AID THE INFIRMARY</head>
        <p>TO AID THE INFIRMARY</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Praise- worthy Efforts. • The Mayor's Commendation.</p>
        <p>That many of the workingmen of Warrington take a deep interest in the welfare of the Infirmary and Dispensary was evident from the representa- tive gateering which assembled in the Town Hall on Saturday afternoon on the occasion of the annual meeting of the Workingmen's C3mmittee. The meeting was presided over by the Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr), and amongst others present were: Councillor John Roberts (chairman of the com- mittee), Mr D Fanshaw (vice-chairman), the Rev Chnon Willis, Mr F' Monks, Alderman Monks, Councillor Burton, and Messrs John Fairclough, T J Down, Charles White, G Dakin, C Leigh, and J Taylor (hon. secretary). Several letters of apology for non-attendance were read. Alderman Roberts wrote: "I hope the good cause your Committee hae,,so much at heart may be increasingly pros- perous.' Mr J J Blackly forwarded a donation of £5. Mr E Pickavance, jun., (secretary of the Cycling Club) wrote saying that the club were awaiting developments from the Council with res- pect to a cycle parade being held during the year. Mr Arthur Booth wrote regretting his inability to attend. Dr Fox wrute to say that he had received a telegraphic message to go into the country and would not be able to he present. He went on to say " I like to be with you on these occasions to show by my presence and a few words how much the Workingmen's Committee is appreciated and how hard and assiduously they work for our Infir- mary, vieing with each other as to who can do the most.lt has been my pleasure at former meetings to point out how high Warrington stood on the list of hospitals throughout England in the subscriptions contributed by the workingmen, and I trust that this year you have maintained your high position."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>INTERESTING SPEECH BY THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING SPEECH BY THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR then addressed the meeting,remark- ing at the outset that what struck him most in looking over the balance sheet and subscription list which the Chairman had been good enough to furnish him with was the very representative character ot the subscription list. It seemed to him to comprehend every description of work and employmant in Warrington, and bore conclusive evidence that the workingmen of W arrington,what- ever their work or employment or business might be, were animated by one feeling and one desire to assist the sick and suffering and by contributing so largely to our hospital to ensure its efficiency and enlarge its sphere of action so that its benefits might be within the reach of all who by accident or illness required its assistance. (Hear, hear.) There was, he believed, no country in the world where charity in the, form of giving and support- ing philanthropic anl benevolent institutions was ao,universal and so far reaching as it was in our own favoured land. From the top of the tree downwards it seemed to:flow in a health-giving constant stream. Our gracious King and beloved Queen both, as they web knew, took the deepest interest in hospital work, not only by their liberal money contributions, but even more by their kind sympathy and encouragement and ex- ample, for as Prince and Princess of Wales they were 6,ever ready with heart and hand to promote the interests of our hospitals, sparing neither time nor trouble in order to relieve our great national and public institu- tions from the burden of debt which was threatening to cripple and restrict their usefulness. (Hear, hear). Jae was happy to feel that tnis noble example had not been lest upon them, and that Warrington was determined not to be behindband in supporting its hospital. (Hear, hear). As Mayor of the County Borough he felt they had every reason to feel proud of their workingmen for their continued support of this noble institution, and especially so for the year now under consideration when so man' calls had had to be met in consequence of the war in South Africa, calls to which the workingmen of Warrington had so liberally reeponded, and that without diminishing their subscription or relax- ing their endeavoure in support of the lecal hospital. The year which was now opening would be a memorable one in our annals, and would they hoped. witness the Coronation of our King and Queen. He need hardly remind them that they could not pay a more grateful and graceful tribute to the Sovereign whose coronation it would be their pleasure and duty to celebrate in a loyal and befitting manner than by doing their utmost to turner the object which they knew the King and Queen had so near at heart and by making their coronation year a record year for the funds of their borough hospital. (Applause.) In conclusion he would like also to recognizo the debt they owed ta the workingmen for their very liboral contribution to the Nursing Institution. They all knew how essential it was that goad nursing shoulc: follow upon good medical treatment, and it was a great satisfaction to feel that their workingmen recognize that and were inclined to contribute 80 liberally to- wards it. He might remind them how very much at heart our late Queen regarded the nursing of the poor, and that she instituted that noble institution, the Queen's Jubilee Nurses. (Hear, hear.) Proceeding to refer to the general efficiency and good management of the Infirmary, Mr Parr said that with their paid staff and their honorary staff, he did not think any institution in England could be more efficiently or better managed, and they thanked those geetlenaen ior the time and trouble thee devoted to its interests. This year no fewer than 6,288 indoor and outdoor patients had been more or less assht cd from the hospital, which was an increase upon the previous year of 309: He thought that was conclusive evidence that the hospital was being worked in an efficient manner and was relieving an enormous amount of suffering and distress. (Applause.) THE PAST YEAR'S SUBSCRIPTIONS. From the subscription list it appeared that the subscriptions for the veer amounted to £742 17s Iod, as compared with £1,136 4s 501 in the previnter year. The committee bad handed over the sum of £52 2s 7d to the treaeurer of the Nursing Association. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Jonit ROE/HITS moved the adoption of the financial statement and explained that the deficiency of £393:as compared:with:last year'e subscriptions was main- ly due to the fact that there was no cycle parade last rear. He was very pleased to hear that the Cycling Club contemplated another parade as it would probably mean the realintion of about £3OO. The remainder of the deficiency was gauged by the reduction of the con- tributions from various firmer. Continuing Councillor Roberts said the noerabers tof the committee had dis- played as much energy in their work during the past year as they had done since its formation twenty years ago. They were still anxious to continue the work on behalf of the institution and would endeavour to incresso the subscriptions as much as possible. The amount handed to the Nursing Association was £l5 8s lid Isms than in 1900. 'Flati question of the adviesbility of forming a committee specially to work for the Nursing Association had been diecussed.and they were quite willing to appoint such a committee if the idea were approved by Mr Monks, who was the founder of the Nursing Association. Mr FAN•H&amp;W seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr F MONKS, in supporting, said he looked upon the Nursing Association as part and parcel of the Infirmary plan, The Workingmen's COomittee had not done quite as much for the association as they would have liked. They were not, however, going to fall back on that account. Re had made an appeal on behalf of the association to the various friendly societies, and had received donations of £3O, £9, £B,and smaller amounts. Others had promised to give the appeal their considera- tion. The result was that the association had a small balance to the good at the present time, and it would not be very much behind at the end of the year. It would, he thought, be a very good thing if certain members of the Workingmen's Committee co-operated with the friendly societies in forming an organhiation outside tha'.; of the committee,and he hoped that by that means a much more handsome sum would be realized than hitherto. Mr Monks commented upon the decrease in the contributions from some of the firms, and said that by simple organization much more could be raised. He still hoped to see £l,OOO handed over. He thought it was the duty of the workingmen of Warringtru, to endeavour to raise a handsome sum because they almost entirely received the benefits of the institution. The financial statement was adopted. THE COMMITTEE.</p>
      </div>
