      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>THE LATE MR. ROBERT DAVIES.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. The remains of the late Mr Davies were interred in the Warrington Cemetery on Thursday last week amid weeny manifestations of regret. The cortege left the re- sidence, Birch Dale, shortly before eleven o'clo3k. A portion of the route of the procession was lined ty the scholars attending St Thomse's Day and Sun- day Schools, the boys being under the charge of the headmaster, Mr Rose, and the girls and infants respectively under the care of Miss Hartley and Miss Morris. Blinds were drawn at the houses along the route as a mark of reepect. The chief mourners were : Mr Vere B, Mr Leonard G and Mr E G DaViet" (sons), Mrs E G Davies (daughter-in-law), Mrs Crosfield, Mr A H Crosfield, Mrs Charley (Ince), Mr and Mrs , Thomas (Frodsham), Mr and Mrs El P Mortimer, Mr Frank James, Mr Charles and Mr Hugh 'I insley, Nurse Jones and Nurse Tattersall, Mr E Kirkconnel, Mr J Burgess and Mr Hatton, (members of the firm of which the deceased was the bead),Dr Parker (deceased's medi- cal attendant.) On arrival at the Church gates, the coffin was carried on the shoulders of eight bearers into the church where the first portion of the burial service was read. The choristers walked at the head and were accompanied by the officiating clergymen.the Rev El V Pigot (vicar),and the Rev E Charley (vicar of Ince near Chester). The coffin was of fumed oak with brass mountings and en- clnsed a shell. The inseription on the plate was " Robert' Davies, born October 10, 1826, died February 24, 1902." There was a large congregation at the church, including the Mayor and Mayoress (Mr and Mrs J Charlton Parr), Sir Gilbert Greeeall, Rev R Greenall (Greppenhall), Rev T S Tot 01 (formerly vicar of the Parish) Rev El A. Lester (Training Celleg,e), Rev F E Powell, Rev E R Tarbuck, Rev J S Hughes (Hill Ode), Meesrs R Wynne Jones, T J Down, Liner Kaye, John White, R I StonceNewton-le-Willows). Aldermen J Shaw Green,F W Monks,Sut6on.Feirclough,Maj or Roome,Major J D Buck- ' ton, Messrs John Fairclough,W B Roberts, D Garnett I G Whin, E Milner, J B Leach (St. Helens), Charles White, W Carson, W Owen, W Skinner, (Parr's Bank), 1 R W Rawson, R Shackletora,S Chandley, James Strange, I J Nield, J Albinson, A Mooree, J Whittle, John Hallows, Brereton. and Partington. The office staff was repre- sented by Messre E Draper, T Welebv, T Cooper, G S Salway, J Gandy, H J Westbrook, J Taylor, J Hewitt, G Smith, 'l' Webster, T R Westbrook, W Taylor, A Brayehaw. S Bailey, J Lee, W Sumner, H Whittle, P Brayshaw, 9 Barlow, W Glover, and H Isaac. The deceased gentleman's bead gardener (Mr Miller) was also preeent. The following officers of the church were present and rendered assistance : Mr Henry Greenall and Mr Richardson (wardens), Messrs J H Clarke. A Down, J McGregor, S W Hallow, T J Lee, John Jeffs, W Mclntyre. The Stockton Heath Parish Council was reprenented by Mr Fairhurst, Mr Whitley and Mr E Bradsleaw (clerk). After the reading of a portien of the burial service, the hymn" Abide with Me " wes sang by the choir and ' congregation. Mr Rose then played the "Dead March," from Saul, and the service concluded. The cortege then proceeded ion its way to the Warrington Cemetery. There was a long line of carriages including private carriages from Mrs Mortimer, Mr H P Mortimer,Mr T II Lyon (Appleton Hall), Mr R I Wynne Jones, Mrs Pick- mere, Sir Gilbert Grapnel!, Mr J Charlton Parr, Mr Charles White and Mr John Fairclougb. There was also a large bus containing the maids from Birch Dale. A representative number of solicitors joined the procee- niou at Bridge Foot and walked at the head of the pro- ceseion to the Cemetery. The solicitors present were : Messrs W D Jeans, son, T S Ridgway, H W ode, G F F White„T Longland, R Dobson, W D Jeans, jun, J L Whittle (Town Clerk), E A White, W J Jenkins, Spencer Wellhead, Sydney Wellhead, E E Woods, H J Willett, T S Steel, C B Unswort'n,S Wilson, W E Fowler,l E A Connor, and TEI Granger. Mr Thomas Tun.tell (actuary at the Savings Bank) also welked with the solicitors. A deputation from the Warrington Cen- servative Association and Club, consisting of Mr W Holbrook, Mr George Jolley, and Mr Herbert Elayes,also ioined the procession. On arrival at the Cemetery.'a large number of towns- men had assembled. Amongst those present were the Rector (Rev Canon Willie). Rev E C E Carleton, Rev E J Wil,cocks (Grammar School), Rev B Percival, Rev John Yonge, Rev G Vale 0 won (Orford), Alderman Hutchinson, Councillors Rogers, Sampson, Smethurst, Mr C E Ilindley, (ex-Mayor of Eccles), Messrs T Birtles, John Harrison, John Dewhurst,F S Whittle. T B Carter, T Cnantry, J M Edelsten, James Craik, S Dunbobbln and other. The service at the graveside was brief and impressive and was conducted by the Rev H V Five. A number of choice 11 iral tributes were placed upon the lid of the coffin from near relatives These included wreaths from Vere,Eintnie and his grandchildren ; "Ted and Mabel," "Leonard." Mr Edward Davies and family (Blundellsands). Wreaths were sent by the following :—Mrs 13eamont, Major and Mrs Roome, Mr James Tinsley (Daresbury Hall), Mrs Morland Crosfield, L Harrison, Mrs John Cresfield and Miss Croslield, directors and officers of the Warrington Permanent Benefit,' Building Society, the gardeners and coachman, Mr Herbert Hatton (Thelwall), Mr and Mrs Joseph Harrison (Letchford), Warrington Conservative Association, Messrs Frank James, Charles and Hugh Tinsley, Rev aed Mrs Edward Charley (Ince Vicarage), el sorrowing friend," E :ward Ktrkconnel, Mr and Mrs Woods and the Misses Woods (Worthing), Mr and Mrs Lyon (Appleton Hall). The wardens aad sideemen of St Thomas's Church, the staff of Robert Davies and Company, Mr and Mrs John White (Moore Hall), Mrs Pye, Mrs Cecil Greenall,Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenell, Mr and Mrs G W Griffith, Mr and Mrs Henry Greenall, " From M.M.," Mice Massy (Latchford), Mr and Mrs John Burgess (Lymm), the maids at Birch Dale, Mwiel and H Percy Mortimer, Mr and Mrs T A Frost (Grappenhall), the servants at Dulcefleld. Mr and Mrs ,Rigby, Mr and Mrs Logan Wright,the staff of St Thomas' e elementary day school, the boys, girls, and infants of St Thomas's echools, Mr and Mrs Thomas (Frodsham), Edward Draper, Mrs Massey (Acton Grange). The funeral arrangements were efficiently made by Messrs J and W Dutton. REFERENCES AT THE CONSERVATIVE CLUB. AN IRREPARABLE LOSS.</p>
        <p>At the annual mestiog of the Warrington Conservative Ass)ciation held on Thursday last week Alderman Sutton said that Warrington had just sustained a very severe loss by the death of Mr Hobert Davies, one of our oldest and most respected townsmen, whose remains were that day interred at the 'Warrington Cemetery. He (the speaker) did not Intend t' refer to the late Mr Davres'a numerous good qualittes cr his charitable disposition towards all the VArioll9 institutions in the town,because they Were So well-known to everybody that it was not necessary for him to do so, but .he would just say that as a member and one of the deputy presidents of the Association they could not have had a better friend. I For a long number of years, and so long as health per- mitted him, the late Mr Davies always took an active and prominent part and interest in the work and welfare of that association, and he was always a generous con- tributor to the association, so that his loss would be very much felt and it would he vary difficult indeed to replace him. He proposed " That the members of the Warrington Conservative Association desire to place on record its deep sense of the itreparable loss it has sustained by the death of the late Mr Robert Davies, and to empress its appreciation of the very valuable services rendered by him as one of the leaders and deputy-president of the Conservative party in Warrington for over half a century." Mr C S BURGESS seconded and said that Mr Davies was a man who throughout kislife had shown an inter- est in every good work and institution in the town. Sir GILBERT GREBNALL, Bart., supported and said Mr Davies bact been connected with his family for many years. Both personally and as a political friend they all knew what his worth had been. The resolution was unaultnonsly carried. At a later stage of the meting Mr John White also made a reference to the sad event. He said Mr Davies was his oldest and most valued friend. Ho bad known him all his life and he had never been anything to him but the very kindest of friends. He wished to place on record hisideep and sincere regret at the loss which had befallen not only himself but the whole of the town generally. They bad thought tney had many' losses in days gone by. They thought that when Mr Beamont died they could not replace him, and many able and Influential men had since died but there was no one who seemed to have entered into the hearts and homes of the people of Warrington so much as Mr Robert Davies bad done, from the highest to the lowest. REFERENCE AT THE LIBERAL CLUB. Spealting at a smoking concert and distribution of billiard prizes at the Liberal Club, on Tuesday evening, Mr A fl (Iromfield alluded in sympathetic terms to the deceased gentleman. He said he frit heard the news of ids Davis' s death as be arrived at Bank Quay Station that evening from London. Mr Davies was an old friend of his (Mr Crostield'a) father and an honaured towne loan. His death was another link severed with the Past. (Rear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
