      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CORPORATION'S LIGHT RAILWAY UNDERI AKING.</head>
        <p>THE CORPORATION'S LIGHT RAILWAY UNDERI AKING.</p>
        <p>The Scheme Approved. -- Inquiry at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>- - For four hours on Tuesday, the application of the Warrington Corporation tor an order to construct a light railway from the terminus of the tramway at Stafford-road to Victoria-square, Stockton Heath, and thence along Grappenhall-road and Hunt's- lane, to the Latchford swing bridge, where it will connect with the Latchford tramway, was the subject of a public inquiry. The inquiry was conducted by Colonel Boughey C.S.I. (coairman) and Mr Henry Allan Steward, on behalf of the Light Railway Cominissioners.—Toe Commissioners were attended by Mr E S Bateman (acting secretary). Con- siderable interest was taken in tlae proceedings and the Council Chamber at the Town Elalain which the inquiry was beld was filled. Immediately on the inquiry being opened, Mr F Pendlebury said he appearea as a rate- payer ;Mr S Gough announced that be appeared as a ratepayer to oppose, and Mr John Pemberton informed the Commissioners that he appeared as an owner of property at Stockton Heath. Mr Sutton and Mr Tobin instructed by the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle) appeared for the Corporation ; Mr Overenn Evans, instructed by Messrs Brown,Brown,and Murphy, appeared on behalf of the Northwich Light R ale/ay Company ; Mr Herbert Hatton represented the Stockton Heath Pariah Council ; Mr Potts, the Cheshire County Council ; Mr Geo F Ashton, the Runcorn Rural District Council ; and Mr W d Hunter, the Manchester Ship Canal Company. Among others present were the Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr), the Deputy Mayor (Alder- man Roberti), and eeveral members and officials of the Corporation ; Major Cardew, (electrical engineer) ; Mr Scoter, (engineer for the Northwich Light Railway Company) ; Mr Bradshaw (clerk to the Stockton Heath Parish Council), several members of these authorities, and a number of ratepayers. Mr Sutton, in; stating the ease for the Corporation, remarked at the outset that the scheme was under the consideration of the Commissioners last year. At that time it was promoted as part of their scheme by the Northwich Light Rail way Camps y. The advisability of the construction of this reheat, was at that time considered, and the Court came to the conclusion that there was no doubt it would be a great advantage to the inhabitants and ought to be mane. 'Thera arose one difficulty in regard to the construction of the railway and that was in connection with the Ship Canal Corn- because ttaere were cert tin arrangements made between the Light Railway Company and the Ship Caaal Company which, in the words of the Earl of Jersey, seemed to put the Light Railway Company very much in toe hands of the Ship Canal Company. The Corporation had an arrangement with th promoters of the Light Rahway to the effect that if the arrangement with the Snip Canal Company was such as did not meet with the approval of the Corporation the pro- moters could give up that part of their scheme and the Corporation would take it up. It was in consequence of the difficulty mentioned that Ihat part of the scheme was dropped and the Corporation had taken it up. Toe promoters at that time proposed to lay only a single line of railway and there was also considerable discus- sion at the time as' to the width of the road. The Cor- poration proposed to lay a double line of railway, and in order to do that were prepared to effect what would be considered a very great improvement of the road. London-road would ae widened as shown on the plan. As they came to Victoria-square, formerly the Market Place at Stockton fleath,l there was a very awkward corner turning into Grappenhall-road. The Corporation brad taken steps to remove that corner altogether. They proposed to make London-road 50 feet wide and never less than 40 feet, which would meat all the requirements of the Board of Trade. Mr Sutton proceeded to describe in detail the route of the proposed light railway. Grappenhall-road and some parts of the connecting roads were winding, and wherever there was a wind in the road as would prevent a clear view, the Corporation proposed to widen the road. Wherever possible a double line of rail§ would be laid, and every precaution taken to make the whale course of the line from beginning to end, a perfectly safe and convenient one. [he railway would enable the people of Warrington to get out into a very healthy locality which was an important matter in all our busy centres of industry. It would also bring some important resi- dential openings to Warrington and also he of advantage to the tradesmen of Warrington by giving the people outside an opportunity of coming into the town for shopping purposes. Another advantage was that instead of having two dead ends of their tramway system at Stockton Heath and Latchford there would be a circular system which was very much better. He was happy to say that eatisfac• tory terms had been arranged with the Cheshire County Council and the Runcorn Rural District Council, and be understood they were prepared to support the scheme. With regard to the Stockton Heath Parish Council he did not understand bow it had an) locus staudi, though of course individual ratepayers were entitled to appear. The Parish Council bad nothing to do with the roads. The Council had asked the Corporation to Jo certain things which they could not by any means admit. He could not help thinking the Panda Council had a very keen eye to its own advantages and a eery blind eye to what was fair to those they were dealing with. Mr Sutton proceeded to read the requirements of the Parish Council and pointed out those the Corporation were prepared to accep) and those which they rejected. Referring to the request for penny fares, Mr Sutton said the Corporation honed they would rot be obliged to charge more than a penny, but the Corporation ought tot to be limited to that fare because at some time or other they might find it necessary to increase the fares for some length of time, and it would be very unfair indeed to the ratepayers of Warrington if the people outside should insist on a fare which was not remunerative and so throw the burden upon the ratepayess. The request of the Stockton Heath Parish Council that the railway should be laid in the same way as the Corporation tramways was rather a compliment to the Corporation's laying of rails in Warrington and they were quite prepared to lay them precisely the same way as in the Borough. Mr Sutton proceeded to deal with the question affecting the Ship Canal. He said the difficulty arose over the al gnment of the swing bridges. i If tramways were taken over the bridges the alignment both laterally and vertically would have to be perfectly accurate. A certain amount of expense would be neces- sitated to provide for that, and the Corporathm did not desire nor did not ask for the whole of it to be thrown upon the thin Canal Company. Any adaitienal expense I incurred by making a perfect alignment would, he Un- derstood, be undertaken by the Corporation. The Ship Canal Company, however, seemed to require a good deal more than thar,and as the Corporation were anable to fall in with their views they (the Comptny) were now opposing the scheme altogether. He (Mr Sutton) could not think the difficulty insuperable or such as would make the proposal impracticable, and he thought, with the assistance of the Conamissioners, they would be able to arrange such terms as would be mutually satisfmctor. It was estimated that the scheme would cost 225,814, and the Corporation asked to borrow £30,000. In con- clusion, Mr Sutton pointed out the improvements in the present scheme as compared with that submitted by the Northwich Light Railway. Major Cardew, consulting engineer in partnership with Sir William Preece, and formerly electrical adviser to the Board of Trade, pyoduced a plan of the railway. He said the railway started from the Wi'derspool-road end of the existing tramways, crossed the Ship Canal and proceeded as far as the Market-place, Stockton Heath, where there would be a junction made with No 2 railway. Beyond that junction there was a Email por- tion of single line taken down Old London-road with a view to forming a connection with the Northwich Light Railway. The secor d rail way would after the junction pass along Grappenbat-road to the Kautstird-road Swing Bridge and make connection with the existing system. The railways formed a loop which would connect up to the dead ends of the Corporation's present system of tramways and which would he a great advantage as regarded the working of the traffic. No 1 railway was double thronighouli excepting, a small portion at the junction with the Northwich Light Railway. No 2 was partly double and partly single. The road was some- what tortuous. A good &amp;al of road widening was pro- vided for, and where it was difficult to see alone the road they had preferred to make a doub,e line. The carriage road on No 1 railway was at its narrowest neint 35 feet. The Cr rporation were prepared to take off the awkward corn' r near the Market-place, Stockton Heath. These railways were to be treated as really an extension of the Warrington tramways. The estimate of what was necessary at the swing bridges was £4OO for each bridge That would provide for the accurate alignment or. the bridges. Be Mr Hatton : He was aware there cyal an existing 'bus service. The 'buses ran along the same route as that proposed by the Corporation and the fare was one penny. The distance was a mile and a quarter. He did not know what fares the Corporation intended tea chara.- They had not been s'ittiod yet. There was no resolution of the committee on the subject to Lis knowledge. By Mr Hunter :He thought the Corporation should bear the expense of anything beyond what was now necessary for the proper pssaage of ordinary traffic. He had never attempted to carry tramways over swing bridges like those over the:Ship Canal. Redid not think anyone had. He thou2dit the Corporation should pay the cost of the signalling, and they would undertake the responsibility for accidettl. Mr Hunter said he was going to submit aca't from matters of detail, that the crossing of these bridges was too serious a matter to sanction in connection with such a small scheme as the one proposed. Mr Fred renllebury : What will be the coat of railway number 2? Major Carclew : The estimated cost is £18,758, and that included the making of the line, widening and electricalequipment. It did not include care. Mr S Gough : Can you tell us when it is likely to open number 2? Major Cardew : I cannot give any definite date ; it a early to say.</p>
        <p>Mr Gough : Is there any shadow of a possibility of this railway paying the Corporation ? Witness : I think it may pay the Corporation. It would be :k distinct advantage to Warrington t 3 have such nice country within easy reach. Mr Gough : Would it not pay the Corporation to lay I£soo a year on one side, giving the inhabitants of the town a treat to the country, rather than carry Out this scheme ? (Laughter.) The Chairman : This is hardly a question we can discuss. .</p>
        <p>Mr Gough : It is absolutely impossible to make this scheme pay for many years to come. It would be a great injustice to the town to carry it out at the present moment. .</p>
        <p>Mr John Pemberton also asked a numbor of questions as:to the width of road outside the rails and between the kerb of the footpaths. Mr Thomas Longdin, borough surveyor of Warring- ton, was then called. In answer to Mr Tobin, he said the population of Stockton Heath was 2,543. A large propartion of the pppulation worked in Warrington daily. No.l railway would serve a district now supplied by 'bus'. From observations taken it was found that during one week 12,372 people came by 'bus and there were 28,974 pedestrians. For No 2 district the figures were—'bus, 5,369, pedestrians, 10,242. The present quarter of an hour 'bus service was irregular, and there had been numerous complaints. Mr Longdin pro- ceeded to give details of the various road widenings it was proposed to make. The narrowest point, he said, was 40ft. At the •• Market Place " at Stockton Heath It was proposed to pull down certain cottages. The 13orough of Warrington was conjestecl, and it was desir- able the public should have facilities for getting out into the country.</p>
        <p>Mr Hatton said that according to his instructions the figures given did not agree with those on the deposited plans. If the promoters would pledge themselves that the road should be a certain width, his opposition on that point would cease. The scheme was being put be- fore the Commissioners on the basis of London-road being made 451 t. 8 inches wide, and he asked if the pro- moters would undertake that it should be that width. Mr Sutton : We do not givi undertakings to you, you are not the proper authorities. The Chairman : I think the Parish Coma is entitled to ascertain the facts.</p>
        <p>Mr Hatton : If you say I can take it as abiolutely correct that the road will be made that width I support your scheme. Do you assure me that the powers of purchase will enable you to make the road 45 feet 8 iccoes wide at that point? Mr Longdin : Our powers of purchase are to the front of the building line. Mr Hatton said he applied to the Commissioners to make the undertaking a condition of the scheme. There was certainly a doubt in the figures and he could not I see how the promote,s could make the road that wide I at the point in question. , The Surveyor, in answer to Mr Hatton, said the Ship Canal did interfere with the traffic. Toe p.csent 'buses were licensed for 12 months. He could riot say whether the Corporation would cJntinue to license them after the trams were made.</p>
        <p>The witness was then questioned by Councillor Plumpton with regard to number two line. Cmincillor Plump:on asked if he thought th-u.a would be sufficient trafE3 to pay for the maintenance of the</p>
        <p>The Surveyor: My own knowledge is that as soon as a tramway is constructed people build and you get a larger number of people to use the trams. Councillor Plumpton : D 3 you think the r atepayers are doing right in speculating on a tram line where there are no inhabitants, or soarcely any, to increase the value of the property Co the land-owners in 'hat district, and neglecting their own town ? The Surveyor : I am not the Corporation. Councillor Plumpton, addressing the Chairman, said the resolution reqoacting No 2 fine was passed by the Town Council, simply because it was stated that the Corporation did not in end to make the line at all. (Hear, hear.) If the Chairman of the Tramways Com- mittee and the present Mayor had not made that state- ment, the Council would never have passed the resolution. He protested against the line being made. Mr John Pemberton asked several questions. At the outset he inquired from the Surveyor hether the Corporation had any serious intention of going on with No 2 line.</p>
        <p>The Sarveyor : I cannot tell you. lam not the Cr- potation. Mr Pemberton : Have you aLy quantities out ?</p>
        <p>The Survey:. r : lam not the engin,•er. I am simply the Borough Surveyor. Continuing, he said he did not think it would be better to widen the road on the church side. That would MeVia a great deal of expense and trouble aad the Ecclesiastical Comrnissioaers woull ob- ject. Mr Pemberton : I want the tramway more than any- body, because it will improve my property, but I know the danger near the church. D 3 you know that tour children have already been killed theta ? The Surveyor : Ido not know that. Witness add- d that the road would be widened.</p>
        <p>Mr Gough asked the witness if he did not think a man would be a lunatic who proposed to run a 'hug service on the NJ. 2 route. (Laughter). The Surveyor: That is an awkward question to answer. (Laughter). ! In answer to a qustion as to whether he considered it was practical to suggeit a tramway for that route the Surveyor said the Corporation aver 3 seeking the power to construct one. Mr Gough : They seek many things against the interests ot the ratepayem. DD you seriously think it will he a paying cone ,rn? Mr Longdin : I think it will evantually. I don't say i:nmediatoly. That de m 7 conscientious opinion. Mr Gough : Is it nr..t a fact that the Corporation were afraid to apply for a Bill because there was every probability that the ratepayers would have demanded a poll and there was no chance of them getting the Bill ? The Surveyor : That I cinnot say. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Nfr Rose, the headmaster at St Thomas's School, said he had seen so many accidents on the road near the school that he felt he could not stay away from the in- quiry. He thought it wou!d be very dangerous indeed if the road remained only fPrty feet wide as 600 school children passed along it four times a day. The parents urged that the road should be made from 50 to 60 feet wide.</p>
        <p>Mr Hatton spoke upon the question of fares. He suggested that the immediate natural consequence of the tramway s nice at a penny fare would 100 to drive the existing 'bus ervice off the road, and the inhabi- tants of Stockton Heath would los at the mercy of the Corporation, and that if the undertaking was not profit- able the Corporation would be at lib.irty to put the fare up to 2L N 3 dotal); the Carporation would suggest that the 'bus proprietor would then come back and pro- vide them with a penny 'bus. But even if the Corpora- tion would licence 'buses under these conditions—which 1' they had threatened they would not do—he submitted that it was not to be exper.tod that a proprietor would sink the necessary capital to establish a servica when he would be liable to be driven off at any moment by the reduction of the tram fare to a penny. This was a case of a municipal Corporation going out of its own boundary for the sake of making a profit. It was an instance of municipal trading which went much fu-ther : than any Case he had ever heard of. They sought to acquire a monopoly in ar.other district which would deprive the inhabitants of that district of a privilege they already enjoyed. He submitted that it should be a condition that there should be a maximum fare of a</p>
        <p>Mr Hatton thought it should be made a condition that the road near the Red Lion Inn should be made 50f. wide as there was no doubt that the Corporation would acquire the land.. _</p>
        <p>Sutton said it was an unusual width. Th. Chairman said it WEI° not an unusual width for two lines.</p>
        <p>Alderman Monks, deputy chairman of thP Tramways Committee, said he was presr-nt when tte resolution authorizing the schemes was passed. He (lid not hear any objcction raised: He had no recollection of hearing any statement to the effcct that the Corporation had no intention of carrying out the line along Grappennall- road.</p>
        <p>Councillor Plimpton ; When the ex-Mayor brought the matter forward didn't he distinctly say the Corpora- tion had no intention of making the line? •</p>
        <p>, Alderman Monks : I never her.l him rnlpluli such a statement.</p>
        <p>Councillor Piumpton : Diin't you hear the Mayor make the same statement? Atierman Monks : N. Councillor Plump;on ; I vouch for it that they both made that statement.</p>
        <p>_ The Mayor was called and said he had no recollection of making the statement. He said that probably the No 2 line would not be rEqurad at present but beyond that be did not go. Mr Reginald Potts, on behalf of the Cheshire County Council handed in the amecdmen's to the Order sugeested by that authority.He said the only point to which it was necessary to call attention %ca the width of the road way opposite the " Red Lion." If the width could be in- creasrd to fifty feet the County Council would much prefer</p>
        <p>Mr Overend Evans said the promoors of tle Warring- ton and North wich Light Railway did nQt want to stand in the path of the Warrington Ccrporation if the latter desired to construct these two lines themselves. There had been considerable discussion between the two bodies with regard to the terms upon which the promoters of his scheme (the Warrington and Northwicb Light Rail- way) should stand outside and allow the Warrington Corporation to take the matter in hand and terms had ' been arranged. They did not want to construct a line from Northwich to Stockton Heath and have no connec- tion with Warrington. He wished for some expression of opinion from the Light Railway Commissioners that it would be fair for the Corporation it Warrington, entering as they did upon the rights of the original promoters to subscribe something to the expenses which had been incurred by the promoters of the I Original scheme with respect to the two lines the Corpor- ation now sought to make. Mr Ashton, clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Coun- cil, said that that authority had considered the matter, and subject to certain amendments would support the</p>
        <p>order. Mr Hunter, on behalf of the Ship Canal Company, alluded to certain errors on the deposited plan—(" no,")—well, he would call them inconsistencies. He submitted that no case of benefit to the public had been made out. The Commissioners were aseed to sanction a mile and a ilea of light railway which had already been sanctioned for another body. He submitted with ' respect and confidence that for the intended exceptional interference with the Ship Canal Company's property on the part ef the Warrington Corporation no case what- ever had been made out, and that no evidence had been laid before the Commissioners, which would compel them to sanction the scheme. If the scheme were sanctioned he submittal that clauses for the protection of the Ship Canal Company should be inserted in the order. He proposed that the clauses should be in the f Mowing terms :—(1) That the rails be laid on each bridge at such levels and in such a manner as may be approved by the Canal Company ; (2) the elerporation to lay the rails at their own Coat and to carry out at their owe cost the whole of the alterations to the floors of the bridges rendered necessary by the laying of the rails, including the repaving of bridges and fixing ot new cast iron kerbs at either end of the bridges; (3) The Cm- poretiou to execute at their own expense the works re- quired for the proper adjustment of tne bridges for the purpose of preserving the exact all gnment of the rails; (4) The Corporation to erect at either end of each bridge, and in positions to be approved of by the Canal Company, prewr signals by which the working of the railways shall be controlled, the signals to ba operated electrically, the Corporation to provide the current, and the Canal Company to provide the necessary labour; (5) The Corporation to pay to the Canal Company such annual sum as may be agreed le: t ween the parties as will compensate the com- pany for the cost of working the signals and for the rasp onsibility to be involved in such working ; (6) In all cases the traffic of the Manchester Snip Canal Com- pany to have priority over the traffic on the lines of railway ; (7) The Corporation to undertake the entire responaioility for the maintenance and working of the railways over and across the bridges and also to under- take the maintenance and scavenging of the whole of the wooden block pavement and the roadway of each bridge ; (8) All works to be executed and operations to be carried out by the Corporation, as far as the bridges are concerned to be in accordance with plans and sections to be forwarded to and approved by the chief engineer of the Canal Company ; (9) all works and operations of the Corporation on the bridges to be carried out under the supervision of the chief engineer of the Canal Company or such person or persons be may appeine to act on his behalf, and the Corporation to pay to the Canal Company the reasonable expanses of the employment by the company of a sufficient number of inspectors and watchmen to be appointed by the company during the execution of the works in connec- tion with the bridges. Mr Fred Pendlebury contended that so far as No 2 railway was concerned there was very little evidence that it was required at all. The Warrington tramways should be constructed before she Corporation went out of town. He (the speaker) had written to the Town Clerk suggesting that the scheme would be a burden on the rates, and that from 136,000 to 140,000 passengers would be required to be carried per annum to pay the interest on the capital expended, not including the trams which would have to be providedor 2,500 passen- gers a week or 500 a day. It would be impossible to zee anything like that number, and the reply of Mr Whittle was that he (the writer) might rest assurred that the Corporation would not proceed with the rail- way unless they were satisfied there was a prospect of a satisfactory return. He (Mr Pendlebury) contended that there was not a propect of a satisfactory return. Mr Rose reiterated his objection with regard to the width of the mace Mr John Pemberton did not think the Corporation intended to go on with No. 2 scheme ; they had come to the Commissioners to get power to make purely and simply a tramway. They had tacked on No. 2 scheme to make the matter look a little larger. The Corpora- tion wished to evade the Tramways Act and the condi• tions which would be imposed on them. He was not opposing the tramways—he was a property owner in Stockten Heath and knew the advantages of it—but the Commissioners should not allow the Corporation to get powers under the Light Railway Act to evade the neces- sary conditioas that would be imposed on them under the Tramways Act. Opposite the church the road was far too narrow, and there had been five fatal accidents there already. He also contended that they were en- titled to a penny fare, and the public ought not to be placed in a worse position than they were in now. Mr Gough objected to the application on the ground of municipal trading. The Cmporation bad failed to show that it was likely the scheme would be profitable or in the interests of the ratepayers. One of the officials had suggested that they should take the ratepayers out of Warrington and put them in Grapnenball-road,which would be a residental district and likely to create fares for the trams: That would be a great disadvantage to the Borough of Warringten as it would reduce its rate- able value. Considering that at the last Council meeting t was stated that the Corporation was almost in a bankrupt position, and that the Corporation had net called upon the Borough Treasurer to show their financial position, he thought it would be folly on the part of the Commissioners to grant the scheme now proposed. He hoped they would, in the interests of the ratepayers, and not so much for a clique in the Council, who were company promoters, and largely traded with the Cor- poration for their own benefit and not for the benefit of the ratepayers,prevent the order being made. Mr Sutton said the matter had been before the Com- missiouers before, and they bad come to the conclusion that it would be a great advantage. There was not a tittle of evidence to show that No. 2 scheme would not go on. The Mayor bad said that it would not be neces- sary to commence it at once during his Mayoralty. The resolution was carried unanimously by the Tramways Committee, and the allegations that had been made ought not to have been made, and certainly not by a Ceuncillor of to borou2b. At a meeting of the Stock- ton Heath Parish Council, Mr Pemberton seconded the application of the Corporation to carry the tramways into the district, and said it would be a good thing if the tramway could be put down Grappenhall road, thus making a circular route. Mr Pemberton . I said I was opposed to the con- ditions. Mr Sutton, continuing, said the gentlemen who sug- gested that the Corporation came before the Commi•- sioners to evade the Tramways Act ought to make them- selves acquainted with the law. The Corporation could not go under the Tramways Act to construct a tramway out of their own district. The only way was to apply to the Light Railway Commissioners. Mr Evans had asked for a payment to the promoters of the original light railway scheme, but be did not know how it could be apportioned. He should, however, be glad if the Commissioners would express their opinion as to what should be done in the matter. It was for them to say. Counsel then proceeded to discuss Mr Hunter's clause+s, and it appeared that there would be little difficulty in coming to a settlement with regard to them. He said teat some of the terms of the Snip Canal Company were perfectly reasonable, and some required modifying. With regard to the fares he pointed otic that the accommedetion provided by the Corporation would be far better than that furnished by the omnibuses. For that increased accommodation the public should be willing to pay. The Corporation hoped to be able to • grant penny fares, but he ventured to Fay that they ' should not be deprived of the power of slightly ' increasing the fare if it were found necessary. THE DECISION. ; The Chairman said the Commissioners would be pre- pared to submit an order to the Board of Trade for both the railways, No 3. and No 2, but they would require to be further satisfied as to the possibility of getting an absolute minimum width of roadway on railway No 1, nowhere less than 32ft carriageway in addition to a reasonable width of footpath. With special reference I to the narrow point opposite St Thomas's Church and the Red Lion Inn, the Commissioners would require to be satisfied whether that could not be got out to the full width 32ft carriageway,in addition to a reasonably wide ; footpath. The Commissioners thought that the desire of the people of Stockton Heath for a special fare of a penny from the Market-place. Stockton Heath, to ; Rylands-street, was not in the circumstances unreason- ' able. Whilst retaining the ordinary sum of 21 in the order any such fare would he subject to the provisions of clause 69 of the draft order. The terms of the Ship Canal Company would be settled by the Commissioners on the lines which had been generally agreed to, and if any further difficulty arose the Commissioners would hear what both sides had to say. There was one simple I matter which was referred to with 'cord to the costs of the promoters of the Northwich Light Reilway,or so I much of this line as would be taken over by the Cor- poration. The Commissioners could only give an unofficial opinion on a matter of that kind, but taking all the circumstances into consideration, they would he ' inclined to say that half the amount contemplated by I the agreement would be a fair sum to be paid by the 3 Corporation on obtaining their order. That dealt with ' the points which had been raised before the Commis- ' eioners, and the order would be submit* to the Board of Trade accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY MARCH 7. 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY MARCH 7. 1902</p>
        <p>• NEWTON URBAN DIS- TRICT COUNCIL. Montkly Meeting. The monthly meeting of the Newton Urban District Council was held at the Town Hall, Earletdown, on Monday evening, Mr T Ormand presiding. There were also present :—Messrs C B F Borron, E T Evans, W Gilmour, N Siddeley, J Simpson, Fitzgerald, E L Bent, Dr Latham, and Dr Patterson with C Cole (clerk) and A Bowes (surveyor). GAS COMMITTEE—A MISUNDERSTANDING. At a meeting of the Gas Comenit:ee the following minutes of the Staff Committee were approved : that the salary of the works manager (Mr J T Eloggard) be iacr?ased to £2 123 per week, with house, coal, gas, and water free,the increase to date from the Ist instant; that the works manager (Mr J T Haggard) be directed to report, in writing, montely to the engineer ; and that the stores clerk (Mr r Swift) continue to be under the supervision of the Clerk to the Council. The engineer reported to the committee with respect to the present elate of the Gas Works, and as to the extensions which, in his opinion, were accessary, in order that sufficient gas might be made to meet the present and future demands. It was resolved that the engineer be directed I to prepare plans, specifications and estimates, (a) For extending the Retort House adjoining Gee-street in a I northerly direction, (b) For providina additional retorts, and (c) For adding two extra lilts to No. 3 gas-holder. It was recommended that the Extensions Committee be empowered to obtain tenders for carrying out the works with a view to application being made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to the rais- ing of a loan for defraying the cost thereof. A letter was read from the Gas worhs manager with re- spect to the application of the Vulcan Foundry, Ltd , for a supply of gas tor their Works, stating that when the proposed alterations are carried out at the works sufficient gas will be made to enable the committee to grant the request. It was resolved that the Vulcan Foundry, Ltd., be informed that the committee will en- deavour to supply them with sufficient gas to meet their requirements, on the terms and conditions to be here- after agreed opon. A letter was read from Messrs Robert Davies and Co., Solicitors to the Vulcan Foundry, Ltd., returning the proposed drift agreement for the lighting by the-Council of the road thrieugh Vulcan Village, which had been prepared and sent to them by the clerk, and suggesting that several of the clauses in toe agree- ment should be altered to read that the road which the Council proposed to light is the private property of the Vulcan Foundry, Ltd., and has never been dedicated to the use of the public. It was resolved that the clerk in- form Messrs Davies and Co. that the committee cannot agree to any amendment of the form of agreement. It was resolved that the question of the revision of the chargesior gas be referred to a sub committee censist- lag of Councillors Boma. Colliagwood, Onnand and Thompson. Dr Latham called attention to the minute referring to the revision of charges for gas and said he presumed that tbe sub-committee would report to the Gas Com- mittee before any alteration was made. The Chairman : I trunk there is some misunder- standing. lam not`aware that the matter was referred to a committee. Dr Latham said be had no recollection of the matter being referred to a committca but the Chairman made aestatecnent that it was just possible that the price of gas might have to be increased. He (the speaker) thought thee the G 39 COAMittle should have power to discuss any alteration, and the minute did not state that. The Clerk sii he had a trete to the off rct, that a sub- committee should consider the matter. Other members stated that they had no recollection of the minute being passed and it was ultimately decided to delete it. The remainder of the minutes were confirmed. WATER COMMITTEE.—"IGNORING THE COUNCIL." , DR LATHAM'S PROTEST. At a meeting of the Water Committee it was arranged that with respect to the supply of water to the district dering the early hours of the morning, the full pres- sure should be put on an hour or so earlier. A ;etter was read from the Haydock Urban District Council applying for the permission of the Newton Council to the supply Vey them of water to "The Woodlands" Ashton-road, in this district. It rwas resolved that permission De granted to supply this pro- perty tonly, provided that the Haydock Council will undirtake to cease to supply tee property in question at any time, upon the Newton Council offering to pur- chase so much of their mains as may be laid along the main reads within the district of the Council, at a rea- sonable price, having regard to the original cost and subsequent depreciation, the main to be laid in accord- ance with the Waterworks Clauses Act and section 54 c the Public Health Act of 1875. Mr Gilmour, in ereferring to the arrangement in regard to the turning on of water, rare that it had not been turned on at 530 until that morning. He thought there had been some blundering when the water was not turned on earlier until the day when the Council was going to nieet. Mr Borron expressed the hope that the early supply would centinuis, and said that from reports he had received daily, the supply for the whole township was now satisfactory. Mr Gilmour said ha did not want to give offence to Mr Borron, but in one part of the district there was no water for three days last week. Mr Borron said that from the reports he had received it appeared that the water had been satisfactory except on one of two occasions when the water was not received at the highest point in the district but was received some six doors below. Mr Gilmour renewed his complaint, stating that he hid tried to get water in the morning and could get it 'no thicker than a pipe stem." There should be a Supply right through the township. The Chairman: You are out of order. Mr Gilmour: As usual. Mr Smith: It is Mr Gilmour's privilege. (Laughter.) Mr Barron said that if Mr Gilmour would mention a specific date when people were short of water in the district he should be pleased to inquire into the matter. The Chairman said he thought that members who purposed raising such debatable questions should give noticiato the Chairman of the committee in order that he might prepare his answers. The matter then dropped. Dr Latham referred to the applicaeien from the Hay- dock Council with reference to the water supply for " The Woodlands.!' He said the house was situated in Newton-in-Makerfield and if the owner knew this he ought certainly to have made application to that Council. It was ignoring that Council entirely—a proceeding which ho thought the members would protest against. Before the minute was passed there should be an inquiry as to wbat was being done and what bad been done prior to the application conline from the nevi:leek Council. Inc Council ought to be careful to preserve their own preserves and keep out ictrudere. In his opinion it was the "thin end of the wedge " for a different township to steeply a commodity which that Council was able and willing to eupply. He proposed that the minute be referred back and that the clerk be instructed to obtain particulars from the Haydock Council with regard to the matter. Mr Berton said it was not for the Council to discutS any prior arrangements that the Haydock Council made sieWla anyone. That Council was not in a position to supply water to the"Woodlands,"and it would be a deg- ir-thermanger policy if they prevented any authority from coming to the verge of the district and meeting the wants of any party who required the water. When the Council was in a position to supply the water notice would be given to the Haydock Council. The Chairman said the arrangement was not fair and equitable. There was no seconder to the amendment, and the minutes were approved. HIGHWAYS AND SANITAILY COMMITTEE. This committee recommended that the surveyosibe directed to prepare the necessary plans and other information required by the Local Goeerernent Board with :he view of renewing the application which wes made to that Board in December. 1900,f0r their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £2,000 for the purpose of constructing additional works of sewage disposal. It was also resolved that the Clerk be instructed to obtain from the Local Government Board tin ir raeommenda- tion wth respect to the anticipatory mearures to be ttakeeinsotifloartohneHisooslpaittiael sun oft fcaosmesmoi,te fsme b a lle-Tequaonsdtedtlitaot h take such steps as they may deem necessary for the provision of a Hospital as speedily as possible. The Inspector of Nuisances was directed to make observa- tions and report to the next meeting of the Committee as to the state of Millineford Breok, and the Clerk was directed htetdte o write pollution tet o f h tot he cG otl h ek before u r nber Urbanoo D istr i enters Council as t ! Golhorne. With respect to the encroachment on the ' footpath in AskAon-road the Surveyor reported that he l had met Mr Leslie (Lord Newton's agent) on the spot • and that he (Mr Leslie) had promised to have the fence • put back. It was resolved that Athol-street having been sewered, levelled, paved, flagged, channelled, and made good to the satisfaction of the Council, the said I street be and is hereby declared to he a highway repair- able by the inhabitants at large, and that notice of the adoption be posted in the said street in accordance with Section 10 of the Private Street Works Act, 1892. The minutes were confirmed. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. THE VACCINATION QUESTION- At a meeting of this committee the Surveyor sub• mitted the plans which had been amended by the En- gineers to the South Lancashire Tramways Co., in otder to comply with the requirements of the CounciT, silQW- ing the construction of the proposed tramways in Com- mon-road Crow-lane. High-street, Church-street, and Southwonth-road, together with the plans thawing details. the proposed Trolley Wire Pillars and Section Pillars. It was resolved that this plans as submitted be appro , • ved A letter was read from the Rev.J C Matthews, stating that a Committee had been formed with the object of holding a May Queen festival in Earlestown, on Saturday, the 31st May next, for the purpose of rais- ing funds in connection with the extension or comple - tion of St. John's Church, and applying for the use of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
