      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>he Market Square for that purpose. It was resolved that the application be granted and that uo charge be made for the use of the Slarket fareand. A letter was read from the Beckenham Urban District Council, en- closing copy of a resolution passed by that CounciLask- ing the Local Government Board to take steps to repeal eiause 2of the Vaccination Act, 1898, which allows a Conscientious Objector to escape vaccination of his child or children. It was resolved that, in the interests of public health, Clause 2of the Vaccination Act, 1898, relating to the conscientious objector should be re- pealed, and that with a view of securing this desirable result, copies of thin resolution be sent to the Local Governmemt Board, the Right HOD. Lord Newton and Colonel Pilkington, MP. It was resolved that the Council be recommended to rescind so much of the resolution of this Committee which was adopted by the Council on Juno 3rd, 1901, whereby it was decided to provide a sum not exceeding £l5O for the supply and er;!ction of a clock in the Tower of St. Peter's Church, Newton-le-Willows, and to order the payment of the accounts of Messrs John Smith and Sons amounting to £136, for the supply of the clock, and of Mr Peter Tickle, anviunting to £26 103 31, for supplying a case for the clock, and for scaffolding and other work in connection with the fixing of the clock. It was also resolved that the Cmncil bear the cost of the gas- fittings, and undertake the winding and repair of the clock,and that incandescent burners be supplied for the lighting thereof, in lieu of the present ordinary ones.— The application of tee hail-keeper for an increase of wages to 30s per week was granted. The minutes were approved. MR GILMOUR AND THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>Bdr Gilmour referred to the near approach of the Coronation and said he thought some scheme should be adopted to celebrate it in that district, in order that the inhabitants might enjoy themselves and at the same time show their loyalty to the King. The Chairman asked if Mr Gilmour would acc,pt, the suggestion that the matter be referred to the General Purposes Committee for consideration.</p>
        <p>Mr Gilmour agreed to thiP, adding " I do so because I want to be loyal to the King." Mr Evans : Area's you already ? (Laughter). The meeting then conoludad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE NEWTON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE NEWTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Medical Officer's Annual Report. The Necessity for an Isolation Hospital.</p>
        <p>Dr. John W Watkins, Medical Officer of Health to the Newton Urban District Council, has issued his report on , the health of the district for the year ending December I 31st, 1901. It vappestre that during the year there were registered 494 births and 240 deaths. The birth-rate was 29'3, and the death-rate 14.2 per; 1,000 of the estimated population. The infantile death-rate was 1619 per cent. The child death-rate was 45 8 per cent, of tna total number of deaths. The causes of death were as follow, viz. : Measles 1, scarlet fever 2, whooping cough 5, diphtheria and membranous croup 4, croun 1, enteric fever 1, diarrlscee 14, enteritis 14, pbthisis 8, other tuberculous diseases 8, cancer, malignant disease, 3, bronchitis 23, pneumonia 27, alcoholism and cirrhosis of liver 6, die- eases and accidents of parturition 4, heart diseases 22, accidents 12, suicide 1, inquests 2, all other causes 82, total 240. The deaths from the seven principal emotes diseases numbered 26, being equal to a rate of 1.6 per 1,000 of the estimated population. Two hundred and nineteen cases -of infectious sickness were notified to him. ieThey were equal to a rate of 13 02 per 1,000 of the estimated population. Fifty-nine cases were removei to the Isolation Hospital. The infected houses received the assiduous and unremitting attention of the Sanitary Inspector. The member of infectious cases was abnormal and should be an object lesson tcr the Council in dealing with infections disease. Dr Watkins attributes the serr ad of scarlatina mainly to the Council not having command of the provision for isolation of the early case,. " Isola- lation Hospitals," continues the report, " are not primarily for epidemics,but to prevent the earliest oases in an invasion multiplying till they are of epidemic pro- portion. An Isolation Hospital in an epidemic area is I but of small value in checking an epidemic. Our past experience demonstrates the absolute necessity of an I Isolation Hospital for this sanitary district. Our esti. mated population for the current • year-17,253 —is surely large enough for a hospital of its own. The estimate for the next census year is 21,682. The census of 1901 revealed an excess of 1,065 over the estimated population. History repeats itself, and there is every reason to believe that our population is increasing at a more rapid rate in the current decade (1901-1911) than In the previous one. We are forewarned, theretoro let us be forearmed. Notwithstanding the large number of CUBS of scerlatins, it is a real consolation to know that they were of the mil lest type conceivable—only two deaths in 166 cases. I would urge the Council not to pause or rest in their determination till the hospital Is an aecomplished fact—it is the one great and absolute- necessity of our people. It is not bard to believe that if the first six of those 166 cases had been absolutely isolated none other mie,ht have followed. We have the power to stamp out these diseases—then de, pray. let us have the will and tie up and doing." The report states that the rear ha 3 been one Of extra- ordinary activity of the Council in public works—more especially (a) the development of the Water Supply ; (Is) the extension of sewerage and the development of the bacterial process in the treatment of It; and (c) street improvement. The pew water supply a year ago could hardly be said to have get beyoude the experi- mental or tentative stage. There was much progress made in 1901, anal now the experts consulted and employed—(geological analysts—both chemieal and biological—andlengineers) reassured the Council by re- newed statements that (I)there is abundance of water to be got from the present source; (2) that it is pure; and that (3) its efficient distribution will very soon be secured. Sewerage basebeen extended by the making of a new sewer half a mile long, and now very nearly completed—the part of it remaining to be done le being pushed on as rapidly as possible. This missing link in the chain has through technical difficulties, be wanted. The sewage treat ment has been developed and improved. In the early autumn of 1901 the Central Works were visited by delegates from the Royal Corn mission on Sewage, and as a result of their visit the Council has been asked if they would allow them to inspect and take observations of the works for twelve , months. The condition of Millingiord Brook has impreved—nertainly so far as the vast improvement in I the efflueat fr)m the Golborne Sewage Works diminished its contaminatioo—but the water coming down from Golborne le very variable in its quality and in an un- desirable direction that the doctor is sure is preventible. —ln teferring to small-pox the , doctqr ears :—" The possibility of our visitation by this disease renders it quite imperative that your Council should decide what measures should be taken to meet it, and make arranze• manta that such measures shall beeome immediately practical in the event of necessit). You have no refuge for small-pox patients. In the first instance I would advise you to ask the Local Government Board for ladvice as to anticipatory measures to be taken, and then follow it. Do not Wait till the disease ceme—and things are done in a panic."—Speaking of the Voluntary 11 notification of phthisis,:the doctor says be Is still of opinion that the voluntary notification of CaS39 of the disease would net ensure the practical r 'sults so much to be desired. As a first step the Council might require the Registrar to furnish them with a special notice of every-death known to have been caused by the disease. AO that Special attention could be paid to infected houses, apartments, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>VICAR OF FRODS- HAM AND SUNDAY</head>
        <p>THE VICAR OF FRODS- HAM AND SUNDAY</p>
        <p>os§EkiAi;ia.</p>
        <p>In the expe-tation that the Rev H B Blogo would make some further statement on the subject of Sunday observance, which he dealt with at the Ruri-decanal Conference at Runcorn last week, a large congregation assembled at Overton Church on Sunday morning. Toe rev gentleman's advocacy ot the observance of Sunday on the linos followed on the continent has been received with strong and open hostility by the general body of his parishion- ers, and that this fact has been brought under the Vicar's notice may be gathered from the remarks with which he.prefaced his morning sermon. He said he understood with much regret that several people had been pained by some remarks that he made at Runcorn on Tuesday and be thought it his duty to them, and to the rest of the parish, that he should explain what he said, and that he should give his views on Sunday observance. For that purpose he proposed to preach on the subject next Sunday, 'both in the morning and in the evening. On Sunday at St. Paul's Church, Run3orn, the ' Rev J B Forster preached on "Sunday Observance." He said the Sabbath had been made by God and the Jews with whom the Cwenant had been made owed their prosperity largely to its observation' The apostles and Jesus Christ himself dwelt upon the paramount element of moral value in the observing of the Sabtath. In view of this it was a calamity that one who pro- fessed to be a servant of the Most High should suggest that the day should be prostituted for pleasure and sport. Englend owed her great- ness to the respect she bad shown for the Sabbath day. It was a thousand pities that the man who made such a suggestion had not the common senFe to see that his course would only qg gravate the evil of which he 6niplained. It was a pity that the chairman of the meeting did not repudiate the proposed desecration. Though the condition had been attached that the man should first go to church it did not require much insight to see that the man who had any true regard for worship would be the last man to give up the rest of the day to pleasure. He prayed God that there mlght never be opened upon our rland the floodgates of a continental Sunday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."_</p>
        <p>The week-end matclles have resulted in Warrington climbing a couple of places higher in the League table, so that the result on the whole, is satisfactory. Prob- ibly no one expected the team to take a couple of points out of Salford on their own ground. Such things have been done but the probabilities were very much in favour of the Reds. The unexpected failed to happen, and Salford won comfortably by the rather large margin of 16 points to 6 points, though the score does not fairly represent the run of the play. Warrington were much better than the figures lead one to imagine, and but for a couple of uncommonly good individual efforts by the übiquitous Lomas it is quite probable that Warrington would have come out on top.</p>
        <p>One often hears it said that Warrington is a "one man" team. Certainly *Tick Fish is the best man in it, but the remark applies just as correctly to Salford Rather curiously the four tries scored by Warringtoa on Saturday and Monday fell to flockenhall and Harris, Fish's share of the spoils being only one goal. He has played in his best form nevertheless.</p>
        <p>A good number of Warringtonians were induced by the fine weather and a desire to inspect the new head- quarters of the Salford ground, to follow the team on Saturday's journey. The majority either took train from Manchester to Weaste, or entrusted themselves on one of the numerous 'buses, in order to get to the ground. A few, however, with plenty of time and energy decided to walk. And they walked! In fact they walked so long and such a deuce of a way that they were beginning to fear that Liverpool-street ,would land them at Liverpool instead of the Willows. All things have an end and so has Liverpool-street, and the wanderers reached their destination in time for the kick off and with a profound feeling of thankfulness that they would not have to walk back unless all the horses died and the trains got lost in the meantime.</p>
        <p>Warrington got to the Willows per 'bus from the Central, and arrived without Edmonds, whose absence nobody could explain. Pockets were searched but only a few quids of tobacco, and not the missing half-back were found. He turned up at last and there was a sigh of relief among the Warrington spectators when his manly form was seen among the rest of the players. Operations were soon commenced and remained pretty even for a time. Both sides played well, and Salford opened the scoring through Bone. Warrington equal- ised soon after, Fish leading to the way to the line and giving the ball to Hockenhall at the right moment. On changing ends with the points equal, Warrington had the advantage of the wind, and their chances looked good until Lomas got hold and dodged under the posts in rather a simple sort of fashion, and added a goal. Play waged backwards and forwards after this, Price adding a third try and Lomas a fourth in just the same fashion as before. Near the end Harris raced in from half-way and reduced the lead of the winners a little.</p>
        <p>At one point, when Salford were only five points ahead, Fish robbed Smith and dribbled clear away, only to be badly fouled in front of goal. Mr Smith awarded a penalty kick which came to nought, and in the opinion of many he should have given a try. Fish would certairrlY have scored had he not been ob- structed, and I rather think Warrington would have been able to hold their own afterwards. The best team won, however, so one cannot grumble much.</p>
        <p>Salford's chief superiority was forward. In heeling out and giving the backs opportunities they were streets ahead of Warrington, and they got the ball three times to Warrington's once in the second half. It seems to me that the Warrington men might pack better. Often enough they tumble into the scrim- mage in a haphazard sort of fashion which prevents them controlling the ball in the proper way. At one time last season they were far smarter, It is time they improved. Cup-tie football is to a great extent dependent on strong and smart forward play. ..,==</p>
        <p>Monday's match, seeing that it had been twice post- poned deserved to be fatoured with the beautiful weother which prevailed. The turf too, was in excel- lent order, and the conditions could not have been better for a Rugby game. Both teams were well repre- sented, Hull putting absolutely their strongest side in the field. The pace in the first half was a "cracker," the ball travelling up and down the field constantly. Warrington had the advantage of the wind, and com- bining well they forced Hull to defend. Two or three excellent openings went astray ere Hockenhall took the responsibility of going on his own and scored as pretty a try under the posts as anyone need wish to see. Fish improved on the point, and had hard luck himself in falling a victim to Taylor, the county full back, when he had got clear. Hull made little headway, though they played smartly, and Harris added a second try by beating Taylor for possession and winning a fine race from half way to the goal-line. On turning round Hull played a man short in the forwards and seemed all the better for it. Perhaps Warrington were -lazy—at any rate Thompson scored a try and Jacques a penalty goal before they awoke to the seriousness of Hull's inten- tions. Play henceforth was confined to the centre of the ground or the Hull half, and Warrington on in the end by 8 points to 5 points.</p>
        <p>It is difficult to understand how it is that Hull have lost every match since Christmas. They are a smart team, and one must credit their assertion that rank bad luck has been their portion. On Monday they held their own with Warrington, and could not be put down as an inferior team on the day's play. They have good men in every position, and I shall be surprised if they do not make some progress in the cup-ties.</p>
        <p>For Warrington, Edmonds has played much better this week-end. His best display was at Salford, where he earned the praise of both sides by his smartnesS and pluck. For his inches he is a determined and effective tackler. He and Hockenhall shape very well together, the last-named also showing much better acquaintance with the necessities of half-back play. Jones has done well in Isherwood's place, though he does not find Fish as much work as the regular centre. By the way Isher- wood witnessed Saturday's match, and afterwards feturned to Allison's. Ile has,the footballer's knee and it requires skilled doctoring. Taylor has apparently lost his earlier form, and in the last few games has done nothing to justify his selection. --- Quite a number of postponed fixtures have been com- pleted this week, and among them was the one at Run- corn on Wednesday. Oldham , were the visitors and they were severely trounced by the Linnets who scored 11 points to nil. Runcorn were badly beaten in the scrimmages but behind they were greatly superior to the once-famous Oldhamers, and gave a display quite in keephig with the old traditions of the Cheshire club. The Oldham backs performed shockingly, the halves being particularly clumsy and slow. Altogether it was a smart perforinance on Runcorn's part.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to date is as follows: For Aeinst.</p>
        <p>P W L D G T PIS ...G T P PTS. Broughton Rangers 24_20 3 I_4o 63-269-19 19— 95..41 Bradford *Swinton 21...16 8 0...28 55-121...23 18-10d...28 *Salford Halifax 25...11 10 4...23 28-134...29 34-160...26 *Runcorn 23-13 9 1...27 ,39-171-16 23-101,..25 *Warrington Oldham Hull *Hunslet Leigh 24...10 11 0...29 61-151 -22 36-151...20 Huddersfield 13atley Brighouie Rangers 25.. 321 1...21 9 69...59 72-316... 7 *Swinton, and Hunsiet have each had four points and Salford, Warrington, and Runcorn. have each had two points deducted for bre aches of the profeialonal rules.</p>
        <p>Widnes did a smart performance in beating Leigh at Leigh on Saturday, and by their success they depose Warrington from their position at the head of the South- West Lancashire League table.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League table up-to,clate is as follows Point</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. Fc;Agst. Pte. Widnes Warrington , 8 ... 5 ... 2 ... 1 ... 57 ... 37 ... 11 Runcorn 7 3 4 ... 0 ... 25 ... 34 ... 6 Leigh Wigan 4 2 2 0 . 37 36 . 4 St. Helene</p>
        <p>There was some important business at the Northern Union meeting on Tuesday evening, the most startling of which was the suspension from club management under the 'auspices of the Union of Mr Bairsto, the Hunslet representative. He had criticised the com- mittee rather severely in public over the case of Hannah's suspension, and refused to apologise. The committee acted with customary severity and Mr Bairsto probably feels now that to apologise would have been the best course to adopt. Two more points .were deducted from the Hunslet club's score and they now rank with Swinton in having been twice penalised. Bradford were also in trouble, and it looks likely that they will suffer in the same way at the next meeting of the League Committee.</p>
        <p>- Foster, of Warrington "A," and A. Davies, Salford "A," were both suspended up to and including Satur- ;day next for the little duel they had at Wilderspool last Saturday, when the Warrington team was rather unfortunate to lose by a try to a goal. In consequence of the severity of the struggle with Hull the Warrington team has undergone several altera- tions for the match with Flunslet to-day. Edmonds is the chief absentee, and the Committee have given him a rest in order that his injured knee may recover, and at the same time to see how Hockenhall and Bate</p>
